      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>MMOUGALVS PATBNT SELF-RAISING YLOUR iNDITITANO THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 30 1900</head>
        <p>MMOUGALVS PATBNT SELF-RAISING YLOUR iNDITITANO THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVEMBER 30 1900</p>
        <p>a... THE FREE CtiIfRCHES.</p>
        <p>PERSECUTION IN NORPOLS.</p>
        <p>The case of Mr: Burrell Hammond, a flrmer near Melton Constable, who has been given notice to quit by Lord Hastings, his landlord, has excited a great deal of attention and sympathy among Free Churchmen. Mr. Hammond's offence is that he is a Liberal and a Noncon- formist, an active working Liberal and a zealous, sturdy Nonconformist, and his land- lord is a Conservative and a Churchman. The Eastern Daily Press first published the facts of tho case, which are not in dispute, as Lord Hastings has expressed them in black and white in a letter to Mr. Hammond. Mr. HA In MOM.' has served the Free Churches in his neighbourhood well, and the Holt Free Church Council, to mark its appreciation of his character and sympathy with him, has eh.cted him its President. A subscription for him has been started. An excellent photo- graphic group of Mr. Hammond, his wife and fam,' appears in the December Free Chum/4 Chronicle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>ANGLICAN INACCURACY.</head>
        <p>ANGLICAN INACCURACY.</p>
        <p>The Dawn of Day is an interesting little Inilj'4inny monthly, published by the Society fr Promoting Christian Knowledge, which has the Archbishop of Canterbury as its President and many Bishops among its Vice-Presidents. In a recent number "May Cochrane" 'was allowed by the editor to say that the Church of England was not only the Church of the British Empire, but the Church of the whole Anglo- Saxon race. The fact is that the vast majority of the Anglo-Saxon race rejects the Church of England. According to the latest returns the Church of England could claim 3,286,573 com- municants, and the Evangelical Free Churches 17,7,23,°49 communicants. That is to say, the Church of England is outnumbered l-)y five to °lle. Further comment is unnecessary. Anglican editors and writers should really be more careful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>VENERABLE WILLIAM ARTHUR.</head>
        <p>THE VENERABLE WILLIAM ARTHUR.</p>
        <p>; • 4*• \ , l' • t • , ' , 11,</p>
        <p>1 ~ :.:,-t:1,: ~ 1 , , 1 I- I</p>
        <p>' -1 , k rl t</p>
        <p>The Rev. William Arthur, a Wesleyan xvhc, is now living at Cannes in extreme old it ge and in feeble health, had a great repu- tation a generation ago as the author of "The Tongue -.)f Fire." It is a book well worth reading even now. and especially in view of the</p>
        <p>:.'7uee Church Simultaneous Mission. It is curious to read in the preface, written forty- four years ago, that "the work has been inte..rupted by travel and sickness; and, at one time, seemed likely to be cut short by death." The volume was "the fruit of meditations entered upon with the desire to lessen the dis- tance painfully fe!t to exist between my own life anJ ministry and those of the primitive Christians."</p>
        <p>REV. W. J. WOODS, 8.A.-REV. R. J. CAMPBELL, B.A</p>
        <p>We are delighterl to hear that Mr. Woods' doctor was able to speak very hopefully as the re &amp;t of his latest examination into his patient's condition. It is difficult for a man of Mr. Woods' nervous energetic temperament not to fret and c7)afo under restraint, salutary though thp,!, restraint may be. However, time and patience will effect a final remedy. Mr. Camp- bell, too, is quickly gaining strength. He is appearing more like his old self than he has done for some time. It is anticipated that:it will not be long before he is again preaching to his people. Pastor and congregation are affectionately devoted to each other.</p>
        <p>DR. WECNYON ON CHINESE MISSIONS.</p>
        <p>Dr. Wenyon, who was formerly in charge of the esleyan Medical Mission at Fatshan, has 1n expressing his views very frankly on the Chinese crisis. He says that the status quo should be restored, that no more concessions should be obtained by force, and that mission- aries should go forth independent of edicts on their behalf. Dr. Wenyon is strongly opposed to young unmarried women being employed as agents, it being absolutely contrary to Chinese notions of propriety, the Chinaman being unql de to regard them as anything but consorts of the missionaries. Further, Dr. Wenyon would give the Chinese mandarins at the treaty ports the right to declareppium contra- band.</p>
        <p>tha 'the Pei 'Bid</p>
        <p>•,. 1,t41, _ _</p>
