      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>DR. CLIFFORD AND MR. CUFF.</head>
        <p>DR. CLIFFORD AND MR. CUFF.</p>
        <p>Dr. Clifford would be called by one set of people " RAvanced" in his theology, Mr. Cuff narrow. By another set Dr. Clifford would be regal Jed as not quite sound, in short, down grade, Mr. Cuff as faithful to the old ways, and the old Gospel. However, the fact is that their standpoint is different. Yet it is signi- ficlut of their substantial agreement on the things that really count that Dr. Clifford preached at Shoreditch Tabernacle at the twenty-eighth anniversary of Mr. Cuff's settle- ment there. Mr. Cuff has toiled hard and successfully in a poor and crowded district. His brave heart will be further cheered by the joyous celebration of another epoch in his life's history. We wish him many more years of fruitful labour.</p>
        <p>WANTED-A REVIVAL OF OPEN-AIR PREACHING,</p>
        <p>Mr. Frank T. Bullen, whose sea-stories have gained him a great reputation within a com- paratively brief period, has been engaged for fifteen or sixteen years in open-air evan- gelistic work, and complains of the many golden opportunities that the churches miss in this field of endeavour. The most incapable mem- bers of the churches seem to be set to this kind of work, and the staple of many open-air addresses is simply nauseating twaddle that repels working men. Wny should not the churches, says Mr. Bullen, take a leaf out of the book of the Socialists, Secularists, and the rest, and send their best men into this work F-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>UNION OF THE SCOTTISH CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>THE UNION OF THE SCOTTISH CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>The Union of the Free Church of Scotland and the United Presbyterian Church was happily consummated on November 1. It was a great day in Edinburgh, a great day for Scotland, and, without boastfulness, it may be added, a. great day for Christendom. The new Church has a membership of somewhere about half a million. As was expected, Principal Rainy was elected Moderator (or President) of the United Assembly. Dr. Rainy is not well known on this side of the border, but he is the first man in Scotland. He is an ecclesiastical statesman and diplomatist of the first rank. Withal, he is a man of great personal humility and simplicity of character. He has worked for Union for more than a generation. His life-work is now triumphantly crowned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>MOST NUTRITIOUS.</head>
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
        <p>E P PS'S</p>
        <p>GRATEFUL.</p>
        <p>COMFORTING</p>
        <p>OCOA</p>
        <p>3REAKFAST-SUPPER.</p>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR Nzrzng,</p>
        <p>The fur Eton coat has taken a fresh lease of popular favour, and is to be had at every possible —or impossible—price, from the article made of some unidentified and mysterious brown or black fur to the exquisite little coat of finest seal at £3O or £4O. The shape most in vogue for these cosy jackets has rather long tabbed fronts, and a very high collar and broad revers either of the same or of a contrasting fur; but quite short boleros, with fronts meeting on the bust and sharply rounded off immediately below, are rather newer. A few, again, have long, flat stole ends of the fur reach- ing almost to the knees, and the sleeves of most are slightly bell-shaped at the wrist. Large buttons are seen on many of the double-breasted. Etons, but those that are single-breasted are fastened after the manner of ordinary sealskin coats with invisible hooks. Black caracul Etons with revers of ermine or chincilla are particularly fashionable at the moment, but sable and ermine are both used for these coats. •</p>
        <p>Sometimes I think that the bolero is by means of over elaboration allowed to become a monstrous little thing, entirely out of harmony with the rest of the dress. One, for instance, made of fawn- coloured cloth, like the skirt, has a sort of border jutting beyond it all round and made of printed panne. Beyond this, again, stands out a second border of blue and gold embroidery; and yet the depth of the whole garment, including these sup- plementary materials, is but nine inches in front and not more than ten at the back. It is a mis- take to over-trim one portion of the dress at the expense of the rest. The bolero can be very orna- mental, and, also a useful addition. without serving merely as an excuse for a style of decoration that means nothing. The best-dressed people are not those who pile up ornament on ornament. The first consideration is the outline; the second suit- ablematerial ; the third colour. The last-named should be used with as much discretion as the vinegar in a salad.</p>
        <p>One-of the prettiest of ora ling gowns that I have seen lately is one that Vs% simple as can be, and yet extremely novel. 4, PRell pink crepe de soie composes it, softensirmvi a pair of sleeves and stoles of creamy laet,r'o.nd bands of glacier blue panne to outline the decolletage, span the waist, and provide the narrow runlets upon the flounces and round the elbows, also the long streamers that fall with some frothy crep.ode soie where the sleeves end. Black panne. might m this case be substituted for the-m3rstio-looking blue, but black is always justira little hard in effect, especially with soft pink, and the blue called glacier is very dark, though it has a greyish white bloom upon it.</p>
        <p>A cashmere dross which Asa been shown me is one of a very soft violet colour, trimmed with bars of narrow black velvet. The softly-lined black velvet collar has an overturned white lawn band upon the upper edge, while below it there is a little vest of tucked white lawn, which givesza smart and charming finish to the costume. It is moticeable that the sleeves of the newest gowns are much more comfortably large than those of yester-year. Brought into a shell-shaded cuff the ones sketched are particularly cosy for house wear, especially to those who do needlework or pursue other kinds of handicraft at home. Cloth seems to be the favourite materials for many costumes. The skirts fit closely round the hips, and are quite plain except for a few gathers at the back. The fulness afforded by these is allowed to flow out quite loosely, the stitched-dovra pleats being now considered a trifle dowdy. The blouse bodice is almost' universally adopted for walking, and white glace is more worn than any- thing else, with a bolero of lace or embroidery. Pale blue or bright turquoise silk blouses are in great favour, and in combination with a pansy- coloured cloth skirt in a warm tone of deep helio- trope was worn a blouse of wisteria mauve panne, stitched in a kind of watermark pattern in white silk. The collar was rich cream-tinted lace, and a cravat of rather a clerical order was in similar lace in two flat ends. These were caught at the top with a jewelled pin. A deep, folded waistband of figured mauve and pansy-coloured panne rose high at the back and fastened over at one side with jewelled buttons. Your loving Jxxvzic.</p>
