      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>HOUSE BY Mb RIVER.</head>
        <p>THE HOUSE BY Mb RIVER.</p>
        <p>BY MRS. C. N. 'WILLIAMSON, Author of "The Secret of the Pearls," "The House by the Lock," "Queen Sweetheart," "The Barnstormers," "Her Royal Highness," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>' CHAPTER XV. DOOR GLADYS I</p>
        <p>"Mr. Churchill I" announced a footman; and Lady Carstairs held out a welcoming hand. She had been waiting for him in a pretty little room called the "Chinese boudoir," placed at her special disposal during her visit to Lady Northmuir, and she had the comfortable knowledge that the coming interview would be undisturbed.</p>
        <p>"How good of you to be so prompt!" she said; for the quaint Chinese clock on the mantel was in the act of striking twelve, and when they parted the i!ight before she had asked that Jack Churchill would call "about noon."</p>
        <p>"One doesn't take credit to oneself for promptness when it is a question of one's own pleasure," Jack dutifully remarked, with a. slight deviation from his wonted sincerity; for, in faot, he would be glad when this call, SO seriously appointed, was well over. _</p>
        <p>Exactly why ho felt this impatience, he could hardly have explained, particularly:as he was still anxious for further news of Sybil Wade. But Lady Carstairs's manner in asking him to come had retained all the solemnity she had shewn during the disagreeable little episode of the orchids ; and in Jack's opinion that affair might better have been allowed to rest.</p>
        <p>"Come and sit down by the fire, won't you ?" said Lady Carstairs. "It is so chilly to-day. She made room for him beside her on a sofa which had been drawn in front of the hearth, and Jack, who was healthily warm with fast walking in the cold autumnal air, martyrised himself by obeying. The seat indicated threw the light from an adjacent window full upon his face, while Lady Carstairs's remained in shadow.</p>
        <p>"I have been with Sybil Wade all the morning, mnd only came home in time to be sure of not missing you," she began, abruptly. "The poor girl has been taken ill, and as I was afraid she might not receive proper care where she was I have put her in a nursing home." Lady Carstairs looked keenly at Jack as she spoke, and was ready with her answer in ease he should ask questions about the place she had mentioned. She preferred, however, that he should not ask, and unostentatiously to prevent his doing so, if possible, she hurried on with what she had to say. "Sybil has been very imprudent of late, it seams," she continued, "about going out in the rain, and then not changing her wet things. Of course, she's quite unused to our English climate, and it is teaching her rather a severe lesson. I only hope that it may be no worse, for—there is consumption, I understand, in her mother's family." 1 This last was an impromptu touch, thought of on the spur of the moment, and Lady Carstairs was pleased with her own quick wit. "Naturally," she went softly on, "this illness will delay the question of Sybil's trying to earn her own living. She has a frightful cold and a high fever. For some time to conic she must keep her room, and even after that at best she will have to be very careful. Did you not think when you saw her here that she looked extremely delicate and anmic ? " "On the contrary," Jack blurted out, "I thought she had the most perfect complexion and exquisite colouring I have ever seen." Lady Carstairs frowned slightly. "These rose and pearl skins are often a sign of painful delicacy," she said. "At all events, this lonely little creature will be well cared for, you may depend. Gladys and I will do what we can, and nothing that money can buy shall be denied to Sybil. We shall be all the more considerate, perhaps, because we were neither of us impressed in her favour, and if you don't forget about the unfortunate girl you shall hear how she progresses from time to time." "Thank you," responded Jack, rather stiffly. "I hope that she will soon be well. Is the nursing home in London ? "</p>
        <p>"In the suburbs," Lady Carstairs hastily answered. "And now, since you know all that there is to know on that subject, shall we turn to another? I want to talk with you about that—er —that fatal bouquet last night. Jack was silent and attentive. 1 "We are such friends that I feel I can speak quite freely," she continued. "What lam about to say I know you will regard as quite confidential. I would not have Gladys suspect for anything. Ah, you look surprised. But you have no idea how wretchedly humiliated and unhappy my poor child is to-day. I don't think she closed her eyes last night, and through the half-open door between our rooms I could often hear her stifled sobbing." Jack opened his brown eyes in bewildered astonishment. He hardly knew what to say, for it seemed that Lady Carstairs was guilty of treachery to her daughter in speaking to him of Gladys's emotion—which, so far as he could see, had no possible connection with him. Still, he must say, something, or appear unsympathetic, and he stam- mered a few incoherent words, expressing his regret. "I —l'm dreadfully sorry to hear that," he said. "But I can't quite understand why—that is, there was nothing " "To poor, sensitive Gladys, there was everything," broke in Lady Carstairs. "Put yourself in her place. She thanks you with an innocent display of all she really feels for a gift which—which could have had but one meaning—a gift which she has accepted, believing implicitly that it was sent by you. Then comes the dreadful shock of finding that you were not, after all, the giver; and since you were not, some malicious person must deliberately have plotted her humiliation." Jack grew pale under his sunburn. He was neither self-conscious nor conceited; but Lady Carstairs had made her meaning plain enough now for the least vain of men to read. She was plainly hinting that Gladys had believed the orchids, with their decorations Of lovers' knots in diamonds on gold ribbon, carried with them a declaration. Believing this, and because of this, the girl had accepted them, and was overwhelmed by shame and grief in discovering her mistake. A week or two ago, he would not have thought reparation so terrible a sacrifice, though even then he had not been in love with Gladys„- but now everything was different. His mind and heart were filled with the image of another girl; and though that girl had passed out of his life as suddenly as she had come in, lie knew that it would be long before he could forget her. _ . . .</p>
        <p>Too honest himself easily to suspect duplicity in others, ho did not for an instant dream that Lady Carstairs was acting a little draina:fori his benefit; but gave her credit for all the embarrassment, the motherly concern her trembling voice expressed—Ho felt as if a hand were gripping his throat as he answered :</p>
        <p>"I'll find out who sent the bouquet, and I pro- mise you, Lady Carstairs' that he shall regret it." But the last thing Lady Carstairs desired was that he should find out who had sent the flowers with their gorgeous ribbon. She shook her head sadly: "The more I think of it (and though I have tried to help Sybil Wade, I have thought of little else since last evening), the more I feel that bring- ing the culprit to justice will be of little avail. The mischief has, I fear, gone much too far, and is already too deeply rooted for that. Try to learn the truth, by all means—though it is too late to hope that poor Gladys can be spared pain in that way." "Tell me what can be done, then, and trust me to do it," Jack said, impulsively. . .</p>
        <p>"Do you really mean that ? " Lady Carstairs's eyes flashed a keen, bright look upon him. "Of course I mean it." Ms tone changed a little, but he faced her bravely. "I am your and Miss Wade's most loyal friend; and, if I am not as quick in taking the initiative as a cleverer man would be, at least you have only to command me." He was going further than he meant; but a man who is frank and honest and unspoilt, is no match for an intriguing woman, and the chivalrous, foolish words seemed to speak themselves. "How good you are!" Lady Carsta,irs looked down, played with her rings, and appeared to hesitate. "You see," she went slowly on, when it was clear that Jack would not speak again until she had given him a lead, "you see, the sending of the orchids with the diamond—lovers' knots, only proves what I had feared for some time." What is that ? " Jack questioned, as she paused. "That there has been a great deal of gossip about —about you and Gladys. She has been such a popular girl, and her beauty—if you won't think I am too vain a mother to call it that—has made her very conspicuous. Since she was presented she has had a number of offers, and naturally has been the victim of much jealousy among those leas favoured</p>
        <p>Luop ipuu ! Abl.n enutquioqu slqi piqd suq 'eSpuio amupg ol polyp .10A0[ 0801161 r o sug ens tuoum uutu etnos .101T1,7 •ans urTr doubt that, in a day or two, it will be all over town."</p>
        <p>"But what could anyone possibly say to Miss Wade's disadvantage?" demanded Jack, who had not dreamt until now what momentous proportions the affair of the orchids might assume. "What could they say? Why, but one thing: that Gladys Wade was ready to accept from you a love that had never been offered. Oh, Mr. Churchill, you can never know how much it costs me to say such dreadful things. But lam Gladys's mother, and I would spend• my very life for the sake of my beautiful girl." "But," protested Jack, "any man might have sent such a present to a lady. It would not be her fault if the gift were in bad taste." "It would be her fault if she accepted it. Why, put the case to yourself, Mr. Churchill. Would you have made such a present to .a girl whom you were not in love with ? "</p>
        <p>"Perhaps 40t," Jack admitted, honestly. "You havJ answered your own question, then. And I tell you frankly, since we are,now speaking plain truths to each other, that Gladys accepted what she thought was your gift because she sup- posed you cared for her, and meant at once to tell her so; because, in return, she-" _ _</p>
        <p>Lady Carstairs broke dowii, and, burying her face between her hands, could distinctly be heard to sob. Jack sprang up, with an inarticulate exclamation. Never in his life had he been so distressed, so be- wildered, so utterly at a loss to reconcile chivalrous impulses and inclination. He tried to say some- thing comforting, something reassuring,. but the right words would not come.</p>
        <p>-"There has already been so much talk about your friendship with Gladys," Lady Carstairs went on, drying her eyes with the air of a patient sufferer; "and now it will become quite unbearable for a sensitive girl. I wanted your.advice this morning (you see how entirely I trust you!) ; for, if you do not really care for Gladys, I am thinking of taking her abroad."</p>
        <p>' "Of course -I care for her," Jack began; and before he could qualify his statement with a "but" —if that had been: in his mind—Lady Carstairs rose, with a little joyous cry, holding out to him both her hands.</p>
        <p>"0 Mr. Churchill—Jack—you do care? Then all my wretchedness has been for nothing. After all, my sweet girl is to be happy! What a blessed ending to my anxieties! But it is Gladys to whom you wish to say this—not to me., I will go to her —with just one little hint to set her heart beating to a happier tune—and send her down to you." Lady Carstairs glided to the door with an arch, backward glance from the eyes which shewed remarkably few traces of their late tears, and in an instant Jack Churchill was left alone.</p>
        <p>"By Jove'!" he said to himself. "By Jove!" If he had not been one of the tenderest hearted young men on earth, and chivalrous to a degree that was almost quixotic, Lady Carstairs's morning's work would not have becn so simple. But she had doubtless based her calculations largely upon the nature with which she had to deal: and she was too clever a woman to make many mistakes in her estimate s.</p>
        <p>Jack walked gloomily'to one of the windows and stared out, seeing nothing. . "If Gladys really cares," he reflected, "there's only one tl4ng for me to do, after this, I suppose. And, of &amp;urge, she must care, or her mother would not have made such a scene tor us both, dragging in her daughter's name. Though what she can see in me—a girl who must have had so many other fellows after her. It can't be money, for Carstairs is rich, and everybody says he's very generous to his mother and sister. If I thought it was money it would be easy enough to know what to do. B tay as well put that out of my mind. I ought to be the proudest man on earth, to think that such a girl as Gladys Wade would look at me. At one time—not so very long ago, either—l was near to falling in love with her; though, somehow, that seems strange to .me now. I admire her as one admires a handsome statue or picture, that one doesn't care to buy. But must have blundered into acting more than I ever really felt for her, or she would never have let herself think of me for a moment. It's all my fault. and I must abide by the consequences of it. It isn't as if I were in love with anyone else." . _</p>
        <p>As he tried thus to assure himself that fortune had been kinder to him than he deserved, the image of a little slim girl in white rose .before his eyes, and a deep, sickening pain contracted his heart. No, "it was not as if he were in love with anyone else." But, since he was to be Gladys Wade's husband, it was as well that Fate had taken her cousin Sybil out of his life. Even if ,they ever met again, it would probably not be until after his marriage; and he thought he knew himself well enough to be sure that he would be loyal to his wife.</p>
        <p>He had not yet been able to put the lovely, wistful face out of his mind, when the door opened again and Gladys came in. She was paler than he had ever seen her, and the big dark eyes held a softer light. Jack, never having had any sisters, did not know how pathetic an effect can be wrought by a little pearl powder, and the touch of a pencil under the lower lashes. He believed the pallor due to the sleepless night which Gladys had passed—for his sake. Still the small white face, with violet eyes and frame of golden hair, seemed to float between him and Gladys's dusky beauty; but he took the hand she held out, pressing it warmly, and said, without any preface: "Gladys, do you care enough for me to be my wife ? "</p>
        <p>CHATTER XVI. A SLIP OP PAPER.</p>
        <p>- Sybil and Hester looked at each other as Mrs. Wool announced the return of the messenger with the letter for "Miss Ward." It was so very mysterious that there should be such a lettek, so much depended upon the contents, that Sybil almost ilreaded to read and learn her fate and Hester's. The boy's waiting downstairs, and he wants you to sign a paper to shew you've got the letter," said the landlady. "I will go with you," replied Sybil, simply rand with a white face and slightly compressed lips followed Mrs. Wool downstairs. Hester waited above, and as several minutes went by without the younger girl's return, her impatience and anxiety became almost unbearable. At last she could endure it no longer, and running to the top of the stairs looked Over just in time to see Sybil coming up. - "I couldn't help stopping till I'd read the letter," cried Sybil, "because the bo,y thought there would be an answer. But, 0 Hester, the mystery is no mystery at all. It's the simplest thing in the world, and we have been so worried for nothing. Mr. Robertson remembered our address, which he saw on my slip of paper this morning, and he writes asking if we can do a little private work for him in the evenings at home. Isn't it kind of him to have thought of it? I did not wait to answer by letter, but told the messenger to say that the answer was Yes.' It seems so encouraging, that I feel better already. Now shall we go out and have our supper in. peace ? " She sprang upstairs lightly, her cheeks glowing, for the way of beginners trying to earn their own living did not appear as difficult as she had supposed. Hester did not answer, though she tacitly acquiesced with Sybil's proposal that they should go out to sup*. She could not make up her mind to dash her friend's rising spirits, but she had her own opinion of Mr. Robertson's dis- interested kindness. "It may be that there's nothing in my fears about Mr. Drake," she said to herself; "but, anyway, I shall be very much mis- taken if trouble of some kind doesn't come from this quarter." Now that they had a prospect of making enough money to live upon for a time, they could afford 1.0 part with a few shillings out of the sum Hester had in hand, and when they had finished their plain little supper, they made the rounds of a few shops still open, and purchased such things as were absolutely needed. They were away from Thanieston- street for more than an hour, and when they came back, there was already a parcel of work from Mr. Robertson. Accompanying it was a note, st,ating that all the documents sent were to be copied, and as he was in no great hurry for their completion, Miss Ward and her friend, whose name he had forgotten, could easily do the work in their spare evening hours. He would like to have the MSS. and the copies back again in four or five days, that was all; and he would be obliged if they could be returned to his private address enclosed, not to the office. Mr. Robertson had also thoughtfully put in a supply of the sort of paper he wished to have used, and as Sybil had already bought ink and pens, she eagerly began her new labours that even- ing. "I owe you so much money, Hester," she said; "Ivit I shall soon be able to pay it all back now."</p>
        <p>So far as she and Hester could see, the letters and newspaper cuttings which Mr. Robertson</p>
        <p>desired them to Oit p it at, in copy, were of absolut no importance. They 'coked, Hester thought, lflt things picked up at random; but then, they werOt not supposed to be competent judges of what was, after all, entirely Mr. Tiol-erfson's affairs. He did not come to the office for several days' or if he did the girls did not see him, and Hester 's fears were somewhat calmed by the fact that Mr. Drake did not again appear. They went early in• the morning and worked till late in the afternoon, and nothing occurred to disturb the monotony of their daily life, until after the work which they had finished had been returned, according tO Mr. Robertson's suggestion, to his private address. Then came a letter with a cheque which was far more generous than either of the girls had expected, and a request that Miss Ward would call upon the writer's sister on the following Sunday afternoon. "Some of the newspaper cuttings copied in your harKlwriting were my sister's," wrote Jar. Robertson, "and she was so pleased with their appearance that she would like to see you person- ally, and suggest your undertaking a little more work for her in your spare time. Sunday will perhaps be your most convenient day, and -rnyr sister would be glad if you could call upon her At about four o'clock." The address given was in Craven-street, off .the Strand, and nothing was said about Hester accompanying her friend. "I'll wait outside and walk up and down till you come out," said Hester, "for I don't like to go in, as I'm not asked. But I won't let you go to the house by yourself." The parcel, which Sybil .posted to Mr. Robertson in Craven-street, arrived just as he was leaving the house in the morning, and he had his own reasons for taking it away with him in his hand. He went straight to the office of Anson and Drake, -the solicitors, where he asked to see Mr. Beauchamp Drake.</p>
        <p>"Look here," said Robertson, tossing the package down on the desk at which his cousin was sitting. "this is the first fruits of our scheme. Do yog recognise that handwriting ? " "Yes," returned Drake, who never forgot any- thing which could possibly be of future use to him. "That address was written by pretty Miss Ward to whom I am impatiently expecting that intro-- duction."</p>
        <p>"You said yourself it wouldn't do to hurry the matter on too quickly," Robertson reminded him; "but I have laid the train, and rather cleverly, too, I think. I didn't waste a moment, for the night of the day when we had our talk I won the young lady's gratitude by sending her and her friend a lot of work to do at home. I scraped together all the rubbish I could find, and told her to copy it, addressing it to Craven-street when it was done-- for I didn't want Kittredge or that sharp-eyed Monstance girl he thinks so much of to get into the thing, you know. Now the work has just come back, and I leave the next strp to you. But as I came along I was thinking the matter over, and had an ideawhich may be an inspiration." "Out with it, my dear fellow," said Drake. "Well, you see, the difficulty lies in getting her to meet you,. without rousing the suspicion that you take a particular interest in her; for of course, that wouldn't be wise at first, with this sort of childlike, retiring little girl. It .occurred to me that I might write her a note asking her to call on my sister, in Craven-street, next Sunday afternoon, for some more work. She's almost sure to come.,, "Perhaps," admitted Drake. "But, unfortu- nately, the sister won't materialise so easily. You've only one that I know of, 'and she's in Canada, unless you have collected a few ladies who have promised to be sisters' to you, in a way they are supposed to have." "I have never given any of them the opportunity," said Robertson, rather sulkily, for he was not par- ticularly popular with women, and was sensitive on the subject. "But what I thought of was this. Miss Ward arrives, is shewn into the study. You are, sitting there, and rise, appearing to be sur- prised. She remarks that she has come expecting to find Miss Robertson. You reassure her by saying that you also are waiting for the lady, who is sure to be in soon. Miss Ward sits down; you sit down; and when you have had half-an-hour 'S talk with her—or more if you like—l 4,urriedly make my appearance to inform Miss Ward that mf sister has been unavoidably detained by the illnesS of a friend, but has given me full instructions about the work to be done. What do you think of that?"</p>
        <p>"It sounds all right, if your man, Johnson, can be trusted."</p>
        <p>1 "He can," confidently returned Robertson. "Very well then, that's settled. Say four o'clock on Sunday afternoon. I have an engagement, but it's not half as interesting as this promises to be; so I'll break it."</p>
        <p>"I'll make the appointment for four,",llobertsort agreed; "and you'll be there earlier, I suppose, so that you can be sure of getting in before the girl comes along." . .</p>
        <p>• "Trust nie for not neglecting any of these delicate little details," said Drake, laughing. "By the war, would you mind leaving this parcel of Miss Ward's work with me, or have you an immediate use for it ? " I "Not the slightest. It will go' into the fire if r keep it. But what can you want with the stuff?" Robertson could not resist sliming his curiosity. "Is it possible you are so hard hit that even the dear one's handwriting is precious to you?" And he grinned. "Not quite s 6 bad as that," Drake rejoined, calmly. 'As a matter of fact, lam not quite sure why I do want the stuff, or whether it will be an good to me at all. Still, you may as well leave it. Robertson, always glad of some new way is which he could please a person so important to, him as Drake, 'willingly agreed. The little matter of business being settled between them, Drake did not disguise the fact that he was btisy, and would not be sorry to be left alone. They shook hands, Robertson impressively, Drake lazily; and, the door having shut behind his ambitious cousin, the solicitor took up the parcel addressed to Craven street. He had cut the string that bound it, opened the wrappings, and was in the act of drawing out the contents when Lord Carstairs was announced. Quick as thought, he flung the broken packet int* an upper drawer of the desk. "Let Lord Carstairs come in," he said to the waiting office-boy; and appeared to be absorbed in the perusal of a blue, legal document. In hiss. haste, he had not seen that a slip of paper had fallen out of the open parcel and fluttered into the chair in which Robertson had lately been sitting. There it remained, sticking between the leather cushion and one of the arms, even when Carstairs had entered and accepted the invitation to sit, down. "</p>
        <p>"I'm in a beast of a scrape," exclaimed Carstairs, irritably, "and I want you to get me out of it." With a nervous movement, he dislodged th,o paper and caught it between thumb and finger before it could fall to the floor.</p>
        <p>(To be continued.)</p>
