      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>There is so much in the life and forthcoming marriage of the Queen of Holland, that recalls the early dsy3 of our own beloved Queen, that it will be a pity if the Kruger agitation on the Continent should be allowed to mar the kindly feeling that is everywhere felt for Queen Wilheln.ina in this country. Her Majesty has returned to the Hague a month earlier thaa usual, in order to prepare for her wedding, tut it would certainly be difficult for any members of our royal family to be present at the ceremony and accompanying festivities, if the Queen, who has some what ostentatiously sent a telegram of welcame to Mr. Kruger, as President of the Transvaal, should seem to countenance any unfriendly demonstration against the British. There is nancli in common between the Dutch people and ourselves, and it will not be our fault if we are prevented from taking that popular interest la the marriage of Queen Wilhelmina in which we should all delight. The Dutch Royal Palaces, which are somewhat numerous, are undergoing much ia the way of repairs and renovations just now, especially Her Majesty's favourite residences—the Chateau du Lao, and the Chateau de Soctjdyk.—For the Royal Palace at Amster- dam, Queen Wilhelmina has never cared, and one can scarcely marvel that this should be so, seeing how grim and uninteresting a building it is. Its only distinction seems to be, that it possesses a world-famed collection of Dutch paintings, and—no front elem.! Few amceg us who noted the sad little paragraph which appeared recently, to the effect that the late Prince Christian Victer'e charger heel arrived from South Africa and been sent to Windsor, can have failed to rea- lise in sOille degree, what a painful homecoming this must have been for the bereaved family at Cumberland Lodge. Like so many of the Queen's descendants, Prince Christian Victor was a warm friend to dumb animals, and nothing roused his ire more than to see them ill- treated or ill-cared for. Both dogs and horses knew this, and loved and trusted him accordingly. Princess Christian has, it appears, now quite decided to lea re for South Africa, as early in the New Year as possible, to visit her son's last restime place, not return- ing to Cumberland Lodge until the close of the London season. Her Royal Highness will be accompanied by Prince Christian, and possibly her younger daughter Princess Aubert of Anbalt. Altogether there promises to be a great dearth of Royal folks—for some months to come—in these Isles of ours. The Duke and Duchess of York, and possibly Princess Victoria of Walea, will be absent in Australia, Prince and Princess Christian in South Africa, while the Duchess of Albany,with Princess Alice, will still be residing in Germany. There is some striking story asscciated with the early life of nearly every great musician, and Sir Arthur Sullivan, the greatest of modern British composers, was no exception to the rule. When in 1856 young Sullivan competed for the Mendelssohn scholarship, he was bracketed equal with another lad, and it was only upon a further trial, that the competition was determined in faveur of Sullivan. The remarkable part of that inci- dent is, that while the successful candidate became therl most distinguished of our melodiats, his rival was Sir Joseph Barnby. Like Sir Arthur Sullivan's famous song "The Lost Chord." several of our most precious sacred poems have been produced in a single night, notably the beautiful missionary hymn "From Greenland's Icy Mountains," which was written one evening by Bishop Heber, in order that it might he sung on the following Sunday morning. Another favourite hymn "At even ere the sun was set," is said to have been written by Canon Twells one afternoon when he was kept in school by a boy who had been set an imposition. The national song of Republican France "The Marseillaise" was prc- duced by Rouget de Lisle, in a single night. • In a recent lecture on the subject of " Woman and the Rural Exodus," Mrs T Chamberlain expressed the opinion that if more women could be got to stay in the country. fewer men would leave the land. In any case she:thinks there is a bright future for intelligent women, who are fond of country life, and who are not afraid to take up small holdings for poultry, or dairy-farming, eardening, fruit-growing, bee-keeping, or other small cultures. This is a subject in which Lady Warwick has taken a great interest, and the success attained by the horticultural training college at Reading, certainly justifle3 the belief that many women with a natural liking for outdoor life, would be better employed in such pursuits than in elbowing each other for what is often a worrying and insufficient livelihood in the towns. With the approach of the Christmas holidays, the queeition as to what to do with our boys and girls, becomes a pressing problem in many a home with a growing-up family. It is the generally accepted time for leaving school, when something has to be done to give the young people a start in life. For any boys thinking of going in for colonial farming, there seems an excellent opening in New Brunswick in Canada. There is a training farm started by the government ot the Colony, under the regular inspection of the Depart- ment of Agriculture, and which offees to boys and young men of an eligible class, a three years' practical training in farming without cost, or fees. Theplace is under first class management, and those who have already gone eut, are said to be delighted with the life and prospects it offers. The schema is etvirely inaugu- rated by the government of the Colony, and no doubt full particulars can be obtained from the Agent-General in this country, viz. the Hon. C. A. Duff-Miller, 17, Leather-market, London. Christmas presents are already engrossing our atten- tion, and there is no lack of choice. The long chains make a most acceptable gift, so do the newest bags in deerskin, mounted in enamel. There is a decided revival of carat too, and the charms to be had are both original and quaint, the soft pink shade going beauti- fay with the pastel tints of frieze, and cloth gowns. Umbrella handles kayo come under the magical influence of the enameller, whose imi'ations of birds, are tru y wonderful. Brooches also have birds singly, in pairs, or in groups, all ot many colours. Althrugh this is the month in which there is " sou, no moon, no proper time of day," there is a perfect craze for white raiment. For evening wear it is charm- ingsbut for the murky daytime.in town more especially, it is not only extravagant but unsuitable,and the white careen' and ermine jackets some women effect, ale in ' doubtful taste, and very ugly. The fur necklet, muff,' and headgear, of the moment is white, arid white trim- mings predominate. For instance, an immense sable muff is trimmed with three large frills of white tulle. :and lined with wnite silk, flow our grandmothers would have shuddered at such a combination.</p>
        <p>This is a furry, as well as a white winter, Fs i many materials have a rough fur-like surface. These have appeared under very wonderful names. Among furs to be aveided, is the imitation grey, whichelacking the rich- ness and softness of chinchilla, and silver fox, is very trying to even a brilliant complexion. In colour, it strongly resembles a slate roof in a deluge of rain,and is hard and unbecoming. The new muffs are an improve- ment on the old cask like shape, for they admit of the hands falling into a natural position, instead of being folded in a eraight line. We hear so much about the increase of "hooliganism' and crimes of violence, in which women are so often the sestims,that something mieht be said for the suggestion of a jury in a recent case at Liverpool. This was to the efff cc that in the worst cases of brutality, either on the police or public, the perpetrators should receive fifty lashes on the morning of their release. Many of the worst class of "hooligans" do not care much for heing pent to prism, but with a flosging in prospect, to reflect upon all the time, it would assume a different aspect,anil the punishment would not bs so readily forgotten. Enceuraged by the success ot various unauthorised collections for the war funds a large number of boys and girls have set themselves earlier than umal,to the thinly oisguised form of begging which consists of shouting carols outside people's front doors. This development of enterprise, which, if not b'aspbemous, is at any rare most irreve ent, is one which pouseliolders will do well not to ecaourage. " MADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>PUBLIC NOTICE.</head>
