      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <p>HILL CLIFFE BAPTISTS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Presentation to the Rev. J. S. and Mrs. Hughes.</head>
        <p>Presentation to the Rev. J. S. and Mrs. Hughes.</p>
        <p>The aneual congregational tea in connection with Hil Chile Baptise Chapel, was held on Thursday last 'week. About 150 members of the congregation and friends sat down to a capital tea which was served in the schoolreom. The following ladies assisted at the tables :—Mrs Hughes, Mrs MOSS. Miss IMoss. Mrs England, Mrs and Miss Hallo ws- "ts Richardeon, Miss Worrall, Mrs Her, graves, Mrs Jones, Mrs Scotney, Mrs Daviesr Zdise Griffiths, Mrs Strange, and Miss S J Worrell. Ate tea a public meeting was held in the chapel, the Rev S Hughes (pastor), presiding. The attendance ir eluded the Revs J T Schofield, J F Matthews, and E T Reed. After devotional exercises, The Rev J 8 Hughes real a most interest- ing paper entitled " A review of my seven Years ministry at Hill Cliffe." He said :—" This iS the seventh anniversary of my settlement amongst You as pastor, and it has been in some respects the most remarkable year of my life. The seven years I tave laboured amongst you have teem amongst the happiest of my life, unmarred by a single hitch in my relatieeship to officers and Church until within the last few mouths, and the difference which has arisen is not, irl my estimation, such as cannot be overcome in course of time, and by the exercise of that mutual patience, forbearance and goodwill, which ought to mark all those 'who bear the name of Christian. My relations with this Church have been of a some what unusual kind, and date from the time when I was a student in Manchester College and I carne here to preach soon after the retirement of the Rev. Abraham Ken- worthy. Then I was approached respecting the pas toratc, but I bad just accepted a "Call" to Bacup,where bowever 1 did not settle, until some months afterwards In the meantime I preached here on the occasion of th Opening of the first trarmonium,that was used in connec- tion with this place,that was in 1877. ,It seems a strange providence that 16 years afterwards, I should receive and accept a" to the work of the ministry here. When I received that "Call" there were 39 members .on the Church Roll. Two of these I had received into fellowship on one of my occasional visits. Seven were absentee members living at Runcorn and elsewhere. Two were aged and invalids and could never attend the ser- vices, so that practically there were only 28 members. To-day we have upon our books 75 members, having received four into membership during last year one by baptism and three by transfer. We have distnis-ed to other churches two, and thus have an increase of two on the year. But in addition to that we have sent for the transfer of two more who have come to reside in the neighbourhood, anti four have been accepted by the Church for Baptism. Thus practically we have to-day a membership of 81. This is the highest number reached as far as I can discover since the Latchford Church seceded nearly 50 years ago. I re- member that when I came as an occasional preacher from E,rlestown in 1892 and 1893, the congregation were sparse and easily counted. A. congregation of 50 was considered exceptionally good. Those congregatioes laanavdette a S,incte been doubled, trebled and often quadrupled ,comp,rumten nwdiatnhctehadtuorifntnhethyeeyaerabrefjourset.cloaslet,l ohwuiglth last winter was the worst we have eliccpehrienced for several years and weather counts atHil school was scarcely 80 strong c" 1893,ffenowritt'eovSeurnlig Finding that there was no temperance ing don- in the village.l called toetherinwotrhk at all be- e la:tter part the connection, with ta of 1894 the friends of temperance in co Ebenezer and Providence chapels, with Heath United aut% t we organised the Hill Cliff° and Stockton Hstfithe Band of Hope, which has done good work arria youcg and is still in vigorous existence. For thge time in the history of the church, in 1897 we B.ntrsat candioate for the ministry, in the person of Mr. James Kenworthy, to Manchester College, where be is still making good use ~. f his educational advantages In the ear,y part of 1898 we started a Y.P.S.C.E. and it bas proved a useful and helpful organization, and I trust it will long continue to be a source of strength to the church. In the summer of 1899 I ins ugurated the open air services in the graveyard, which were con- tinued again last summer, and were largely attended, and by many who habitually neglect the services on Lord's day-evening, and we have received many testimonies of the good influence, which has gone forth from these services. Though by no means a latitu- dinarian, I have done what I could by precept and practice, to broaden the sympathies and enlarge the Sphere of operations of this church, with the result that the church has come into closer touch with all denom. inational work and enterprise. The Foreign and Home Mission work hss been better supported. The extension scheme was helped to the 'extent of nearly £2O, and the 20th Century Fend to the extent of nearly £BO, and we took a very early, if not the earliest part in the formation of the Hill Cliffe sub- lisnrict of the Association of Baptist Churches. There has also been developed in our midst a stronger bond of union between us and the other free Churches, so that we have become allied to the Federa- tion of the Free Churches, and we have taken active part in the three great missions organised, and various .Other general operations for the public good, and we hope to take cur part in the great sitnitaneous umission of February next. The finances of the church and echool have been kept all along is a healthy conditiou ; thanks 'to generous helpers, none of whom are more worthy of recognition firm the Bradford family. I think there is a radiant future before us. The young are risieg to iselp us. The growing neighbourhood favour us. and best of all God is with us." (Applause), Mr E England, the treasurer, informed the gathering that the church was in a very flourishing condition and the last year had been most successful. After all ex- Penses had been paid there was a balance on the right 'Side, which, though small, was extremely gratifying, Addresses were then delivered by the Revs E T Reed. T Schofield and J F Matthews. At a later stage in the proceedings, Mr England said he had been desired by practically areventy subscribers to present Mr Hughes with a hand- some study chair which had just been placed on the platform They had a very high regard for Mr Hughes and even those who were somewhat crotchety oh a matter which occurred in the summer, admitted that Mr Hughes was an excellent pastor. They had also a very high respect for Mrs Hughes and they thought Mr Hughes had honoured the church by .tselecting one of its members to be his wife. (Applause.) It gave him much pleasure to present Mr Hughes with that chair and he trusted he would long be spared to use it. (A pplause.) Miss B Moss in a few appropriate words, then pre- mented Mrs Hughes. with an easy chair, and Mr Strange Presented Mr and Mrs Hughes with a handsome time- piece. _ _</p>
        <p>M. E Lloyd Jones also made a few remarkg. Few</p>
        <p>zien had been put into such a difficulty as their pastor had been, and be was .tne very few women hsd under- ;gone such a trial at the commencement of their married life as Mrs. Hughes. They intended to cling to them</p>
        <p>and support them in toe iight,and -vrirkhed Mr. ard Mrs. Eluellea a long and prosperous life. (Applatric.) The Rev. J. S. and Mrs. aughes thanked the sub- seribers in feeling terms for the gifts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>e , -—7 IA &lt;4414: 1, • 5111110bMN t:' .s„ S</head>
        <p>e , -—7 IA &lt;4414: 1, • 5111110bMN t:' .s„ S</p>
        <p>FABIAN LECTURE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>"The Railways and the Nation."</head>
        <p>"The Railways and the Nation."</p>
        <p>The subject of Dette S D. Shallard's lecture at the Druids' Hall on Thursday last week was "The Railways and the Nation." Councillor James Evans presided over a good attendance. Toe CHAIRMAN, in opening the proceedings, said he happ med to be one of those unfortunate men who had hitherto not considered some of the subjects upon which Mr. Shallard had spoken, but he had had his attention drawn to other questions, and had seriously studied them—questions which had in view the wellbeing of the masses. For their moral and social elevation he had always contended. With regard to collectisism versus individualism he would give no definite opinion, but in had occurred to him. As to the subject on which Mr Shallard was to speak, they could never have municipalization or nationalization until the people were educated up to that idea—until, by education, they could rice to the occasion. (Hear, hear.) Mr SHALLARD, who was cordially received, said the subject of his lecture was as vastly important a one as could possibly be conceived. The question of our great railways lay largely at the root ot the prosperity and well-being of the country. Wnat we had to face in the future was no longer a question of socialism versus in- dividualism, but as to whether a man was going to bet an over-worked, under-paid servant of a private com- pany, which could boast of a monopoly, or a servant of a municipality or of the state. There were 500,000 rail- way workers in this country, aud not more than one out of every thousand had the slightest chance to advancement. Their labour and diligence benefied not thenaFelvee, but those who did no work at all. The lecturer went on to point oat that the prosperty of a country largely depended upon its means of rapid transport and interchange of material. This country was, he continued, one of the first to realise that fact, and that was one of the means by which we became as rich as we were and whin enabled us to get such a start of all our competitors. But we had never yet en- joyed anything like the full measure of prosperity which a good railway system made possible, and which would obtain if the State had control of the railways. (Ap- plause.) The lecturer briefly touched upon the history of railways in this country,and incidentally pointed out that while the tendency of prices of other commodities of life was downward the railway rates for the carriage of goods were steadily going up. He alluded to the amal- gamation of the railway companies when they bought the canals, in order to do away with competition, and said the railways had control of the State instead of the State having control of the railways. Nearly every reform for the safety of the public and the welfare of their servants the railway companies had fought against, and nearly every reform they had c trried out had been forced upon them at the point of the bayonet.so to speak, either by the Board of Trade, th House of Commons, or the Post Office, which last named department insisted upon punctuality and fast trains to carry their mails. A railway company Would rather spend £50,000 in oppos- ing a reform than £5,000 in carrying it out. (Laughter). Mr Shallard went on to deal with a number of abuses connected with the present railway system, and re- marked that if the railways were in national hands, and properly managed the change would add enormously to the well-being of the public and the develop- ment of trade, for the evils of competition and monopoly. To sum up, under the esisting system they had an inefficient service. Charges were notably high, wages were notably low, the hours were shamefully long, and there was a loss of life and limb which the companies were quite reckless abetit. The nationalization of the railways, like the municipalization of the tramways, would mean a better system, higher wages, shorter hours, and a bigger prefit at the end, because the railways would be msde popular, and would therefore be more liberally patronised. IF, however, they ever wished such re- forms to he effected they would .have to send te Parlia- ment to represent them men who would be in favour of their views—whe would use their high position to bene- fit the people; and not, as now, merely for the aggran- disement of a small section of the community. (Ap- plause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>PAINFUL CASE OF WIFE DESERTION AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>PAINFUL CASE OF WIFE DESERTION AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington _ Borough Court on Friday, before the Mayor and other magistrates, Harry B. Furnese, of 171, Ordfore-lane, was summoned on a charge of desert- ing his wife, Bertha Furness, on November :17th Mr. Browne represented the complainant, and Mr. Steel, the defendant.—at the outset Mr. Browne stated that it was a very painful case and he was prepared to accept a reasonable offer from the defendant and thus prevent the details. of the case being gone into.—Mr. Steel said his client was anxious for the case to be heard A mutual separation was offered on Saturday morning last 'tit the complainant did not accept it. Tee Mayor said it would be a much happier thing if a settlement could be arrived at.—Mr. Steel produced a letter referring to the defendant which be was anxious should be read privately. The Mayor afterwards stated that the magistrates would adjourn the case for a month if some arrangements were made to protect the wife in the meantime, or they would proceed with the case if they had an assurance that no objection would be raised against their decision on the ground that the defendant was incapable of pleading.—The defendant's actions in the box were of a very strange character and were commented upon in Court. He insisted upon the case being gone into, and Mr Browne proceeded to state his case for the plaintiff. The parties were married he said, on the 23rd of July, 1396 at Great Sankey, and there had been two children of the marriage. Unfortunately during the last eighteen months the defendant had given way to drink and he was perpetually drunk. All means had been taken to get him off the habit. His parents and friends had done everything possible they could for him and re- cently they sent him to the North of Ireland, but he came back before the time. During his absence his wife maintained herself by keeping lodgers, two of them being highly respectable ladies and another an inspector of musketry. The defendant turned up unexpectedly on Saturday morning and his wife asked him if she must keep on the lodger. He said "Oh yes " and then went out, saying he would be back at eleven o'clock. i He did not return until night and he was then found hiding n the yard. He proposed to his wife that they should have a mutual separation and afterwards left the house aeain. He was very drunk when he re- turned and said he had been drinking whisky and he had also had a bottle of champagne to fit him for the job of shooting his wife and himself: He got his cartridges from a a bag and also his gnu :which was in the sitting-room. His wife begged of him not to shoot her, and asked him to think of what he was doing. He replied " I will do it 3nd am ready for the occasion." His wife with commendable forethought asked him for money to buy some bread. He gave her SOW money and she immediately ran out of the house to hr sister's. Information was given to the police and Sergeant Dean on visiting the house found the defendant there with the gun on the hearth-rug. The defendant put his wife in such bodily fear of him that she was bound to leave him. On the following day, he screwed up the windows and fastened the doors so as to prevent her from se tiug into the house. She had tried to get into the house but had failed to do so.—The c amplainent Rave evidence beating out her solicitor's statement.—Mr. Steel crome xamined her with the ob- ject of showing that there eee- eo case of desertion. She admitted that the defendant offered a mutual separation and 15s a we' k ant she did not sign any agreement to that effect. It was (lily a verbal arrangement. The de- fendant's wages were £2 Ss a week when be worked as vanenan for Messrs Furness and Company, buc she ad- mitted that the company was ' now in liquida- tion.—Mr. Steel addressing the Bench as to the amount to be allowed to the complainant said there was no prispect of the defendant being able to earn a livelihood for seine time to come, owing to the condition of his health. If any money was paid at all it would have to come not of the pockets of the defend- ant's parents.—The magietrates granted a separation 1 ordembe defendant 10 pay 208 a weekeued the complain- ant to have the custody of the children.—Tbe Mayor said the magistrates were very sorry about the case coming before the Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>IHE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVENIBER 30, 1900.</head>
        <p>IHE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, NOVENIBER 30, 1900.</p>
        <p>FOREIGN MISSIONS.</p>
        <p>Anniversary Services at Wycliffe Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>Foreign rniQsionary anniversary services were held in Wycliffe Congregational Church and school on Sunday. There were large attendances at all the services and it is ,ratiQtactory to record that despite the many urgent calls which have been made, the collections excueded the amount raised last year. fete p'reacher in the morn- ing was the Rev T Cole, of Garston, who spoke from the words: " And Pilate wrote a title and put it on the Cross. And the writing was, Jesus of Nazareth, the King of the Jews : for the place where Jesus was cruel- fii:l was nigh to the city: and it was written in Hebrew, and Greek and Latin." In the afternoon an adlress was delivered to the scholars and friends in the schoolroom the REV J Parker, who is a returned missionary from Mongolia. Mr George Wallington (the superintendent) conducted the proceedings and in introducing the missionary referred to the great work of the Rev J Gilmour in Mongolia. The Rev J Parker delivered an address on the homes and customs of the Mongols and showed a number of curios which he had brought with him from Mongelia. He also preached in the church in the everting, taking for his text the words : " So then after the Lord bad spoktia unto them, He was received up into heaven, and sat on the right hand of God. And they went forth, and preached everywhere, the Lord working with them, and confirming the word with signs following." The speaker dwelt chiefly on the difficulties missionaries had to contend with in their work in Mongolia, and described some interesting exper- iences. Mention was also made of the medical work which he had done there and of the blessings which had followed it. The people were exceedingly poor and it was with difficulty that they were able to survive the cold weather. Yet at their doors there were large de- posits of mineral wealth which if the people only knew how to work them would make them amongst the wealthiest in the world. Referring to the rising of the Boxers he showed how Chinese society lent itself to the formation of secret societies which misled the people who were very ignorant and easily deluded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>MISSIONLEY MEETING.</head>
        <p>MISSIONLEY MEETING.</p>
        <p>The annual missionary meeting was held on Tuesday evening. The Rev J Yonge presided and the deputation consisted of the Rev J Hadfield (from Lifu, South Seas) and the Rev J Parker (from Mongolia). They were ac- companied by three persons dressed in Mongolian COE- tumes. Miss L Wallington and Miss M Wallington wear- ing the dresses of Mongolian princesses, and Mr W R Beswick dressed as a Mongolian prince. The CHAIRMAN spoke of the gratification it gave him to see so many present and proceeded to state that the Warrington Auxiliary of the London Missionary Society which included Warrington, Lymm, Widnes and Run- corn contributed £B2 last year. As regarded Wycliffe and Cockhedge mission the amount raisedwas £65 6. lid and it was probable that this would be increased this year. Referring to the contributions from native mis- sions he said a large sum of money was raised annually for the London Missionary Society in the native churches in all parts of the mission field. Rev J HADFIELD, of Lifu, s*id he came from a very interesting, successful, and prosperous part of the mission field, and yet he thought there was hardly any corner occupied by the London Missionary Society about which so little was knowa as tbo Loyalty Islands. The missionary proceeded to show that prior to the visit of the missionaries to these islands the inhabitants were cannibals and polygamists. They were now Christians, 10,000 being Protestants and 3,000 Catholics. He bad seen greater self-denial and enthusiasm in Cnriatian work than in the churches of this country. It was sometimes said that they could find no truer test of the spirituality of any church tr.an the mialure of its missionary zeal. Juded by that standard these islanders came out exceedingly well. Tbe annual missionary meeting was a greatlevent in the islands and was attended by all the people. He was glad to be able to say that the amount raised by the natives for missionary effort was more than the expenses of the London Society in the island, land he would be speaking within the limits of truth when he said that the earnings of the people were devoted to religious purposes. The people not only provided the money but also native teachers who had willingly gone to adjacent islands to spread the gospel. He had recently received convincing testimony to the good work which had been done by these native teachers in New Caledonia. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Rev. J. ParticEn,at the outset, described the costumes which were exhibited, and proceeded to relate his ex- perience in the preaching hall which he had erected in the chief city of Mongolia. The difficulty in getting such a hall owing to the opposition of the lahma and the peeple was referred to and mentian was made of the difficulties which were put in the way of converts. Great success had, however, attended the efforts of the missionaries despite many drawbacks. Referring to the charge that missionaries .eet the native magistrates at defiance he said that so far as it regarded the protestant missionaries, the charge was utterly untrue. They had, no doubt, heard about what was called the t.unboat policy in China. It was impossible to do without it. It was no use trying to convince a mandarin by talking to him, he would do nothing unless he saw there was some power behind. Speaking of the recent disturb- ances he said between 20,000 and 40,000 native Christians had been slaughtered because they were Christians, and many of them had been faithful,showing to us what the gospel could produce in the hearts and lives of these people. More than 100 Europeans had laid down their lives for Christ, hospitals, schools, mis- sionary buildings had beets swept away by the great flow of fanaticism. The society was now awaitin;.; the res- ponse of English Christians, which would decide whether their work was to go forward or backwards. The CHAIRMAN said he was sure their desire was that these Chinese missions should go on. Many times in missionary history God had permitted checks ta come in order to try the faith of His people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>ALLEGED WRONGFUL REMOVAL</head>
        <p>ALLEGED WRONGFUL REMOVAL</p>
        <p>OF GOODS. ---- Case at the County Court.</p>
        <p>Before his Honour Judge Gwynne James at the War- rington County Court last Thursday, Thomas Williams, builder, ef Stockton Heath, claimed £4 10s 9d from John Maginn, auctioneer of Bridge-street, damages caused by the wrongful removal of goods impounded in a distraint for rent at the house, 219, Wilderspool-road, Mr. Willett appeared on behalf of the plaintiff and de- fendant was represented by Mr. Forshacv. Mr Willett stated that on the 6th of September the plaintiff instructed Mr Robert Lowe to make a distraint for rent at the house number 219, Wilderspool-road. Mr Lowe went to the house, made an inventory of the goods and gave it to the tenant. Mrs Lamb in the pres- ence of her daughter-in-law. A man was left in posses- sion but as there WAS no sleeping accommodation on the premises the bailiff left at nine o'clock the same night, taking the front door key with him. He went back to the house on the following morning and Mrs Lamb came to him and said " I have something to tell you. My daughter- in-law and her husband went to Mr Maginn's last night and told him what had happened and he came about two o'clock with two or three men and removed some of the furniture which was impounded" The amount of rent distrained for was £8 103. He submitted under the statute that complainant was entitled to treble damages. Mr. Lowe, the bailiff saw Mr Maginn do the following morning and he admitted that he had taken the furniture and refused to return it. A sale of the re- maining goods took place and after payment of ex- penses, realised £3 19s 3d. There was a piano in the house which had been let cut on hire by Messrs Crane and Sons. The. inctrurnent Was not removed as the firm paid Mr Lowe £4 lOs 91 under protest. Mr. Forshaw contended tha the action could not be maintained, because the goods were not the property of the tenant.</p>
        <p>Evidence was also given for the defence, to the effect that the bailiff left in possession a man named Bromley, and did not take the key with him when he left the house. There was also an empty bedroom, in which he could have been aceommodated. Mr. Magiond g as not aware that a bailiff had been in the house, and when he removed the goods he saw the key in the door. His Honour, in summing up, said that Mr. Willett held he had a right to recover treble damages. On look- ing at the plaintiff's statement of claim he found that he did not claim treble damages. Treble damages were by way of penalty and not compensation. It was con - tended by Mr Forshaw that because the goods removed</p>
        <p>were not the tenant's property, the action for breach of poundertge would not lie. He was entirely against Mr Forshaw. Under toe statute of William and Mary the fact of the removal of the goods which were legally impounded, mi,viit entitle a person to recover damages whether they belorged to th.l tenant or not. He oil not think Mr. Williams had suffered any damage, because he had received £3 19i 31, the proceeds of the sale, and his agent had received £4 103 9d from Messrs Crane, which it was his duty to hand over to the plaintiff. They were doing perfectly right in seizing the piano, because the hiring agreement did not protect the hirer from a dis- traint. His Honour was also of the opinion that the bailiff did not lock the dom. at night. He had no right to leave the house, and if there was no accommod ,tiou in the bedrooms he should have remained in the kitchen , He gave judgment for the defendant.</p>
      </div>
