      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>ASTHMA CAN CAN BE CURED.</head>
        <p>ASTHMA CAN CAN BE CURED.</p>
        <p>A Noted Physician will prove this to all Sufferers in Warrington.</p>
        <p>The progress of modern medical science has led to the discovery of successful methods of treatment for most of those maladies once regarded as incurable. Abthma has always been regarded as belonging to this obstinate class, baffling the skill of the greatest physicians. Thou- sands of sufferers know by their own experience that little if any relief has been afforded them by the methods beretcfore employed. Dr. Rudolph Schiffmann, the noted physician, has, after a life-long study of Asthma and kindred diseases, discovered a remedy which not only gives instant relief in the severest cases of Asthma, Hay Fever and Bronchitis, but has actually cured thou- sands whose cases had been pronounced incurable. So complete is his confidence in his remedy,that to overcome the scepticism cf the public growing out of the failure of other remedies, Dr. Schiffmann requests this paper to announce that for the next three days he offers a liberal sample box of " Schiffmanu's Asthma Cure" tree of charge to all persons apply ing at Hill and Co., 62, Buttermarket-street. Be believes that an actual test will be the most convincing and in fact the only way to overcome the natural prejudice of thousands of Asthmatics who have heretofore sought relief in vain Persons living out of town can obtain a packet free by enclosing name and address and a penny stamp to Dr. R. Schiffman, British Depot, 31, Snow Hill, London, E.C., before DecNnber 3rd, as no free samples can be obtained after that date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WARRINGTON FANCIERS' ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FANCIERS' ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Show of Cage Birds and Pigeons•</p>
        <p>The fifth annual show of canaries, mules, British and foreign birds and pigeons was held under the auspices of the Warrington Fanciers' Association in the Gym- nasium on Saturday afternoon. The show which was opened by Councillor L Greening, was a most successful one in every respect and was well attended. For the first time the show was practically an open one, outside exhibitors being allowed to compete in the whole of the pigeon classes. The result entirely justified tbP depar- ture. Whereas last year the entries totalled 250 they numbered on this occasion 464. During the last year there has been a considerable accession to the member- ship of the sssociation,land the local exhibitors were more numerous. As regards the quality of the exhibits there was a marked improvement. The judge of canaries said the birds were better than those he had judged on the previous Wednesday at the show in the Aquarium. London. The pigeon classes also were very strong, and many of the exhibits were of exceptional excellence. In the British bird (any variety) class Mrs Woodruff gaioed the first prize for a redstart and Mr J Borrill the second for a nightingale The birds attracted a good deal of attention. Visitors to the show were thoroughly satisfied with the exhibits geyerally. Mr A Wellings judged the cage birds and Mr H A Monre the pigeons' The Committee of Management was as follows :—Presi- dent, Mr Thomas Mather; vice-presidents, Messrs. fi Clare, J W Johnson, F •Lockyer. W Leigh, .1 Dnsworth. S Fay ; committee. Messrs. I Hampson, F Burrows, R Barker, J S Clare. r Mason, and J Summerfield ; secre- taries, M'a'r. E R Goodwin and S Aay ; as,istant- secretary. Mr F Boyle ; treasurer, Mr S Booth. Appended is the prize list : _ _ _</p>
        <p>PIGEONS (Open Classes).-Working homer (cock) bred 1900, that has flown 100 miles or over: 1, H Moston ; 2, J s Clare; 3. E Smith ; v h c, G Bowdrn ; h c. F Boyle: c, J H Scarratt. Working homer (hen) bred 1900, that has flown 100 miles or over: 1 and 2, J Heald; 3, J Dean; vh c, J H Scarratt; hc, 8 Taylor; c. A Bracegirdle. Red or red chequi,red cock, likeliest for flying purposes ; 1, E Burrows; 2, S Taylor ; 3, C R Kirby; v h c, T Mason; h c, W Gerrard; c, E Burrows. Red or red chequered hen, likeliest for flying purposes : 1, G Lait; 2, J Heald; 3,3 H Scarratt; v hO, W Gerrard; hc, J Forster. Blue or b ack chequered cock,likeliest for flying purposes : 1 and vIIa, E Burrows; 2, D Shakeshaft; 3, J Heald; hc, F Bloor; c, Green and Caldwell. Blue or black chequered ben* likeliest for flying purposes :1, W Leigh; 2, W Hen- ney; 3 and c, E Burrows; v h e, W Kinder; h c. W Spar- ling. Any other variety working homer, for flying pur- poses • 1. E Smith; 2, J Edgerley; 3, E Burrows; v b c, W Leigh, hc, J H Scarratt; c, C Wilson. Any variety fancy pigeon: 1, v h c, and h c, Appleton and Shurrock 2, J Forst e,; 3, Bagley Bros; c, W Humphreys. CAGED BIRDS (Open Classes) --Yorkshire yellow, any variety : 1 and 3, H Cawley ; 2 and vh c, Rathbone ; hc, A Hirst. Yorkshire buff, any variety: 1 and 3, D Orme ; 2, J and J Smith; v h o and h c, H Cawley; e. A Burst. Norwich yellow, any variety: 1 and 3, A G Davies ; 2, H Cawley; v h c, T Fenney ; h c and c, W Bowyer. Norwich buff; arty variety : 1 and 2, A G Davies ; 3 and v h a, F Fenney •, h 0, W Bowyer • c, H Cawley. Crest or crested bred Norwich: 1 and vbc, J Gray ; 2 and h C. W Davies ; 3, T Penney ; c,J D Leigh. B : 1. Mrs Woodruff; 2, J Berirtriislhi ;b3i,rdv, h aanayhdvahrcie,tF c sharp; e, Edwards. Selling class-104 6d including cage: 1, E R Godwin ; 2, A G Davies; 3. II Cawley ; Vh c, J Gray ; h c and c, Davies.</p>
        <p>MIMI:SEES' CL/OSES.-Yorkshire yellow, any variety: 1, J Unsworth ; 2 and v b c, I Hampson; 3 H Clare. Yorkshire buff, any variety : 1 ana 2, I Hampson; 3, E Smith; v h c, G Bilfington ; h c, H Glare; e, J Waine. Yorkshire hen, any variety: 1 and 2, 1 Hampson; 3, J Waine ;vli c, H Bark-r ;h c and e, H Clare. Norwich yellow, any variety: 1, S Fay ; 2, R Barker ; 3 and h c. G Hllington ; v h C. S Booth. Norwich buff, any variety 1, S Booth ; 2 and 3, R Barker; vhc. E Smith. Norwich hens, any varlety : 1,2, and v h c, R Barker ; 3, S Fay ;hc, E Smith ; c, S Booth. Norwich crested : 1, J Appleton ; 2, and v h c, J Newton ; 3, J Unsworth. Norwich crest-bred : 1, J D Leigh ; 2, J Appleton ; 3, J Newt m. Any other variety canary, not pre- viously mentioned : 1, S Fay ; 2, 3 and h c, I Hammon ; v h c, E R Godwin ; c, G Billington. Goldfinch, or linnet mule: 1,2, and vh c, I Ramp- son ; 3 and h c, G Billington. Any other variety, mute, or hybrid : 1, I Hampson ; British goldlnch, or bull- finch : 1, J Lowe; 2 and 3, I Hampson; vhc, 8 Booth; h c, E Smith. British brown linnet : 1 and v h c,S Booth; 2 ana c, E R Godwin ; 3, J Lowe; he, I Hampson. Siski, redpole, or twite : 1 S BooLh ;2, J Lowe; 3, E R Godwin; vhe I Hampson. British hard billed, not larger than hawiinch : 1,2, and c, S Bootn ; 3, I Hampson ; v h c, E R Godwin ; h c, S Fay. British soft billed, not larger than thrash : 1, II Care ; 2, S Booth ; Foreign, any variety, single or in pairs : 1. G Billington ;2, S Booth. Selling class, bird and cige 12s 6d : 1, J D Leigh : 2, G Selling class, pairs, £1 : 1, I Hampson ;2, E R Godwin. Gift class, birds to be sold for Society's benefit : 1, 1 Hampson. NOVICE CLASSE3.-Yorkshirs yellow, any variety : 1 1 and h c, J Drury (Hyde's); 2 and c, J Ryder (Hyde's); 3, J Lowe; y h c, F Burrows. Yorkshire buff, any variety: 1, S Fay; 2, J Lowe; 3, F Barrows; v h c, H Tyson; hc, R Cook; c. J Ryder. Yorkshire hens tinF variety : I, J Lowe; 2, H Tyrer; 3. P Drury ; hc, F Burrovis; hc, G Holden; c, J Ryder. Norwich yellow, any variety: 1, F Burrows; 2, S Fay. Nor- wich huff, any variety : 1, S Fay. Norwich hens, any variety ; 1, E Houghton, Norwich crest or crest bred : 1, J D Leigh; 2, J N-wton ; 3, J Appleton ; v h c,ll Tyrer. Any other variety canary not previously mentioned : 1, R Cook. Mule, any variety : 1, J Lowe; 2 and 3, F Burrows ; vh c, R Co^k ; h c, 11 Tyrer ;c, E Houghton. Bri.ish goldfitvzh : 1, J L )we ; 2, S Fay ;3, El yrer. Any other variety gold inch : 1, J Lowe ; 2 and 3, W Buckley. HYDE'S SPECIAL.-1, J D Leigh. I Hampsoo, Penketti, won a cloy of Pope's Works for the most entries, while the two winners in the two first pigeon classes divided 10d 6d between them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>OVERCOAT WEATHER</head>
        <p>OVERCOAT WEATHER</p>
        <p>Now is the time that we plain Lancashire folk call " overcoat weatber"—it is cold and bleak at nights,and a rasty and:chilling dampness pervades the air. One is only too glad when work is done to hurry away home- wards, and get near the fire. Still there is always the walk home through the damp to be done, and to those who spend their time in the hot, dry, unhealthy atmos- phere of th, mill, this journey is eften very trying. Mufflers and shawls won't quite keep out the cold, and clugo are not always rain-proof. Consequently, colds and sore throats are frequent ailments, and have come to be regarded as two ot the usual annoyances of winter but.friends, colds and coughs and sore throats may even yet be avoided, and rrnv always be cured if the right remedy is taken in ,on. Lavan's Lung Linctus is a right remedy. Man) -.I is the finest and safest and meet efficicnt yet pt, ,suc e, Try a tettle yourself, and judge by results. You wet, be sati.fied, we feel sure. Lavan's Lung Linctus may be had at all chemists, drug. stores. and Cc--ops., and post free in bottles, is. 9.1., and 4s. 61., from the proprie- tors, Green and Co., chemists, Bolton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS. Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovalist."] Another erratic pertormance by Warrington is to be chronicled. On Saturday they went t) Sc. Helens and defeated the Saints, though they were only able to make a draw with them earlier in the season. Since that time, however, the St. Helens club has suffered severely from injuries to players and it was only a weak back division they were able to place in the field for Saturday's match with Warrington. The visitors were fully represented but their advantaee in this respect was soon lost through an injury to narrisort who was kept out of three patts of the game. To win,therefore,with only fourteen men and in fact do all the scoring in that handicapped state, must be counted a good performance on War- rington's pr t. It must be confessed,though, that St. Helens were about As shiftless and shapeless a team as Warrington have encountered for many a long day, and did not take much beating.</p>
        <p>Boundary-road ground is always an awkward place to get to, and no Paradise when you do get there. Under tne conditions which prevailed on Saturday it was a " heluva " place—to use a Kiplingism—except that the cold was intense which is distinctly at variance with ordinary notions of Hades. There was some unpleasant fog about to begin with, and then an east wind sprang up which chilled the very corpuscles in one's veins. gh ! Talk about shivering fits and ague, why, even the players could not keep warm. I marve:led at the idiocy of people who could leave a warm fire side to stand starving round a fence while a lot of tbeir own fellow-fools chased a ball about more or less according to preconceived notions and rules. Chiefly less, by the way. Personally I thought the game was not" worth the candle ;" and certainly I should have preferred the candle if only to warm my hands over. "It were Co</p>
        <p>On the stand, which seems to be a meeting-place for all the winds that blow were most of St. He'ens' crack players." Dave" Traynor, Barnes, and Liversage formed a trio who would have hePn of great service on the field cf play, but the fortunes of war ruled that they should play the unsatisfactory part of spectators. The only thing they could do tor their team was to lead the cheering, which task they attended to very strenuously, and particularly when Fisb got into the grip of his enemies. There had evidently been instructions about " marking" the little tbree quarter, and after the match be assured me that they had been so well carried out that he felt sore all round. For a wonder be did not score a try, but his own side were as much accountable for this as St. Helens. They passed badly and gave him no decent openings. To compensate he kicked a couple of goals and gave the "Saints "a very warm handful generally.</p>
        <p>The losing of the toss was a blessing in disguitie for Warrington, as St Helens had sacrificed their only chance of scoring by half-time. This gave Warrington many anxious momenta, but there was such lack of method and reliance on force on their part that it was not very difficult to keep them out. Boardman kept one man out by the simple expedient of grabbing him by the shirt Collar and after a preliminary swing dashing him on the ground like an enlarged flail. It was quite a fair tackle—t,ne of Boardman's patents you know. He is a useful man at a pinch is A. B. Still St Helens ought to have scored five points for Unsworth at centre missed one of the easiest tries under the bar through not going on, tbat ever I saw. This was after Morrison's retire- ment witn a badly bruised collar-bone as the result of a reckless charge. There was a clear sheet at the in- terval.</p>
        <p>The wind in the second half seemed even stronger and Warrington very soon made their superiority apparent. After ten minutes play Isherwood made a dashing run and gave Dickenson an easy try behind the posts which Fish converted. St Helens uot penaliked immediately after and Fish popped the Lail over again. 'ibis was all the setring but Warrington's paint-compiler had another shot at the sticks and hit one of them, the ball glancing outside. St Eft lens rkrely made headway, the seven Warrington forwards working like fiends and more than holding their own, a, bile in the rear:was Jack Hallam trying to keep warm inside two jerseys and by the aid of occasional mighty punts. It was a very easy victory and with a full team and smarter tactics Warrington should have run up a big score.</p>
        <p>On the St. Helens side was a new three-quarter named Pearson, a schoolmaster, and an ex-Yorkshire County Rugby Union player, I was informeo, who played a fair game everything considered. He is tall and weighty, but hardly seems to have the speed and smartness for the position. He may,tierhaps,improve in this respecc.Foulkes had a lot to do and did fairly well, though not so safe as Hallam. Briers was a long way the best of a set of forwards who ought to have done better than they did. He was always to the fore either in attack or defence. Perhaps St. Helens would transfer him for a cmsidera- tion ?</p>
        <p>All the Warrington backs played a fair game, though the halves did nothing to open the game and apparently want another curtain lecture. The forwards deserve high praise for thn phenomenal display they gave both in the open and in the scrimmage. More weight is wanted before such displays can be looked forward to with any regularity.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows: For Agst. - - - -</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G T. P. ay. P. P. Swinton 11...11... 0... 0... 9 33-117... 8 4 7.8...22 Oldham lO 8. 2. 0...12 31-117... 7 7 35...16 Runcorn ll.. 8. 3 0...17 21— 97... 1314-. 58-16 Broughton R'ng'sll... 6... 5... 0...14 15— 73... 8 13— 55...12 :Rochdale Hornetslo... 5... 3... 2...10 11— 63...11 15— 67...12 Warrington 11. 5 5. 1...12 19— 81... 7 13— 53...11 Salford ll.. 5. 6. 0...17 26-112...19 16— 86...10 Leigh ll. 5 6. 0...13 13— 65-10 21... 83-13 St. Helena ll. 4 5. 2.. 9 10— 48-10 20... 83...10 Stockport 10. 3. 6.. 1. 4 9 3'5... 6 9 39... 7 Barrow lO.. 3. 7. 0.. 9 12— 54...12 18— 78... 6 Millom lO. 3. 7.. 0. 8 8— 40...13 15— 71... 6 Widnes 11.. 3. 8. 0. 8 6 34...10 25 95... 6 Wigan lO.. 1.. 7.. 2. 7 9 41...23 33-139... 4 It will be geen that Warrington have climb'd higher and now occupy a fairly creditable position, though a point per match is not a satisfactory result of the season's play. Runcorn have dropped to third place in consequence of losing at Salford. The Reds just got the upper hand after a game struggle, the final score being eleven points to ten. Swinton brcka another record by winning on the Rangers ground and have now a clear lead of 6 points, though Oldham are coming up fast and wiil have to be reckoned with. Barrow ful- filled my expectations of better things by going to Wigan and winn;ng handsomely. Widnes furnished the surprise of the day by defeating Stockport, notwith- standing their weakened forces. The junior players seem to be settling down a bit.</p>
        <p>Widnes pay another visit to Warringtrn to-day, and as they have already lost twice to the locals they ought to do so once more. lam expecting Warrington to win this match and as Rochdale Hornets visit Swinton Warrington should climb a step higher. I hear that Harupson, the Widnes captain was hurt on Saturday and will be out of the field for a week or two. 6o that this ought to be an additional factor in Warrington's success. There seems to be no end to the run of bad luck which Widneli are experiencing with their players.</p>
        <p>A Warrington Detective's Smart Capture. Detective Foster, of the IVarrington Police Force. effected a smart capture on Tuesday. It appears that information was received by the police, of a case of housebreaking in Salford, and on Tuesday it was discovered that a portion of the stolen property had been pledged in the town. Detective Foster obtained a slight description of the man who had pawned the articles, and at 11.45 he was proceeding along Church-street, when he met a man who seemed to be a stranger, and who tallied with the de:.•cription. The officer questioned him as to where he had come from and he said his name was John Shaw, and he was a collier, of Bolton. The property had been , pawned in the name of John Earnshaw, The de- tective was not satisfied with the man's answers and brought him to the Police Station. Whilst he was being questioned there, the detective noticed that he was wearing a watch chain which re- sembled one that had been stolen. On examining it he found that it was attached to a watch, which was also stolen property. The man then admitted that he had committed the house-breaking and said his name was John Thomas Roberts. He had formerly resided in Pendleton, but had been travel- hi-Jig about the country for the past two or three years. Later in the day a sergeant from the Sal- ford police took the man to Salt ord. The whole of the property has been recovered. Swine Fever.—lnfected Area Freed</p>
        <p>The" London Gazette" on Tuesday night con- tained e.n order of the Board of Agriculture, dated .November 26th, which orders that an area compris- ing the petty sessional division 3 of Wigan (except the parish of Haigh). Leigh, Warrington, St. Helens, Childwall,Prez,•cott, Kirkdale (except the pari,l3 of Garston), Ormskirk, 6outhport, Kirkham, and Amounderne:?s, and the parishes of Inskip with Sowerby, Myerecough, and Bilsborrow, in the administrative county of Lancaster, and also com- prising the boroughs of Wigan, Leigh, Warrington, Widnes, St. rielens, Southport, Blackpool, and Pres- ton, which was a swine fever infected area, shall now cease to be o intected. The order further states, as regards the said area,that no part thereof shall be subject to the provisions of the swine Fever (Movement) Order of 1898. Tbe order_ -oomes into operation on the 2t)h just.</p>
      </div>
