      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>WHOLESA.LE poisoning by Pure Beer. bear which is not pure</p>
        <p>has once more raised the question which has agitated the growers of malt and hops for many years. It is not neces- sary to enter into the technicalities of the dis- cussion to see the gravity of the case presented for public decision. A definition of pure beer has 11 been pressed for and it has been withheld. Under no circumstances could it be regarded as beer con- taining arsenic, and that must be conceded by the brewers mad the excise authorities and the general public. Beer so contaminated has been supplied to a large extent, and no one can tell what injuries have been due to it—diarrhoo 1, in the summer, degeneration at all times. Half the evils put down to alcoholism may be due to the chemicals used in the manufacture of beer. The cases in Manchester, Liverpool, and Birkenhead had seemed to be wholly- due to excessive beer- drinking. It is now found that they are duo to a deadly compound in which arsenic has been figur- ing to a considerable extent. Arsenic accumulates in the organs of the Lody, and therefore the daily beer-drinkers have been simply poisoning themselves without being aware of it, and their friends have been moralising at their expense when the guilty parties have been the brewers' chemists and the brewers, whose neglect appears to us to be almost criminal. The public will not rest satisfied now, however, with being simply told that the arsenic is due to impure sulphuric acid used in the manufacture of brewing sugar and glucose, and that steps are being taken in the districts which have been affected to prevent a recurrence of the evils which have wrought so much mischief and caused several deaths. The law will have to be altered so as to prevent similar evils in futare, and the firms which have supplied the impure beer ought to be at once prosecuted by the Government. The latter action may assist in showing what ought to be done with respect to legislation. But we do not find any alacrity on the part of the Government. Fifty lives have been lost by poisoned beer, and yet the brewers are not prosecuted. Increased expense has been cast upon the local authorities, and the ratepayers will have to suffer. Another point suggests itself, involving the inattention and laxity of the local authorities. Samples are supposed to be taken of the foods and drinks sold in a district for analysis by the local authority. How was it that none of the beer sold in the places mentioned was analysed before the general outbreak, or, if analysed, was not found to contain arsenic ? This is a question for the Local Government Board, and it is a very serious one indeed. Under the recent Act more stringent regulations were:made, and local authorilies were supposed to have been braced up 6 their duties. That Act came into force at the beginning of the present year. But we have :been unable to find a single case in which this poisoaed beer was independently and spontaneously analysed until medical men began to suspect that something was wrong. This is not creditable to large municipal authorities like those of Manchester, Liverpool and Birken- head. The brewers have been making very large profits for many years, and the materials they have used in addition to malt and hops, or in sub- stitution for them, have been extremely cheap. The danger the public runs in allowing this range of chemical substitutes is now made apparent in a striking fashion, and it must give a stimulus to the agitation for pure bear, which has often been re- garded as a form of protection to agricultural in- terests. „Sir Cuthbert Quilter, M.P., will now see that thepublic will look upon his pure beer crus- ade as something more than a humorous effort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <p>IT is satisfactory to find Municipal Purity. that there is some dispo-</p>
        <p>sition to press home the moral of the Manchester municipal scandal, and to demand a higher sense of rectitude amongst m 11111•• cipal bodies. Direct contracting has seldom been attempted, and it is expressly illegal. But sub- contracting has found much favour. The first is, condemned by the Municipal Act of 1881 ; the second is aimed at by the Local Government Act of 1892. The latter is as bad as the former, if not worse, because likely to be more insidious. No member of any representative municipal body ought to be interested in making any profit out of business done with the body to which he belongs. This is the one condition of incorrupt service. Any suspicion on this paint should be pushed home and disposed of in one way or the other. Coun- cillors and Aldermen are elected to serve the com- munity, and not to serve themselves, and when they begin to abandon the former for the latter, or the former is not its own reward, they are no longer worthy of public confidence and respect. The election of persons connected with the buildhig trades upon Town Councils, County Councils, and local bodies, is a very questionable proceeding, and it can hardly fail to lead to abuse and scandals. Why do such persons dabble itt public affairs at all? That is a pointed question. The answer may be equally pointed, and in many cases it will be true. Because they want to know what public improvements are likely to go on, - because they desire to dabble in matters con- nected with theirlown occupation,and because they want to have a hand in hindering or passing the plans of others. Our readers, who have been be- hind the scenes, will probably say that some of these reasons are strictly true. Lord ROSEBURY warned the London electors against putting any persons on the new Borough Councils who were connected with the building trades. Good men there are in these occuputions, men who would scorn to be unfair, favour a friend or attacx a foe; but there are others to whom no such language could be applied. They are better outside. , The Council has its paid officials to advise it, its- legal assistance, its architectural assistance, its instructions from the Local Government Board. What does it want with builders, cement makers, architects, timber-dealers, and brick makers upon the Council? They are almost sure to be animated—consciously or unconsciously— with trade ideas, sympathies and prejudices. If they can at times be of use to the Council and the Municipality, there are many occasions on which they are likely to do a disservice. In other ways too, the standard of personal rectitude and public morality needs tod'be higher and better. We want to see men entering upon public life with a clear and a keen conscience, and intent on giving to the locality in which they reside a pure, a patriotic and a wholly unselfish service. As we are in our local so we shall be in our Imperial politics. We shall have more and not less local politics in the future, and our ideal must rise instead of fall. Public affairs should be in good hands. No com- mon prejudice against any class is implied. We do not object to the working classes sending their best men to the service of the Municipality. It Is their duty, and the men are wanted, and they are indispensable. And so of other classes. True men alone are required, and when the electors ask for them, elect them, and honour them, the supply will be ample.</p>
        <p>In their memorandum to the County CJuncils the members of the Automobile Club seem make the not unusual error of ovbrstating their case. There is an endeavour to make a point. I'7 referring to the prejudice which was felt againscl railways at the time of their introduction, an arguments are quoted from high quarters to the effect that trains could not go with safety at a greater speed thati eight or Line miles an hour. Of course, the cases are not analogous. The question as to the rate at which trains could travel safely was a scientific one in the main, and had re- ference to the conditions under which the whe.ele of engines and carriages would keep the rail&amp; The matter is entirely different when the question is to determine practically the rate at which a driver can be trusted to direct a vehicle propelled by mechanical means through the midst of other traffic. It also seems unwise of the autornobilists, if they really wish to be allowed to travel along the public highways at the rate of twenty-four miles, to raise so strenuous an objection to the numbering of vehicles, as has been suggested. Even at present, when a speed of twelve miles an hour is allowed, and a considerably greater rapidity often indulged in, according to the confessions and, indeed, boaStS of automobilists, it is difficult enough to bring to book any driver who may have committed one of the small offences of the road. If automobilists would give way on this point it would greatly smooth the path towards a removal of special legislation with regard to speed, placing on drivers of motor-cars the same obligation to regard the public safety which rests on those in charge of vehicles of any other kind.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rouse, the American millionaire who has been offering huge rewards for the recovery of his eyesight, has now abandoned hope. He apparently clung for along time to the belief of little Paul Dombey that money can do anything, and he ev hibited it in a curious way. He heard of a man who was afflicted, like himself, by the atrophying of the optic nerve,and he offered this sufferer large sums of money to submit to experiments by sill' goons, who were also handsomely recompensed for their tremble. The result is nil. The hope was that what was designed to benefit the individual might at least produce some general results to the advantage of the community. But it was not so' This method of employing one's riches was nob very noble ; but the other blind man was a free agent, and eager to enter into the contract ; and, apparently, he was of much less use in the world than the millionaire manufacturer, who not" wichstanding his infirmity, still conducts alinge business. On the ground of utility the actin° might perhaps be approved, though any extensive„ use of the principle would be highly dangerous. would lead to an exaggerated appreciation of golu' Mr. Rouse made his own fortune, beginning as e country-side pedlar. He was a tremendous worker, and is the victim of his inability to rest.Mr, Pulitzer the proprietor of the" New York World." 191 threatened with a similar catastrophe, but p up in time to be able to distinguish faces and t° ride on horseback, although unable to read. Jut cannot be said, in either case, that the greed 01 money has been the cause of these trials. Ft,' strangely enough,hir Rouse brought on disease PaJ giving all his leisure hours to the telescope, Whe.,o after the strain of the day's work he should bay rested.</p>
        <p>High-class music is not getting the sup Port once had from the general public, and especia''' is this the case in provincial towns like Warrhigtet A good number of people attend good concerts, i law s true, but the number is not large enough, an, moreover, there is a distinct tendency on the Pa.' of the elite—or those who like to be included_,ln this category—to patronise the cheaper seats. we cannot blame them for such economy, but vie would point out that it is rather hard ea beat the concert promoter to have his afford, seats tabooed by people who can to pay for them. Mr. Edward Lloyd, in_ conversation last Wednesday, alluded to the. po,ef public support given to high-class concerto IP t" provinces, and said the tendency was very notice- able. Probably he had Mr. Pearson's concert, at</p>
      </div>
