        <p>THE EX &amp;MINER, FRI DAY, NOVEMBER 30, 1900</p>
        <p>SUCCESS OF A LOCAL DOG EXHIBITOa. Mr. Witdgoose, of the Wilsou'R Hon!, Runcorn, was again successful at Barton-on-Trent Dog Show, on Sauurday, winning two second prizes and three third prizes with his smooth collie bitch puppy Marston spray, which met as many as 28 competi- tors in the Open class for rough or smooth collies</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH. At the above place of worship on Sunday the Rev. J K Hill, from China will preach morning and evening, on behalf of the Foreign Missions. In the al ternuon there will be a young people's Mission ary meeting, in Brunswick School, at two o'clock and on the following day (Monday) the annual meeting will take place at St. Paul's Church. Tle chair will be taken by Mr. R E Garnett, and ',Aro interesting branches of mission work will be uealt with, the Rev J K Hill, speaking of 'China ;" and, the Rev. J B Foster, of " the West Indies," An earnest appeal is made that the meeting should be successful. See Advt.</p>
        <p>BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL. The members and lriends of Mr. T. Simister's Bible Class, assembled in the Guild Parlour of the above school, on Monday evening last, and enjoyed themselves socially. Mr. Simister was present and was greeted warmly upon rising to open the proceed- ings He spoke of the good work the class had accom- plished, and hoped that each individual member would realise what a power for good he might become. A capital programme followed. Mr T Norman Johnson gave " Neath the rolling tide" and "The Skipper" ; Mr E Kniveton, " The Rely City" and " Mary " ; Mr W F W Jones, several sketches, including " Various " and "Morning Toilets," causing uproarious merriment ; Mr A Povah sang "Simon the Cellarer," and recited " A.8.C.1; "On the rolling deep" was sung by Mr A Williamson; Mr W Millington read" The Spins- ter's Meal " ; Mr J Leathwood rendered " Calvary"; and Mr George Hayes contributed a humorous speech ; all of which items were accented and enjoyed by a decidedly appreciative 'audience. In ' the course of the evening also Messrs W Milling- ton, James Lea, T Sanders, J Littiemore, and others delivered short addresses. Mr Reginald Johnson was accompanist. The proceedings were of a most enjoyable character throughout, and were brought to a close with the singing of the Doxology. NEW TRIAL QUASHED. In the case of Linaker v. Hindley, which Judge James adjudicated upon the last County Court day, and which he allowed to stand for application, for a new trial, owing to the defendant being unrepre- sented through the mis-carriage of a telegram, Hiudley did not appear on Thursday.—His Honour. There is I suppose no:miscarriage of a telegram this time. (Laugnter.) The case was struck off and Mr Linaker was allowed his costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>MASONIC PROVINCE OF CHESHIRE</head>
        <p>MASONIC PROVINCE OF CHESHIRE</p>
        <p>The Hon. Alan do Tatton Egerton, M.P., the Pro- vincial Grand Master-elect of the Province of Cheshire, presided on Wednesday at the annual m_etings of the several Cheshire Masonic benevo- lent institutions, held at the Crewe Arms Hotel, Crewe. Colonel Dean, the retiring chairman of the Masonic Charity Institution, was re-elected,and Mr Henry Jackson was appointed vice-chairman. The Charity Couticil were appointed for the various divisions. Mr P H Annett,Rancorn, was re-appointed treasurer of the fund. Messrs C S Dean. H Jack- son, and F Broadsmith were elected trustees of the Bsnevolent Institution. Bro Sim was re-appointed treasurer of the Educational Institution, and. Bro tevenson hon. secretary.</p>
        <p>A DEMOCRATIC SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>That Runcorn has possessed a Democratic Society</p>
        <p>for a considerable time, might surprise some people but if they could have peeped into tha Royal Hotel on Wednesday evening last, when the Society held its fint annual supper they would have been more surprised. They would have beheld a goodly com- pany of about 40 respectable working pe3ple, clothed and in their right minds, enjoying them- selves finely over a delightful supper well served by Nir flaming, the proprietor. The president, Mr J Kendrick addressed the gathering, and was ably supported by Mr Bancroft, after wnich songs were given by Messrs J Wakefield, Pye' Prince, Shingler, and a reading by Mr flaming, the whole concluqing with "Auld Lang Syne.' The secretary of the Sniety is Nlr J Wakefield; treasurer, Mr A nam- ing, and Committee, Messrs J James, J Colman, C Davies, G Curzon, J Leyland, and John Massey. OUTRAGE ON THE BRIDGEWATER CANAL AT</p>
        <p>RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, on. Wednesday. Ff. Spencer and his</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <p>wife, Elizabeth, boat-people-, were charged with infl cting grievous bodily harm upon James Lamb, an elderly boatman. Lamb's boat was moored opposite the alum works, Runcorn, when the prisoners' boat came along An altercation ensued. Spencer jumped upon Lamb's boat, throwing his waistcoat off as he did so. Lamb picked up a bolt to defend himself, and ran Spencer off. Spencer struck Lamb with a whip,and his wife joined in the attack with a mop. Both then got the old man down across the rail of the boat, and fiercely assaulted him. The old man wa3 carried home. Dr Nl'Dougall informed the bench that his injuries were very serious. Probably the internal organs were injured. He would be bedridden for a month or six weeks. Prisoners were remanded to Saturday next. ABOMINABLE MISUSE OF CHARITY AT RUNCORN</p>
        <p>Thomas Mackie, of Widnes, Williun C Lk-Iwatt, a Chester rough, and his wife Mary, a Wrexham wo- man with a recard,wera charged at Runcorn, before Messrs F J Norman and W Handley, with being drunk and disorderly,the previous day. The woman was also charged with obstructing Sergeant S Greg- ory whilst in the execution of hi a duty, with wil- ful damage to the cell, and with using obscene lan- guage.—Prisoners pleaded not guilty. —From the evidence of Sergeant Gregory and Constable Ed- wards it appeared that the party had been on the "spree" together, and were having a row near the Duke's Stables, in Waterloo-road, wneu Sergeant Gregory came up to them, and told them to stop Gregory then want in seen% Of assistanca,and Con- stable Edwards was found. On returning, Mackie was found in Mr Weston's chemist shop, where he had gone to tryand get his wounds attended to,and his condition had been the means of attracting a very large crowd of children. Caldwell was found with his head against a wall vomiting. The officers I took Mackie into custody, whereupon the woman ran up and dragged at Gregory's arm. Mackie though violent was at length safely locked up, and shortly afterwards the Caldwells were secured. The woman was violent and abu- sive, but the man was very quiet. In the ced Mary Caldwell conducted herself very ill, and several times Sergeant Gill, the Divisional Secretary, was disturbed at his work in the office above,and had to go and remonstrate with her. To concha le the woman knocked the glass oat of the call " peep- hole" with her thimble.—The Superintendent said that the Caldwells had only, the previous Saturday, been liberated from Walton prison, where they had been undergoing a sentence of four months for stealing from the person at Froisham. It appeared, also,that since the party had come to town they had been spending money lavishly,changing a sovereign here and a half-sovereign there. He could only sur- mise that the money was a portion of that which was stolen at Frodshain, tor there was a matter of .£7 lOs unaccounted for, and supposed to have been hiddeu.—The female prisoner said she could account for the money they had. They had been given .E 2 lOs by the Visiting Committee and the Catholic priest.—Mr Norman said it was the more abomin- able, that, what was given as a charity, should have been spent in going about tne town playing the " Davil."—Mackie was sentenced to fourteen days; the male Caldwell to twenty-one days; and the woman was convicted for each offence, and fined fifty shillings in the aggregate, or twenty-eight days' hard labour,</p>
      </div>
