        <p>COUNTY COUNCIL LECTURE.—Under the auspices of the Technical Committee of the Lancashire County Council a lecture was given on Friday evening in the Church School, Wqolston,h7 !Tv, Rrowc, on "Poultry 11.reping." !dr William fought • s .(1. The lecturer dealt with the subject in an able m r. dhl the interest of her hearers throughout. At tile )f be lecture several questions were asked and answered.</p>
        <p>BLISS FliSDlllt Suites anl Coal Vases in large varietv.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Briclo-street.—Advt. LADINV BHT GLACH 80013 AND SHOR3 urni RE CLOCK, BUIDIaIt-STIMIM—Advt.</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM• PLEASANT SATURDAY EvENINGis —A. meeting was held in the Town LW' on Saturday evening, wnen Mr A. Stanley White presided. The speaker was the Rev G C Percival, wha spoke upon the temperance question with Biblical referenoes. Various songs, recitations ik3., were contributel by the Misses Isaac, Miss recitations, Maddock, Miss Harper and Mr T j ohnion. THE DEATH OP MR JOHN POLLARD.—me remains of Mr Pollard, whose death was recorded in our last issue were conveyed to their last resting-place in the grave- yard of the Frodsham Parish Church on Thursday in che presence of a large number of syrnpathiz -re represent- ine the Frodsham Savings Bank,the schools, and othere. The Vicar (the Rev U B Blogg), officiated, assisted by the Revs W Dawes and R Colston (curates). The ser- vice was choral. The coffii, which was a handsome ono bore the words" John Pellard, born 14th April, 1826 ; died 20th November, 1900," was borne upon the shoulders of ',Veers James Illidge, John Forster, T Tanner. and John Holland. A number of beautiful wreaths were sent. SUBSCRIPTION CONCERT.--Tho first Of Mr C H Hibberct'e concert&amp; was held in the Town Hall, Frodsham, on Thursday evening. Messrs C R Riley and C H U bbertt opened the proleedings with the piano- forte dust overture to "Caliph oT B agda I." It was brilliantly ezecutel. and was followed by Mr A M Proctor with Pinsuti's Bedouin Love Song " which WAS greatly enjoyed. Subsequently, he contributed tile ballad " Only ouce more" and "The Ban iolero" wirh an encore for each. "The Vicar of Bray " was his last sow,. Mrs Rees' harp solos of selections of Welsh airs ancrlrish airs were greatly appreciated, and the violin solos by Miss E Newton Smith—Max Bruch'e " Swedisn Airs," and Erafield's "Romance," with Wienawaki'e " Mazurka "—were delightfully renderel. Mr F W Spencer mad 2 a reappearance before a Fradsham audience, his ceetributions, "Alice, where art thou? and" lam waiting," being faultlsssly dealt with. Miss Cameron's slags were also a success-3 thee'a die and "One spring morning," and in conjunction with Mrs Rees the duet" Echoes,' Mrs Rees' songs were " When Celia sings," and " Villanelle " ; for the former, responding to an encore, she gave "Come back to Erin," with harp accompaniment. A musical sketch ani a humorous song by Mr C R Riley completed the pro- gramme.</p>
        <p>A Grammar School Mister Thrown from a Bicycle. A. serious accident betel At. R Anion, A.A. one of the assistant masters at the Grammar School on Saturday afternoon. While cycling home he had the misfonune t 3 be thrown from his bicycle at the corner of Church-street and Mersey-street. He I was taken into Ar W Ashton's shop, and afterwards sent home in a cab, where it was found he was suffering from serious internal injuries. lie was attended by Dr Langdale, who called in Dr Rich- mond for consultation. Re is progressing satisfac- torily. Accident at Longford Wire Works. A shocking accident occurred at the Longford Wire Works on Wednesday afternoon, to a man named Joseph Jackson. While following his em- ployment, his clothes were caught in the machinery and he sustained serious injuries, namely a scalp wound and the loss of a finger. He was taken to the Infirmary and detained a 3 an in-catient.</p>
        <p>Sankey Bridges Wesleyan Band of Hope. A. successful entertainment in connection with the above Band of Hope,was given in the school- room on Monday evening. The chair was occupied by the Rev J S Hughes,of Hill Cliffs, who delivered an encouraging address. An interesting programme of recitations, glees, songs, &amp;c., was submitted. The Sankey Bridges United Male Vole - Choir, under the able conductorhip of Mr T Broug'n make their first appearance and scored a distinct success by their rendering of "Comrades song of Hope," for which they were encored. They complied with" Bumble Bees," and later in the evening were again recalled for their singing of " Boys of the Oid Brigade," responding with "Men of Harlech: Recitatieas were contribu- ted by Clara Halfield, Je4sie Brown. E Kindon. It Webb, B Payne, Martha Darby, A Foster, Hilda liiiditch, and E Pacy. Messrs R Cook, junr., and D Dunn, were heard to advantage in the duet" Peace and War" and Mr Cook, sear., creditably sang " ['he Slave Ship."—On the motion of Mr Brough, suppor- ted by Messrs Corradine and Blythe, a hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman for presid- ing.</p>
        <p>Warrington Markets. At the Warrington corn market on Wednesday new wheat fetched 3s 64 to 3s 7d (701 b to the bughel) ; new oats' 2s 4d to 23 51 • flour, best seconds, per 2401 b, 21s; bran lid per 201 b. At the Provision Nfarket prices were as follows :—Fresh butter is 31 per lb; cheese, 6:1, 74 and 8i per lb ; fresh eggs, 6 for is; fowls, 4s to 54 61 per couple ; English beef, 6id to 104; mutton, 7d to 10d; lamb, 94 to lid; veal, 74 to 101 ; pork 61 to 811. Death of a Workhouse Inmate.</p>
        <p>The police -leite received a report of the death, which took place at seven o'clock on Friday even- ing, of Joseph Taylor, an inmate of the Warrington Workhouse. Taylor was suffering from chronic bronchitis when admitted into the house ou Wednes- day lay, and had been attended by Dr Braide. On the Oth inst the deceased attempted to cut hi; throat, but the wound was only a slight one, and death is not attributed to that cause.</p>
        <p>COMMERCIAL FAILIIIIES.—ACCOIdIOZ 0 " FCrmitys Mercantile Gr,z Ice." the number of New Bankrup-cv proc?edinge in E,iv,land and Wales gazetted duricg tit week ending November 24th. was 108. The number in toe corresponding week of last year was 83. singeing an increase of 23, being a net increase in 1900, to date, of 225.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>'which he sang so well, in his mind at the tima. The better seats were only sparsely occupied, and the proceeds cannot have been reassuring to the promoters. In fact Mr. Pears= threatens us with comic opera or the ballet, unless our appreciation for good concerts increases. Musical societies, generally, have the same complaint to make, and we believe it is a fact that few, if any, of the provincial Musical Societies are at present able to make their concerts pay. This is a serious reflection oh the public taste for music, and we hopo the reproach will soon be wiped out.</p>
        <p>During thA eight years and a half of its exist- ence the Wai rington branch of the National &amp;)ciety for the Prevention of Cruelty to Children which includes Runcorn and Frodsharn has done a large amount of valuable work in the district. hundreds of IL mos and thousands of children are the better for the ministrations of the Society !Ind. its officers, but like many another deserving institution it is not supported as it ought to be. It is all the more necessary that the Society's use- fulness should be impressed upon the people able to subscribe to its funds, because quite recently the Altrincham and Bowdon section was detached from Warrington and formed into a separate 'aistrict. Prior to this severance—necessitated by the num- ber of cases--Altrinchain, Bowdon, Lymm and Oughtrington subscribed yearly about £2OO to the Warrington district, so that the loss is a very severe one financially. In comparison also it is worth noting that the subscriptions for Warrington itself are just under £lOO. The need for assistande is therefore very great if the work is to be carried on effectively. During the half year, ending Septem- ber 30, in the Warrington district there were 186 eases investigated, 150 warned, 19 convicted, and 17 otherwise dealtiwitii, the number of children affected being 682. Such figures speak for them- selves, and we trust their eloquence will persuade many people into helping the cause of the young and helpless.</p>
        <p>His Honour Judge Gywnne James has had a very brief stay in this circuit. At the rising of the Warrington County Court last Thursday night CO uttered a few farewell sentences prior to his departure for the Bath and Swindon circuit where he succeeds the Hon Arthur Russell, Q.C., son of the late Lori Chief Justice. During the twelve months his Honour has presided over the Warring- Court his unvarying courtesy and impartiality !lave gained for him the respect of all who have n.ad business there and his departure for another circuit will be generally regretted. On many afl. occasion in dealing with poor persons his Honour's firmness has been tempered with kindness and sympathy and last Thursday in taking leave of the Court he expressed the opinion that a word of kindness from the Bench did a great deal more than a severe sentence to induce Persons to pay their debts. Owing to the large number of judgment summonses at 'Warrington he Suggested that an extremely good work could be 'dune if they obtained the services of a Court Dlissioner, for, said his Honour, "a little kindly advice from a Court Missioner would have greater effect than even kindness from the Bench." The suggestion is one worthy of consideration and there seems no reason why the Police Court bilissionary might not find in connection with the County court, an extended field for his useful and benignant labours.</p>
        <p>are a staple article of winter food for a large popu- lation. The expon trade, which extends further every year. now reaches to the Mediterranean and the East. The real Yarmouth bloater may be had at Suez. The value of the herring voyage to Yarmouth is considerably more than a quarter of a million annually.</p>
        <p>Poor-law guardians in some of the Unions are not taking kindly to the new order of the Local trovern- meat Board relating to Workhouse dietaries. In most cases the matter has been submitted to the medical officer and the master of the house, for recommendations to a committee or the whole body of guardians. But certain of the latter appear un- willing to accept any recommendation, and urge their respective Boards to petition the Local Govern- ment Board to repeal the order. Others, while ac- cepting the order as inevitable, declare that the new dietary will cause an enormous increase of expendi- ture. Thera do not appear to be any members,how- ever, who will plainly say that the new order would not be advantageous to the poor, and especially the more feeble and ailing of the inmates, and certainly this is about the most inaportant point.</p>
        <p>Fond mothers who rush to the nearest chemist the moment their offspring develops the lease sympton of uneasiness or possible illness, should pay some attention to the remarks of the Bolton corou- er this week,at an inquest on a child who died from an overdose of "infant cordial." The Coroner de - dared that the health of cnildren in the large manu- facturing districts was being ruined by such drugs. The extent to which children are dosed with these mysterious compounds by ignorant mothers is really amazing. The cordial as a rule does more harm than good, and then more is purchased to remedy the evil which the first administering has caused, and the innocents are thus made continually to Buffer.</p>
        <p>There has been a very sad outbreak, with fatal consequences, of typhoid fever at Plymouth, directly traced to the milk supplied by a dairy- farmer in the neighbourhood. The epidemic is now subsiding, but a few days since there were more than forty cases. and there have been several deaths. According to a statement made by Dr Noy Scott, medical officer of health for the district, the disease was imported from Ireland, and though the original sufferer does not appear to be very ill, be was able to communicate the fever to his mother, and through her to the whole of the persons who have since contracted it. One effect has been to direct attention to the milk supply: Dr Scott said that if the Dairies, Cowsheds, and Ililkshops Orders had been in force, the dairy in question was one he would have condemned. And this is the lesson which authorities elsewhere ought to take to heart, since the best of orders is of no use unless it be enforced. Professor Long, referring to the matter, says that thousands of lives are lost every year in consequence of the filthy conditions under which milk is sometimes handled, both before it reaches, and sometimes after it has reached the consumer.</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGIUS RESBILVEDd LIOH.NSING REFORM,</p>
        <p>BY THE HON. SIDNEY PEEL.</p>
        <p>Iv. DEFECrS IN THE LICENSING AUTIIORITY (CONTINUED.)</p>
        <p>One of the causes that, especially in the counties leads to inefficiency of administration is the vague ness and shifting composition of the licensing body and the consequent absence in too many cases of a real sense of responsibility. In some divisions there are a very large number of justices qualified to sit, sometimes too many, sometimes too few attend; those who attend on one occasion will very likely not attend the next, and any real continuity of policy is thus made very difficult, Stories are not unknown of applicants for licenses delaying their applications, till they can come before a bench so constituted as to be likely to be more favourably disposed. To some extent this is remedied in the towns by the licensing commi..tee, but this techni- cally can only deal With new licenses, though in practice, especially in the better administered places, the lieenhing committee tends to deal with renewals and transfers as well. The evil effects of</p>
        <p>this lack of definiteness in the licensing authority are seen most, perhaps in the case of transfers, which are granted at special transfer-sessions, and of what are called temporary transfers, or inrerim authorities to sell till the next transfer sessions,</p>
        <p>which can be granted by any two justices in petty sessions. Transfers are really much more impor- tant than renewals. As we have pointed out, they . _ . .</p>
        <p>afford 'a real opportunity for inquiiing into the con- ditions under which a licensed house is carried on.</p>
        <p>and should never be granted without careful con- sideration of all the facts. Con•inuity of policy, however, is most unlikely to be carried out by a licensing authority of consmntly shifting composi- tion,and it too often happens that inquiry is passed over at the temporary transfer on the ground that everything will be fully gone in o at the transfer sessions; while at the transfer _sessions inquiry is</p>
        <p>equally omitted on the ground that eveiything has been already fully inquired into at the tempor- ary transfer, especially as the applicant for trans- fer comes up wi.h all the prestige of a man already in possession. Whatever change is made in the constitution of the licensing authority,there can be no doubt that it should be composed of a definite committee, not too large and not too small ; large enough to provide a proper quorum, and not so I large as to take away the sense of personal respon- sibility from its members.</p>
        <p>judicial point of view would be" whether one house</p>
        <p>-should lase its license more than another," to which the answer must be "_no " The administrative point</p>
        <p>of view would be, "Here is hum being done 6y an excessive number, one or more licenses must be abolished, so we will do something.'' It is the same wi.h transfers, the imp .rtance of which we have so _often dwelt on. What can be</p>
        <p>done by a bench acting with more appreciation oZ its powers, is shewa by the case of Bradford. After the Act of 1882, giving the justices free and un- qualified discret on to refuse off beer licenses, the justices decided to grant no more transfers, wlic:rt after inspection of the neighbourhood and the pre- mises, they came to the conclusion that the license was not required, except when a license holder wished to transfer the license to _a _member of his</p>
        <p>own family. In this way 254 off licenses mostly held by grocers have been abolished since 1882, and there has only been one appeal against ref usals. Liverpool is perhaps one of the best examples of more efficient administration About 1889 an agita— tion arose in that city, which resulted in freeing the Licensing Committee from a close conneclion with the liquor trade, in similarly purifying the Watch Committee, and in improving the system of police inspection. By 1896 the Licensing Com— mittee, under an able chairman, had reduced the number of on-licenses by 144. They had a full knowledge of the circumstances of individual houses, they weighed their own conduct in the bal- ance, and sometimes withheld a license, even whea there was no conviction against the house. The Licensing Committee undertakes practically the whole licensing business and make excellent regula- tions as to the alteration of premises, c osing of back doors and the like. They have also endeavoured to prevent sale to children under 13 -IR would be:easy to find instances of ulaces,whera an exactly opposite state of things prevails, but it would be an invidious task to select particular ones and a long one to name them all. EXEMPTION ORDERS, &amp;c. Besides the ordinary licensing business the jus. tices have in their hands the granting of what are called exemption orders and occasional licenses. Exemption orders are rather temporary or perman- ent extensions of hours of opening of a licensed. house for particular reasons; they are granted by two jus ices sitting in petty sessions. Occasional licenses are authorities to a publican to sell liquor on premises other than his licensed house not earlier than sunrise, not later than 10 p.m., on a special occasion.; e.g., fairs, &amp;c. They are in fact licenses for an extra temporary public-house, and can be granted by one justice usually acting for that sion, sitting anywhere, Many benches have passe 1 resolutions binding themselves to greater strictness of procedure t han that laid down by law. In Leeds, for Instance, a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter. Now the electric lighting is becoming so general it is needful that attention should be directed to the danger which occasionally arises from the leak- age of electricity. This has recently been the cause of many serious fires in various parts of the pro- vinces. When electricity was first used as an illuminant, it was hoped that the public would escape the perils attending the use of oil and gas. This, however, is not the case. Indeed, almost every week conflagrations are heard of that are due to the leakage of electricity, and it would be well in the interests of the industry and the country if not only electricians, but the Board of Trade would pay special attentiom to the subject. It is of import- ance, of course, that a careful inspection of the installation should be made from time to time. The prudent man will do this as a precaution against possible disaster ; but as neighbours may suffer through the neglect of this simple z measure, the authorities should consider whetner . n is not time. that inspection should be insisted i upon, f necessary, by statute. It is well known that through " earthing " a very high and danger- ous current may be tapped through telephone wires, gas pipes, or ordinary voltage electric cables, and a defective system of installation may BODIIe day cause a dire calamity. It will be generally admitted that the system of wiring should be reformed as soon as possible. The dangers have already been pointed out in the press, but hitherto nothing is known to have been done to lessen or remove the danger. -Yet regulations can be intro- duced without much difficulty while the system is practically in its infancy; though the authorities may find it no easy task when the system has been extended as it is very likely to be.</p>
        <p>Members of the Leicester Board of Guardians who recently " tested their strength by measur- ing it with that of the Local Government Board on ' the Vaccination Question, and were found wanting, have now had the little bill, amounting to £1,351, presented to thew, and, as the sympathisers with them in their persecution have subscribed the necessary funds, there will be no further oppor- tunity for these gentlemen to add to their notoriety by going to prit-on in default of paying the costs of the cuabble they caused. At any rate, it is hoped they will be ,:ontent for some time Po come with sur:h satisfaction as the quarrel with the authorities .afforded them.</p>
        <p>As an aid to temperance, the Manchester revela- tions as to the possible constituents of a piut of " fourpenny " Hbould carry soma weight with habitual beer-drinkers. It has there been dis- covered that the extraordinary epidemic of arsenical Poisoning, which for some time past has caused Widespread consternation, arises from a quantity of arsenic in the beer supplied by a Manchester brewer 'rile poison the other day was traced to a sugar, manufactured by means of what is termed "Com- mercial sulphuric acid," which is used in brewing the common kinds of beer. Yet, while Adam's ale find milk are under auspiciou, there are many thirsty creatures who find solace, and will continue to do so despite these "warnings," in what the Bachannalran poet has described as "Beer, Glori- ous Beer!"</p>
        <p>Christmas will have a special attraction for many of the employees of the Great Western Railway. 'Company, and one that may compensate them in some measure for the extra labour imposed upen them by the British public about that time. A commendable effort is made hy the Company to render their stations more attractive by means of tastefully arranged gardens. For a quarter of a cen- tury, indeed, the staffi have been encouraged to manifest a pride in their stations by making the best of. the otherwise vacant land adjacent to the , line, and travellers over their rails have frequently acknowledged the result to be admirable. As an ad 'itional stimulus to this kind of" hobby," the Company are offering liberal money prizes in suit- able instances, ranging from 103 to £5, and for the pa,t, twenty-five years the money so disposed of t,eaches the considerable total of .C 250 per annum. zlo that the staffs at the various stations have thus been placed in healthy as well as pleasing com- petition with each other, the outcome of which is seen in the attractive floral displays observable along the Great Western Railway routes. In award- 'l4): the prizeg, regard is bad to the maintenance ot uniform standard ot excellence throughout the season, the progress made compared with former y.ears, the special circumstances of each station, situation, climate, soil, and so forth, and the 'neat- hess and cleanliness of the stations generally. The re!mults are considered by the Company at the ter- mination of each season, and the prizes are distri- buted at Christmas. Other of the great companies !ave somewhat bimilar arrangements, the panicu._ ea's of which, however, are not so available, or they would be as cheerfully noticed.</p>
        <p>it is good for a poor aud tleeerving class to hear hat the herring fishery sPason. which is now aIEII3St, ,iver, has been a record one. The great fleet of boats hat gathers at Yarmouth every autumn is dispers- ing in sections to every home port as far north as erhead. Thirty steamers with salted herring ",+igoes have now left Yarmouth for the Baltic, Where the fish from our east coast curing-houses</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>RUNCORN CYCLE CLUB.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN CYCLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>Annual Dinner at the Wilson Hotel.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <p>The large Billiard-roam of the Wilson Hotel, was on Wednesday evening last, almost filled,when about 60 of the members of the Runcorn Cycle Club with their irieuds sat down to an excellent dinner, capably served by the host Mr Wildgoose. Mr Taylor, LP., Rydal Mount, presided and there were also present—Messrs F J Norman, J.P., and H Congrieve, C.E., of the Man- chester Ship Canal. Dinner over, and the tables cleared Mr S Taylor addressed the assembly. Having referred to the pleasure the occasion gave him, and to the success of the club, he said there was perhaps one object more important than anything else which the Runcorn Cycle Club had practically made its own and that was support of the Cottage Hospital Fund. (Applause.) In the past year he was sorry to find they had not made the pro- gress they usually did in twelve months. In fact a twelve-month ago he had proposed that a Cottage Hospital would be an accomplished fact, but alas it was not so. This was in a measure due to the luncorn people having to contribute very generously to the War Funds. However, those funds would soon be required no longer and he hoped that the club would be successful in turn- ing the generous flood into the coffers of the Cottage Hospital Fund. (Applauie.) He took a deep interest in it and he felt sure they all dicl—(hear, hear,)—and if the Cottage Hospital was only the means of saving one life, when it was erected, ho felt that anything he could have done would b 3 fully repaid. (Applause.) Mr Taylor in the course of his remarks also threw out a suggestion that the Club should run a ladies' branch. (Hear, hear.) The first part of the progranime was then proeeeded with. Mr E Johntsen sang "Stand by the Flag" with such verve and effect as to fully merit the recall he got. The inimitable Matt. Kilduff gave" I'd like to go halves in that," and a female representation of "rho Anchor's Weighed" that was perfect. Mr W Shaw followed with" For Qaeen and Country," and gave " Mona " as an encore. " When Other Lips " was a graceful tenor rendering by Mr E Worrall. "We went a hunting" a most humorous comic song by Mr G Whinyates followed, and resulted in his unanimous recall " I wonder why 1" was the encore. Mr Mitt. Kilduff's "When father laid the carpet on the stairs" was altogether too amusing to withstand, and he added " I stuck just so" in capital style.</p>
        <p>Tie Chairman called upon Mr. H Congrieve, the late president, to distribute the prizes. On rising Mr. Congrieva received an ovation. He pw eenvt e by hhei a had f the t thatpi d e ofpleasure said that he had been with the club for the past threyears.ta . Ttehle, efirtasttwoyeware,rehos of handing over the presidency to the safe keeping of Mr, S Taylor- (Applause.) And this year they had succeeded still further and had Mr, F J Norman present (applause.) He hoped they would go on and eventual! have the whole array of Vice-presidents laminar; at that table. (Hear, hear.) Having deait with some reminiEa c 'aces of his hobby in cycling. Mr. Coniarieve sounded a note of encouragement regarding the Cottage Hospital, and hoped all would go on and on until it was an accom- plished fast. The workingmen of the town had done their share and in fact contributed ot the amount promised. It now remained tor the better classes to take up the gauntlet that Mr Taylor had thrown down a twelve month ago and do their part. (Applause.) Mr Taylor had also re- ferred to the fact that the people had been exeeedinglv generous in contributing to the soldiers and Tailors, and otter funds. So they had. (Hear hear.) He could say that the working people connected with his own par- ticular place, had contributed something like £129 to the war funds and had some £2B collected for the Indian Famine Fued• (Applause.) Having reviewed the eitua- tion Mr Congrieve concluded by remarkinf. that they only wanted some £4OO to complete the £l,OOO required before they could start, and exhorted all co put forward their best endeavours to secure it. (Loud applause.) Prizes consisting of valuable engraved gold medals were then presented to the following :—Wednesday Sec- tion : Mr George Whinyates. Mr S Hughes, and Mr W Hind. Saturday Section: Mr W Booth, Mr A Steel,and Mr H Niell. Mr Congdon) again offered a medal as a prize for a two mile race, an announcement which was greeted with applause. S Caylor proprised,and Mr F J Norman seconded a hearty vote of thankg to Mr Congrieve, as he had to leave for another engagement. Reverting to the musical programme the assembly were then favoured with " Mother's Darling " from Mr George Whinyates. Mr Matt Kilduff then appeared in a humorous selection. First came "The Pilgrims of the night," then "Are you coming back," and a recitation "The schooner phosphorous." The latter was of a side- splitting nature, and descriptive of a voyage on the Ship Canal. Toe duet " Excelsior," very effectively rendered by Messrs Jonnson and Worrall followed. Another break was made at this point, and Mr S Taylor caged upon Mr E J Norman to propose the toast of the evening, the health of the club. This Mr Norman did with his usual grace and ease, and paid a high compliment to the club. Referring to the costaae hospital he illustrated that his workpeople had raised about £220 for th soldiers fund.—(applause)— and had a further balance of some £3O in hand. He was not going to say anything that would commit them in any way, for his :lien were a thoroughly domocratic lot, and carried on their clubs etc., haw they like). (Hear, hear). But from whispering that he had heard he did oo think it unlikely that if they considered that the £3O was riot needed for the naldiere, they wou'd be prepared to hand it over to the Cottage Hospital Fund. (Leiie Applaose). He was seilerised that a teem like Runcorn stuck at raising £l,OOO for a colage hospital, and felt it ought to be raised in twenty-five minutes. (Hear, hear, and ;applause) Having credited that club with all they had done. he pat the toast with pleasure, coupling with it the name of Mr. W Boothe-he eiceretary, who responded, thanking</p>
        <p>Mr Ta!,-ior, Mr No:man, and Mr Congrieve for their attendance on beha!f of tho Comrnitto 4 and members. The rest of the eveniug was spent in a convivial manner, songs being given by Mpqsrs G Urmson, E John- son, E Worrall, and W Neil!. Mr Syd. 13,ckatt was the very efficient accompanist. Votes of th, rills to the Chairman and host concluded a jolly gathering,</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER THE CLOCK Fon BRsr BOOTS IN LANCASH I It E. —A.dvt</p>
