      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>SERIOUS RECEPTION.</head>
        <p>A SERIOUS RECEPTION.</p>
        <p>Another most potent factor in producing a King; Log rule is the widely prevalent idea that the jus- tices in their capacity of licensing authority are a purely judicial body, which cannot act except upon evidence officially brought before them, and tha-, they are a court of summary jurisdiction just as much as they are in the trial of offences. The real fact is that they are an administrative body, just as much as any board of guardians, or any other local administrative body. For some time the High Court gave colour to the purely judicial view by holding that the licensing justices were a court of summary jurisdiction,but the recent case of Boulter v. Kent carried to the House of Lords has set the matter right,. It is true that by the Licensing Act evidence given on the hearing of an application for renewal must be given upon oath, but the jus'ices are not bound to act upon that evidence; it is perfect- ly within the competence of justices to refuse the renewal, because on coming down to the sessions they have passed through the district in question, and seen,- with their own eyes that the licensed house is not required. They can even refuse it without hearing any evidence at all, only if they do hear evidence, that evidence must be on oath. When the Lord Chancellor in Sharpe v. Wakefield, said that the justices must act judicially,he did not as has since been explained, mean that they were to act as though they were a court, bat that they were not to act arbitrarily, and without considera- tion of the merits of the case :he was in fact merely laying down a rule of conduct,which should govern all human action, whether in or our, of court It is not unnatural that the justices should be inclined to take the purely judicial view, because the rest of their business at petty sessions is nearly all purely judicitA, and when they act first as licensing authority and then as judges hearing the trial of offences in the same room, it is not easy for them to</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>divest themselves of the idea that they ought to act as members of a judicial court; in both instances. The old licensing law carefully laid down that the licensing authority was to be composed,of the local justices, because they were supposed to be person- ally acquainted with the requirements of the dis- trict; it was their personal knowledge that quali- fied them for their position, and the same is true to-day; evidence given before them is merely to assist them.</p>
        <p>A right view on this subject is of the utmost importance; it goes to the root of good adminiscra- tion Evidence of a far reaching nature of the mis- convp- ion is riot far to seek. In Hun the justices, startled at the frequency of transfer, and the reported insanitary condition of many of the licensed premises,requested the Watch Committee to personally inspect the houses, as they themselves were reluctant to do so, believing them- selves "tobe a judicial body." The Watch Com- mittee declined, and the justices eventually went and saw for themselves, and were glad that they had done so, as it gave them " such an intimate acquaintance with all these houses." Mr. Roberts, clerk to the justices of Newcastle (speaking before the decision of the Boulter case) complained very strongly of the inconvenience caused by the supposed fact that the justices were a judicial body and could not take cognizance of anything until it came before them. In Staffordshire, the Chief Constable, Captain the lion. G.A. Anson, said that no one but the police ever thought of raising objections to renewals or transfers, and it it were not that the police had special instructions to bring up the record of a house he did not believe that the justices would take any cognizance of a conviction against the house, and would not consider it among the facts brought before them, even when it was recorded upon the face of the license. By these means in many places the past conduct of a house escaped scrutiny, and renewals and transfers were granted without due examination. The justices of Stafford- shire, he was careful to add, are not at all worse than their neighbours. This no doubt explains, apart from their desire to protect what they conceive to be the rights of pro- perty, why the jus ices a-e so much more active in refusing new licenses. New licenses always excite a good deal of opposition; no one likes to have a public-house planted next door to 'him, and objec- tors, among whom neighbouring publicans are not the least energetic, are usually ready to come for- ward; so that there is plenty of evidence to go upon in refusing, But even so there are not want- ing instances in which the justices have planted licenses in localities in flagrant disregard ot the wishes of the inhabitants. The justices are indeed as a rule very careful not to increase the number of licenses,and some benches will no grant a new license, unless one or more are surrendered somewhere else This plan has at first sight the advantage of offering a method of reduc- tion, but it has its dangers which should not be overlooked. The licensing authority may be tempted to grant a new license in a district where the in- habitants do not wish for it by the expedient of re- moving two or three licenses from a congest -d dis-. trict. And the process is really an illogical one ' for, if the new district really wants a license, it ouzht to have one, and if the old district can do without the surrendered houses,their licenses ought to be taken away in any case. In fact the wants of each district ought to be considered on its own merits, without reference to the wher Khowever, the surrendered houses are ante-1869 beerhouses, whose licenses cannot be refused, he justices under present conditions are justified in making the bar- gain, provided that they have first satisfied them- selves independently that the new license is re- quired, and that they have made a good bargain in point of value. WHERE LICENSING JUSTICES ARE ACTIVE</p>
        <p>The notion that the licensing justices ought to make no effort to find out things for themselves,but should wait to decide till things are brought before them, is largely responsible for the growth which he tied house system has been allowed to at tau. It is remarkable that in Scotland where the licens- ing authority has been more active and has never been hampered by any theory of" purely judicial" [powers, the tied house system has scarcely gained a footing. Such a notion makes any Yeduction of hou6es an impossibility, for who is able to decide which house of several ought to go when each house is superfluous in an equal degre9, with the others, if the decision is looked at merely from a judicial point of view, and not from the administrative? The</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <p>good example, occasional licenses and exemptions are only granted on a particular day, when most of the licensing committee are on the rota for attend- ance, after application has been duly entered in book,and the occasion is also nocitied to the police. Elsewhere notice of the application to the police is required beforehand. But individual juAtices some- times ignore these recommeniations, and grant oecasional licenses freely without ths. knowledge of the police. It should certainly be statutorily laid down that no occasional license should be granted except in open court after due notice to the police. As an instance of the abuses, which may now arise, a case from Cardiganshire may be quoted, when on the occasion of a fair held in a hamlet of 11 houses, 9 occasional licenses were granted, the only houses not licensed being the school and the meeting house. There is also a curious custom itz the same county. When a person has lot a pig, or cow, or horse, an occasional licenoi is granted for his benefit to some publican; the neighbours assemble and drink, the profit going to compensate the loss sustainsd. In one case a jusrice granted. such a license on behalf of a widov.- whose husband had committed suicide through think. Heavy drinking went on for days, but the police did not know ot it till afterwards. At Aberystwith a justice granted 20 occasional licenses (eacn lasting 6 days) in the year to a place, which had been refused a permanent license by his:own bench. Considering the great number of public-houses there must be few occasions on whicl an occasional license is really necessary at all; and ct -eany large meetings for sport or business, t ley are not in fact allowed at all, with conspicuous success. We have said enough, to show that the licensing justices are ordinarily inactive in the performance of their duties, and the abuses connectA with occa- sional licenses shew that some at Least of them are not fit to be entrusted with any licensing duties at all. But the al ministration of the county justices cannot be properly estimated,till we have examined. their action at quarter sessions. THE APPEA.L TO QUARTER SESSIoNS, . .</p>
        <p>The sins of the licensing justices in the first in- stance are chiefly sins of omission, the fact than they Ii Te in the district, and must kuow something of what goes on there, has no "oubt same good effect; gut it is not too much to say that the jus- tices at quarter sessions have done their very best on many occasions to suppress any effort of the local illBLiCt3B after good administration. Here we have not a mischievous inactivity to complain of, but an actual, almost malignant activity, the par- alyzing effect of which can hardly he estimated. It must be remembered, in estimating th s effects of the borough justices, that they always have this dead weight of county quarter sessions to contend with, quarter sessions from which they are ex- cluded. Accordingly the grievance is greatest in reference' to appeals from the great towns. anyone will take a list of the 50 largest towns in England out: side Lendon (which alone is organised like any other county in this respect) he will have a list of the 50 largest towns which complain of this system of appeal. This partof the law dates from 1828 be fore the growth of the great cities, and is now ut- terly antiquated. If there is anything at all in the,. principles of local self-government, in which all Englishman believe, our town and cities must be, able to manage their own business beater than ap, purely ex, ernal authority, superior in no degree always inferior in some respects, and often in all to the local authority. For the appeal lies from the borough justices to a shifting set of county justices who know lees of the local circumstances, are not superior in ability,nor better lawyers, an appeal too on points of administration, not of law merely. Sometimes a decisien arrived at after most careful consideration, and with full knowledge of the his- tots, of a house by a most competent bench of city justices is reversed by two or three county justices, sometimes by a perfect mob whipped up by a doubt- ful influence from all parte of the country. At Sheffield, in 1894, 14 licenses were refused by the city justices, some of them after repeated con- victions for permitting betting or drunkenness. One house in particular was of a disorderly char- acter,bad a conviction recorded against it for being " the habitual resort of prostitutes," had been put out of hounds by the nailitare authorities, and was further not required, there being 35 public-houses within:e radius of 210 yards,and was even still occu- piee by Ye same tettent.Yet this license,and 12 out of the remaining 13 were given back by the West Riding quarter sessions at Wakefield-60 justices from all parts of the county assembledenost of them complete strangers to Sheffield. At Plymouth seven justices unanimously refused a license to a tenant twice convicted within the year for serving drunken men. Three county justices sitting at Exeter, 52 miles away, reversed their decision. At Hull the justices refuse renewal to a man wtee had been granted a license on condition that he would keep only a hotel pure and simple, and, who, then breaking his pledge opened a mere dram shop. Quarter sessions at Beverley immediately gave the lieense back. Innumerable instances could be given. No won- der that the licpior trade regsrds county quarter sessions as its stronghold. Can any reasonable man doubt that the present licensing authority and the appeal body stand most urgently in need of re. modelling ?</p>
        <p>Next Week: "RECONSTITUTION OF THE LICENSING AISTIXORITY.'^</p>
        <p>Death of a Local Baptist Minister.</p>
        <p>The. death is announced at Little Leigh, near Northwich, of the Rev John Aldis, Baptist minister. He went to Cheshire from Batley five years ago. Mr /Adis, who was 60 years of age, was one of a family distinguished in the Nonconformist world. Ills brother was the first :to secure a degree at Oxford when the University was thrown open to DitVietltdr.4. The deceased gentleman was well- known in Baptist circle in this istrict and oa several occasions addressed meetings in connection with the Baptist denomination.</p>
        <p>aaVfotiILNISIG Ask to see the New " WAVERA Cloth. Very sruart nd se.yiable for Blso, D..etutns. Also a cboico miortrocut of other Idakes.—W. T. WILLiAms. Ssnkey•stri•et and Leet-street.—Advt. ktes. Oil ~to - . to 6, Bri4e-3treet. Warringtou.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>• et, • •r• – •</head>
        <p>• et, • •r• - •</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES</p>
        <p>FOREIGN MISSION SERVICES AT lIALTON-ROAD. Tee Rev C II Hocken, missionary from East India, represented the Foreign Missions on behalf of the parent society of the Wesleyan Body, at tne Halton-road Chapel, on Sunday last, where he con- ducted the services both morning and evening, and held a successful meeting in the afternoon. The collections amounted to about £lB. A SMALL CALA.NDER AT THE SESSIONS. The magistrates who composed the Runcorn Bench on Monday, were Messrs A Thomas and W Handley. Owing to the scarcity of offenders they soon dispose I of the work in hand.—Alice Johnson formerly of Rune )ra, was sent to jail for fourteen days' hard labour. as a punishment for indecently ! conducting herself in the street. John Rauican, Riley's Row, Weston, was fined 6d. an I 43 61 costs for neglecting toe educiv,ion of his child.— Chis , case was proved by Mr W B Brimelow, scnool at- tendance officer to the Runcorn Guardians. FORMATION OF A PUPIL TEACHERS' CENTRAL CLASS AT RUNCORN. At a special melting of the Runcorn School Board, on Thursday evening, the Rev Canon Wood presiding, it was decided to establish a central class for the pupil teachers of Runcorn and district at the Granville-street Schools. Mrs F. Hawkins Ambler has been appointed to conduct the class. GOSPEL UNION MISSION, WIDNES. On Monday night, a reading entitled, "The Man that Spoiled the Music" was given in the West Bank Mission Room. The reading was illustrated by limelight views. Mr Emery was in charge of the limelight apparatus. At intervals refreshments were served oat. There was a crowded audience and the proceedings :were a decided success. BURNING ACCIDENT AT WIDNES. On Sunday a schoolboy named James Ward, aged four years, 88, Dock-street, was playing with some other children in an empty house. They made a fire, when, unfortunately, Ward's clothes became ignited, causing severe injuries to his back. He was conveyed to the Accident Hosuital, where he was attended by Dr. Hutchinson. He is in a very critical condition.</p>
        <p>TEMPERANCE SUNDAY IN WIDNES. At nearly all the Nonconformist places ot worship in Widnes special services were held on Sunday in aid of the Temperance Niovemeut. Mr J Gallon, a student from Manchester College, occupied the pul- pit at the Trinity Schools, and preached two very powerful sermons in condemnation of the liquor traffic. All the churches were well filled and go3d collections were taken at all the services. UNITED METHODIST FREE CHURCH, WIDNES. On Sunday the annual sermons were held in con- nection with the above place of worship. Tne morn- ing and evening services were conducted by the Rev, J Osburne, M.A„ Runcorn, but the afternoon musical service was held under the presidency of Mr. T L3cke. On Monday evening a public tea was given, and a grand concert was held under the chairmanship of Councillor T Atherton all the items being contributed by the Sunday school children. WIDNES WESLEY GUILD. There was a good gathering at the usual weekly meeting of the Lacy-street branch, held on Friday evening, where a very brilliagt paper was read by Mr. Frank Littler on " Some British Methods of Tne essayist contended that Britain's method of civilization did not at all redound to her credit. The various means by which Nnglani ob- tained sole possession of her colonies were exhaus- tively dealt with, and, on the whole, condemned. Mr. :Littler demonstrated that he p issessel a thorough mastery of his subiect. A very lively discussion followed,in which Messrs. Seddon, Poole and others took 1110. The Rev 11 Barraclough occupied the chair.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN LIBERAL CLUB. Mr C Kniveton took the chair at the weekly debate, at the Ruucorn Liberal Club, on Tuesday, when the Lecturer was Mr I 0 Williams. The sub- ject was "The British Democracy a reflection." In the debate that followed, Messrs J Lea, E Pear- son, Richard Ilughes, T Dean, Bowyer, W B Brime- low and others took part. The usual votes of thanks closed a successful meeting. —Next week, E McHugh, Liverpool. will lecture on " The Labonr Question; What it involves and how alone it can be settled." The meeting is opm to the public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <p>OAKLA.ND-STREET WESLEYAN P S.E., WIDNRs. A large gathering assembled at the above rooms on Saturday, under the presidency of Mr J II Spiby Miss Clare sang very ably " Tivickenham Ferry," and further enhanced her reputation by her render- ing of "The New Kingdom" Mr W Abbott's " I seek for Thee" was creditably rendered, but "The heavenly War" was more suited to the tastes of his audience,wbo compelled him to grant an encore. Much praise must be Riven to MiSA J Leach's recic- ing of" tary Queen of Scots" anti '` Prayer and ' Potatoeq," the moral attaching to the latter poem being enforced in a very striking manner. To the violin solos of Miss Anderson, entitled," Jenny Jones" and" Blumentied " can be recorded much of the success of the concert, while Sir Abbott's sketch, entitled "Engaged," evoked con- siderable enthusiasm. The pianoforte solo of Mr 0 A. Leach was very much enjoyed, and he also per- formed the duties of accompanist in a praiseworthy manner.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS FIRE AT WIDNES. On Monday morning a somewhat serious fire broke out in a woodea erection which stands by the side of the Cheshire Lines Railway, situate in Appleton.street. The building is principally used as stables and piggeries. Considerable damage was done to the building, and also the harness and straw which were stored inside. The fire was dig- covered by John Davies, signalman, West Barak House, Widnes. He immediately raised an alarm, and a plentiful supply of water was poured on to the burning mass. The fire was therefore fZ ot under in a comparatively short space of time. The Wid- nes Borough Fire Brigade, with hose cart and reel, also materially assisted in fighting th 4 flames, The property belongs to Patrick Leonard, 2, Catherine- street, Widnes. It has not transpired whether the damage is covered by insurance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>DEATII OF A. RUNCOMNIAN IN AUSTRALIA.</head>
        <p>DEATII OF A. RUNCOMNIAN IN AUSTRALIA.</p>
        <p>The " Toowoomba Chronicle," of Saturday,October 20th, contained the following notice of the death,at the age of 65, of Williatu Dutton, a native of Run- corn:—" We regret to chronicle the death of a i well- known and respected Toowoomba resident n the person of Mr William Dutton, who passed away in Brisbane on Wednesday. The deceased gentleman was for many years a teacher of music in this to vn and district, and he was also highly accomplished in many other ways. He was very prominent in , Masonic circles,and was for many years organist of Itl:P.1 Scottish Lodge. To his respected wife, who ic well-known among us as the matron of the Brodribb Home, we tender our sympathy in her bereave- ment " The deceased was the son of Mr William Dutton, who filled the post of superintendent of the old Brunswick Sunday School from 1829 to 1858. He served his apprenticeship as a school teacher at the Granville-st. ••••;.;liopls. The family were highly respected in the town,the deceased's father being a prominent figure in the early history of local Wes- leyan Methodism. Deceased was the uncle of Mr Charle:i Dutton, of Runcorn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>HARDSHIP.</head>
        <p>A HARDSHIP.</p>
        <p>Mr. George Walker, a young man almt 23 years of age, died very suddenly on Wednesday last, from an attack of pneumonia. He was at his work at Messrs. Timmins, on Friday last, •apnarently enjoy- ing good health. Arrangements for the funeral were made for Saturday,but in the interval the one-year and five months old child died, and application was made to bur 3 it along with the father. The Ceme- tery Cornmittoe,however,owing to their regulations were obliged to ask the poor bereaved widow and mother for another Li, which she could ill-afford, betore that arrangement could be carried out. It is said that an attempt will be made to refund the sovereign, but that is beside the question. The fact remains that the poor woman was asked to find the money. Could not something be done to migitate such cases?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>TrntMOTHER OF A LARGE FAMILY.</head>
        <p>TrntMOTHER OF A LARGE FAMILY.</p>
        <p>The grave closed, on Thursday, over the remains ! of a retnarkabl 013 lady. Mrs Ann Ciiffe,an inmate of the Dutton Workhouse, for the past six years, died on Sunday last and was buried on Thursday. All her life previous to her removal to the aim'- house was spent in " Ddph a property belonging to Mr. Strirger. of Delph Bridge, which was condemned by the Urban District C3uncil as unfit for human habitation, and here she attained tha ripe age of 78. She was very eccentric, and was seldom or never seen out of her house by any of her !, neighbours. She was the mother of eight children: and had 19 grandchildren; and 39 great-grand- children. At her death she had attained the age of 86 years.</p>
        <p>PRoFESioR SMITH. BUSINvAS Writing rini BooRKEPT- IN6 Academy, 47, GREENA.LL-BTREET, WARRLNGTON, Operl every day and eveuiug.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>– :AA&amp; • .</head>
        <p>- :AA&amp; • .</p>
        <p>RUNCORN NOT ES</p>
        <p>[By a Correspondent]. Let us look for a moment or two neon the affairs of the Runcorn School Board,especially now that the town is divided aud agreed upon its political policy It is easy to see from its composition that at pre- sent it is a Conservative body, and this is a fact which must not be forgotten in the near future. By a peculiar coincidence the expenditure of the &amp;wool Board has for the past three-half years been £3,000—.£1,000 a half year. But whether that is a too heavy or too light an expenditure is beside my present question. What I. am anxious to know is. how has the expenditure been met? Upon the rate- demand note I rind that 4411. in the is required for School Board Purposes, and as id in the pro- duces £2OO, a little calculation gives us the grand total ot .C9OO a half-year. Therefore,for three half- years in succession the Board has over-spen'L itself £lOO. Where has this £3OO come from?</p>
        <p>These are the sort of questions it is the duty of all Radicals to keep in tcuh with. I may not be right but it looks as if the School Board, are, (it they are using up the old surplus balance in place of current revenue), anticipating a fall at the next appeal to the polls, and exhibiting a little "slim- ness." In fact, it looks as if they have studied too much scripture and become enamoured of the parable of the "Unjust Steward."—" How much owest thou? £l,OOO —Well take thy bill and write £9oo."—Maybe their motive is diplomatic, and their only idea is that if a reverse does come, they will have the satisfaction of knowing they compelled the Radicals to raise the rate, they did not raise it themselves.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Cycle Club annual dinner reminds me of the fact that the erection of a Cottage Hos- pital in Runcorn, is not quite so hopeless as it once may have seemed. Roughly, but some .E4OO is required to put the Cottage Hospital Committee in a way to commence operations, for with .£4OO they will have about .Cl,ooo,and that was the figure they reckoned to commence the undertaking with. Mr. P. J. Norman says seriously, and surely he ought to know something about it, £1,030 ought to be raised in Runcorn in twenty-five minutes for so good a cause. With the object of getting the required amount up to standard by next Cycle Parade, might I suggest that a collecting box be put in each Church and Chapel doorway, and in every public-house willing to accept one, on the same principle as the collecting boxes the poor working- man is so familiar with at the office of hi g depot _ .</p>
        <p>It only afe oicl c)ppers were gained by the yen- would b.) so qething. In the meantime let the whole town think seriously over the matter,and put its whole soul into the next parade, and a deal of coppers into the collecting baxel.</p>
        <p>His Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands made his first appearance as a Judge in the Runcorn County Court on Thursday last, and by the smartness of his orders has /done perhaps more within a few minute to destroy the " tick " bystem than any judge we have had in the circuit for years. Orders tor is, 6d and 4id a week were common and when trades- people realise the length ,)f time that will have to lapse before their accounts come in, they, will be a little more chary about giving trust. Judge. Row- lands created a favourable impression on the whole, and whilst exhibiting a keen sense of wit, did not ; seem to lose sight of the sense of justice. For instance he intimated that in the cases of "travel- ling clothiers" he would require very clear evidence that ttie order had baect given with the husband's c3usent for he considered that in many eases" they knew nothing about it but were suddenly swooped 'down upon.'</p>
      </div>
