      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>A DOMESTIC SERVANT TARES LAUDANUM. At the Daresbury Petty Sessions on Tuesday a young woman named Ellen Britland. in the eervice of Miss Massey, of Crumley Heath Farm, Little Leigh, was charged with having on the 22nd inst attempted to com- mit suicide by taking poison.—Ellen Massey, danghter of Daniel Massey, of the farm named, said the prisoner was in her service as domestic servant. On Thursday, the 22nd November, she found the prisoner lying on the floor with her head resting on a bench. She was rolling . about and looked very il!, and was evidently in pain. She sPnt for a doctor,and Dr Smith of Weaverham came. He asked her what she had taken, and her reply was "Landannm." The police were also communicated with. A bottle containing laudanum was now produced which witness explained, was usually kept in a cupboard in the kitchen. The laudanum was used for some Purpose connected with one of the horses. The bottle was labeled " Laudanum—poison." The prisoner had been in her employ about five years.—ln answer to the Clerk the witness stated that about a fortnight ago one or two hares were missed and two cheeses. She mentioned the matter t-) the girl ,but did not accuse her of the thefts —Dr Smith said he saw, the prisoner at abont 730 on the evening in question. She was suffering from the effects of poisoning, and she admitted to him that the had taken laudanum. There was a large amount of laudanum in tha liquid shown to him. She told him that some hares had been missed and that" she was best away" He had known her for a long time. He stayed with her for an hour, and it was owing to his advice that the police were communicated with. The girl's mistrees had been exceedingly kind to her. On the night in question the girl was not safe to be at liberty.—The Banch were satisfied that prisoner had been guilty of IN very reprehensible act which might have had very serious consequences. But they con- sidered it had loom a warning to her.—The girl said she was very sorry for what had happened, and her parents promising to look after her she was dit:charged. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court. FRlDLY.—Before the Mavor(in the chair) Councillors William Plerpoint, Roberts, and Smethurst, Messrs Hepherd and Thomton. -- A VrordeNT Womier.—Sarah Massey. Bewsey-street, was summoned for Rs9aultinc! Tames Cliffe, 65, Buttermarket- street on the 19th inst. Thomas gassey, her husband, \veil stimmoned for threatnincr Cliffs—Mr Steel appeare I for the complainant and defendants were represented by Mr Connor. —Mr Steel stated that complainant had on several occasions complained of the misconduct of Mrs Massey. On one occasion when she was fighting with a woman named Richardson he had threatened to fetch tht police. Last Wednetida, week he bought a couple of truck-wheEils from Mr Massey. and finding on Monday.that they required some alight alteration, he went to Massey's yard but could not find Mr Massey. Ho shouted and Mrs Massey appeared with a bottle of beer in her hand, apparently In a drunken stuoor. She shouted " T have not forgotten you going for a copper when I pasted Richardson," and med a lot of filthy language. Twenty mimic.. afterwards Cliffs Was in the shop of a wheelwright named Vernon when Mrs Massey rushed in and struck him on the nose causing it to bleed. She then took her boot of? ned attempted to strike him again. Cliffs warded off the blow and Mrs Massey appeared to have acted like a madwoman and seized bold of a erowbare strike him. A witness prevented her from dealing a blow. Massey then came in and threatened to " knock Cliffe's neck out "and if he caughthim in the street he would only have to hit him once and he would "finish him "—The assault and threats were adneitted by the defendants, but it was alleged that when Cliffe went to the yard he assaulted Mrs l`cassoy.—Defendants were bound over to keep the peace aid ordered to pay the costs (15s</p>
        <p>each.) ALLE4ED COwinDLY AB3sULT.—Daniel Daniels. 15 Winwick-road, was summoned for assnulting John Meets. 53, Orford-lane, on the 4th inst. There was a cross-summons against Wrees for assault,— Mr. tongton, on behalf of Mees, stated that his client was returnirsg home on the night of the 4th inst and • met the defendant. Some eltereation ensued, add Daniels struck complainant a severe blow in the eye• knocking him to the ground --Defendant alleged that Mees poked him in the chest with the umbrella in such an aggra- vating manner thei, be struck him.—Dassiels was fined 5s and Costs, and the Buremon3 against Mees was dismissed. ASSAULT olf A WIFE —Thomas Butter. of 8, Begent-street. was charged with assaulting and beating his wife Mary Alice Butter on Monday evening.—The complainant stated that her husband came home clruak less usual" on the night named and struck her with the handle of the shovel on the face, giving her a black eye.—Defendant denied that he was drunk or that he struck his wife. He blamed his wife for not looking after the children properly. The Mayor said th,t defendant was trying to put all the blame on his wife. It was the defendant's duty (rite as mild) as the wife's to look after the children. The defendant was fined lOs and costs, -SATUnDIy.-11efore Mr T Birchen (in the chair), Alderman Heaton, Councillor Roberts. Messrs Thornton, Hayward, _ and B T Fairclough. FAMILY NEGLFCT.—Thornas Fairbrother w.s charged with running away from the boronels on the 13th of 3nne, leaving his wife and four children chargeable to the Warrington Union.—Mr B Shearer stated that the prisoner left the town about seven month. ago and the wife and children had been inmates of the workhouse since Jnne. The authorities had endeavoured to trace the man but hls whereabouts were not discovered until Friday when he was arrested at Middles- borough. Prisoner was a:wiredrawer and could easßy ea-n enough to keen his family if he chose to do so. The family had cost the Union over £3o.—Prt oner was committed to gaol for a month. Moamar —Before Mr. F Monks (in the'ehair) alderman Heaton. Coundller Smethurst, Messrs. Birdsall. Hayward,</p>
        <p>§-Wright and T Thornton. IMPU'DENT BEGGARS.—John Taylor was charged with begg- ing in Bridge-street at 6.35 on Saturday evening.—P.C. Clough said the prisoner went up to several gentlemen and asked them for money in a moat impudent manner. He was sent to gaol f'r seven days with hard Mont..— Thomas Rutter, who made his 19th appea-ance, was sentenced to 14 days' hard labour, for n similar offence, A WOMEN'S EQUABBLE —Jane Wilson. 653, linutsford- road ; Alice Percival. 18, Thelwall-lane ; Ada Dickson. 20, Thelwall-lane ; ring Sophia Maiaghan, 299 Th. lwall-lsne, were snmmonedlor committing a breach of the peace by scti.Ag in a disorderly manner in Knutsford-road on the 20th Inst.—Mr. W. H. J. 'Jenkins appeared on behalf of Wilson and Dickson, and sc.id that their conduct was perfectly inde- fensible. He expressed their deep regret for what had occurred, anda.ked the Bench to itecent the apology and order them to pay the enstL—The macistrat‘ a agreed to this oourse.—Mrs. Percival pleaded not guilty, and P.C. Newey gave evidence to the effect that she and the other women were fighting together in the street, and making use of most fearful language. They continued this disgraceful conduct until two o'clock the following morui, g.—Defeniant was bound over and ordered to pay the costs 'lss), ilJalaghan was fined Is Costs. CBSTRUCTION.—For allowing a handcart laden with fruit, to stand in SarikeT -street for the space )f twenty minutes. on the 19th inst. Edward Oxley, 262, Knutsford-road, was, on the evidence of P.S. Smith fined 10s. FIRING GUNS ON THE HIE/BATA:T.—Elijah Page and Arthur Sherratt were summoned for discharging guns on tho high- way at Arpley on the 18th inst.—P.C. Clough proved the case and the defendants were each fined 2s. 6d. NUISANCES—THE Ilt-sroxeinfrlTlES OF THE INSPECTOH— Thomas Morrell, 1-47. Lovely-lane, was summoned to show</p>
        <p>There Is Security ln A fra crops on :Al. toothbrush every morning ,IrEws 4 ,L • Or Small Pill Small Dose: SOZCooNT Will Sweeten the Breath all day. and make all the diderence St. iche, —iver. Indi- alma Teeth and Bad Teeth. gestion. Constipation, Dizziness. Vhita 'Teeth and Ye: low Teeth Furred Tongue. They Touch the Pretty Teeth and Ugly Teeth Liver. e Complete it. Toilet 74ph all W are Vag ar arrtai• • Icnala remiss, aso.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <p>cause why an order should not be made for him to abide a I nuiGanc, arising from the keeping of pigs and poultry—ln- spector Norman sail he visited the premises on the sth inst, and found them in a deplorable condition. The nuisance arose from the keeping of pigs and poulty, part of the land being a perfect swamp.—ln answer to the Chairman the Inspector stated that there were no special regulations as regarded the keeping of pigs in the borough— The Chairman was of the opinion that the Health Commit- tee should makes byelaw. Under the existing circumstances too much power was placed in the hands of the Inspector, as his opinion had to be the law on the matter.—The Inspector said that the Medical Officer had certified that the nuisance was injurious to health. He would bring Mr Monks's ex- pression of opinion before the committee.—Mr. Monks There ought to be a regulation so that people will know what they have to do.—An order was made for the defend- ant to abate the nuisance within seven days and pay the costs.—William PArkinson, Buttermarket-street was sum- moned to show cause why an order should not be made upon him to abate a nuisance in a stOle yard in Union- street, arising from the accumulation of manure.—The magietrates made an order, the defendant to pay the costs. UDSTAMPED SCA.LEs.—Samuel Jeff, Howley House. and Ann Littler. Castle Hill, Kingsley were each fined 5s for having unstemped scales in their possession for use of trade. —lnspector Place proved the glees. TUESDAY —Before Mr. T Birchen (in the chair). Aldermen Shaw Green and Hutchinson, and Dr. Fox. OBSCENE LANGUAGE.—Mary McGrath was fined 5s and costs for making use of obscene language in Buttermarket- street. at 9.10 the previous evening ARREARS Olt 111AisTEXANCE.—Thomas Warburtnn was charged on a warrant with arrears of maintenance due to his wife amounting with the costs to £2 12s. —Prisoner was ordered some time ago to pay his wife 10s per week, but he had given her nothing since the order was made.—He was committed to gaol for a month.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>TITESDAY.—Before Mr. j C Parr (in the chair). Colonel Ridgway, and Messrs. W Long. H Neild and Joseph _ .</p>
        <p>STEALING TIMDER.—Jobn Jeffs a respectably dressed man was charged with stealing a quantity of timber, of the value of 25., the property of the Ship Canal Company, at Moore. on the 29th October. —Mr. Preston prosecuted on behalf of the company, and explained that the timber was piled in a heap on an occupation road belonging to the company. The prisoner had a tarry outside the road and he climbed a fence and was seen to put some sleepers on his lurry. He was told to put them back and he di ' so. The company had quantities of material stored in different places. and it was, of course, impossible to watch it all. It was important that these robberies should be stopped.—Fined 5s and costs (21 is 6d in all). "A SERIOUS AND DANGEBoUS OBSTRUCTION."—WiISaaI Stevens was summoned for having on the 3rd October, at Preston-Ali-Hill, obstructed the free passage of the highway by allowing two traction engines, a van, &amp;c., to remain thereon an unreasonable time, namely. six honrs. without just cause,—P.C. Ridgway proved the case, and stated that the engines, &amp;c., remained on the road all night, and there was only a clear space of 51t. in the road for vehicles and pedestrians. The defendant informed him that he was go- ing to Mr. Tickle's, at the New Manor Farm and that he did not know the way there.—Joseph Spruce. district councillor for the parish of Preston-o'th'-Hill, gave evidence as to the obstruction. whicb, he said, he retarded as both serious and dangerous—The Chairman: You will be fined 20s and costs. What you did was extremely dangerous. ANollivß OBSTRUCTIoN.—Robert Martin was summoned for having onithe 23rd October, at Little Leigh. obstructed the free passage of the highway by leaving , a horse and cart on it from 1.30 p.m. to 2 p.m.—Sergeant Carter stated the facts of the case, and the defendant was ordered to pay the costs, 38 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>STORMING A POST-OFFICE.</head>
        <p>STORMING A POST-OFFICE.</p>
        <p>The postal authoriti s at Sheffield raised some objec- tion to the acceptance of hundreds of small parcels sent in bulk by Mr. J. G. Graves. the well-known manu- facturer of watches and jewellery. etc., of that town. The latter accordingly adopted the expedient of send- ing all his employees—same 500—to the post office in a body, each carrying a parcel. They poured into the office and overflowed into the main thoroughfare, where traffic speedily became impossible. The curious public helped to swell the throng, and the trameand 'buses had to be stopped.. .</p>
        <p>It was some tims before the 'pinta! clerks were able to overcome the extraordinary demand on their services, and the greatest inconvenience was caused to the public. This is another instance vf Post Office red-tape We understand that Mr. Graves pays the Sheffield Post Office :21,000 per month regularly in the despatching upwards of 250,000 parcels, letters, etc., for the same period, and yet, in spite of this the Post Office will not go out of their way to even lighten their own duties. Mr. Graves is to be congratulated on his action, and we trust it will be a lesson to the authorities which sitould react to the benefit of the public generally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>ST, HELENS FIRST TEAM.</head>
        <p>ST, HELENS FIRST TEAM.</p>
        <p>FIXTURES AND SCORES.</p>
