      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Wigan V. Barrow.</head>
        <p>Wigan V. Barrow.</p>
        <p>Wigan, though playing at home, sufferel another defeat—tlpAr rivals winning comfortably by two goals and one try to a goal—dropped by Woods. Bowker and Vickers each dropped a goal for Barrow, for whom dickie scor-4 the try. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>Oldham'v. Rochdale Hornets</head>
        <p>Oldham'v. Rochdale Hornets</p>
        <p>This gam was brought to an issue at Oldham. The play was fast, awl at times very keen. o:dharn, how- ever, won decisively by a goal and five tries to a goal and two tries.</p>
        <p>Millom V. Leigh.</p>
        <p>Play WAS very keen and always interesting, at 'Mil- Eion in tura held the upper hand, but Leigh eventually woo bY two gnal3 and a try to tSVJ tries.</p>
        <p>Widnes-v. Stockport.</p>
        <p>Tim meeting of these fifteens at Widnes furnished an iuteresting coute3t, the home selection being ultimately hailed the winnerq by a goal and a try to nil.</p>
        <p>Broughton Rangers v. Swinton.</p>
        <p>It was not until tho second portin of this encounter, at Broughtln, that Swinton scored a goal and two tries, and woo by these points to nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>YORKSHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Leeds v. Liversedge</p>
        <p>From first to last Lee Is ha I tho b tter of thi play in this cont-st upon their ground. A.P, the intermidiate stage they led by a dropped goal (Varley) and two tries (Coleman and Lumley). Varley subsequently placed a goal from a try by Jackson, Leids winning by two goals and three tries to nil, Leeds Parish Church v. Bradford.</p>
        <p>Fully 8,000 spectators witnessed a determined game between these fifteens at Leeds, which Bradford only won by two tries to a goal 9.a i a try, or Rix poin,s to five. Marsden and Cooper for Bradford, and Crampton and Wade for the Church were the scorers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>Manningham v. Huddersfield.</head>
        <p>Manningham v. Huddersfield.</p>
        <p>At Manninzham, Chambers dropp .d a ;val. an,lanrne smart paqsing in which Mar4den, D.whur3t, and Dire participated ended in the latter gainiae, a try for Hud- dersfield, who won by a goal mai a try to a try. Castleford v, Hull.</p>
        <p>Sharp first scored for the visitors at Hall, and Soped equalked. The Smirt got blhind, and his try gavo vic— tory to CAstleford h 9, six pllnt4 CI three. Halifax v. Batley.</p>
        <p>Esrly in this qarns Eitlifax, ths horns ein!ingent showed their superilri7, and tries wero snored bV N tieton. Jon os s.nd Xlneey, and Rigg landed a 13,n:tatty goal. Finally Haditax won by two vats and four tries t) 4 vat and t try.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Hunslet V. Wakefield.</head>
        <p>Hunslet V. Wakefield.</p>
        <p>The first half, at Hamlet, went in favour of the visi- tors who, Ind at the interval by a g )al and two tries &amp;lain in the second half they had toe best of it, aryl re- peating their previous score they won by sixteen p )iuts to nil. Hull Kingston Rovers v. Bromley.</p>
        <p>The Roverq, playing upon their own ground, suc- ceeded in scoring a win by thirteen points to eight,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>"A" TEAMS COMPETITION.</head>
        <p>"A" TEAMS COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Warrington Trounce St Helens.</p>
        <p>About 1,500 spectators witnessed the engagement tween these teams, which took place at Wildersoord on Saturday. Warrington kicked off, and play immed- iately settled in the visitors' half. Dribbling by the borne forwards resulted in Cunningham scoring near the posts, Ratcliffe imprGving. Varrington continued to press and bad passing spoiled numerous chances of augmenting the score Ratcliffe displayed capital forna,and succeeded in getting over, but the try WAS disallowed owing to a forward pass by Jewkes. Half time: Warrington, five points ; S. Helens, nil. Shortly alter :he rretart Jewkes scored for Warricgton, the goal kick failing. Warrington were allowed a free kick, and Ratcliffe landed a goal. Allen notched a third try, which was not converted. For the rest of he game Wgrrington had the best of matters, and scored again. Final : Warrington, 21 points; Sc. Helens, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>LEIGH "A " A. FLETCHER RIISSELL'S.—At Leigh on Saturday. Play was even at the outset, but eventually Towers rushed ~ver from a ~crimm age tor Leigh, and al.° placea a goal tar Fletcher's. Then from a penalty;Ashurat dropped a goal. Towers placed:another goal for Leigh, an I Clare szored a try for Fletcher's. Ashurst improving. At the interval the score was sever points each. In the second half Kelly scored from a scrimmage, Towers improving. In the last minute an unimproved try was scored for the visitors. Final : Leigh (A), three goals two tries (12 points) ; Fletcher Russell's two goals two tries (ten points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>OLD FALSI3I TIMM BOUGHT.</head>
        <p>OLD FALSI3I TIMM BOUGHT.</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington Trinity v. Chester College.</p>
        <p>Played at Warrin4ton on Saturday. The —College- kicked off, and at once made for the home goal, and Simpson opened the score,soon followed by :5. second and third, by Lawson. When halt-time arrived the visitors led by three goals to nil. Upton remming, the visitor:s rushed away, and scored a fourth goal. Towarda the finish, the Calege had the best of matters, and woo by four goals to nil.</p>
        <p>Peasley Cross v. Earlestown Rovers. Played at Peasley Cross before a moderate attendance of spectators. Peasley Cross kicked off and at once commenced to press, but the Rovers drove them back time after tinae. Fouls were given to each club, but neither could score. A foul was given to the Rovers, but 13rothertoo in goal was too good fn. them. Half- time—Peasley Cross 1, Earlestown Rovers 0. Final . EArlest)wa 3, PEasley Cross 2.</p>
        <p>Warrington St. Austin's v. Penketh Friends. This match was played at Warrington, on Saturday, before a fair Humber of spectators. The home centre commenced operations. Peaketti with the aid of a strong wind pressed rather strongly,but the home backs kicked well. floe homesters went a way on tlse left, and the visitors' custodian was forced to handle. Shortly afterwards Youde put the ball in the centre. One of the horns backs missed, and Sinclair scored the first goal for the visitors. Restarting, the home right wing got away and Bond made a clever run, until he was bowlad over by D Davies. A fine burst of passing among the home front rank was the next feature of interest, and the visi- tors' backs had an arxious time. A brilliant combination by the home forwards was spoilt by Rowntree, who payed his usual fine game. Half-time arrived with the visitors leading by one goal to nil. Resuming, the visi- tors were much indebted to Davies, who scored goal number two with a good shot. A moment after- wards Redfern scored a third,the home custodian having no chance. The visitors' forwards came again,and Rath- bone cleverly scored, whilst Nelson scored toe fifth, and Penketh friends won by flys gaals to nil.</p>
        <p>Earlestown v. Chorley. The visit of Chorley to the Mesnes had been etgerly looked forward to by the Earlestown supporters as past games between the two teams have always produced a fast and exciting ninety minutes with usually a close finish. Owing to the late arrival of the Chorley eleven, it was found impossible to play more than two thirty's and consequently the match will only be regarded as a friendly. This fact was not let out till after the match so that play did not suffer as a consequence. The previous week's display of the Earles- town train led to important changes being made, Pennington and Linton giving way to Lee and Anthony, a new recruit from Rotherham Town. Leach and Settle were traniderred to the right wing. Bartley took the centre forward position, and Lee crossed over to the extreme left whilst C:ibborn went right half and Wilitc- huuse lett half. From this it will be seen that the team was largely an experimental one.ang as E+rlestown won the experiment cannot, ny any means ba called 'a failure.</p>
        <p>There would be about 500 spectators present when Whittle started on behalf of Chorley and a sudden rush on the home left nearly brought about the nownfall of the visitor's goal in the first few minutes. Ostick missed his kick and Lee pounced on it Charlton having to concede a corner to clear his shot. This was correctly placed and an exciting melee in front of the visitors goal ensued. Lee met the ball from Settle and placed it into the net but the whistle had previously gone for offside. At half time there was no score. Soon after resuming, Lee met a beautiful centre from Bartley and easily defeated Charlton and as no more scoring was doae Earlestown retired victorious by one to nil. THE COMBINATION. - Warrington v. Birkenhead.</p>
        <p>This postponed match was played at Arpley on Satur- day the attendance being only meagre. Playing with the wind the Warringtoniana press3d, and Broster early on caused the Birkenhead custodian handle. Weak play in front of goal robbod the locals of many favourable openings,and in reply the Cheshirernen by excellent combination got to within shooting range of the home goal, Heaton netting with a capita! shot. Toe Warringtonians improved after this reverse. and Postlethwaite and Murray were instrumental in main- taining a prolonged attack. An attempt to clear was checked by Cook and Dilworth, and Warrington again attacking, Broster put on the finishing touch to some smart passing twice in quick succession. The Birken- head goal keeper got rid of dangerous shots, but dis- appointment was experienced when Murray failed to place a goal from a penalty, the interval arriving with the score a goal each. Early in the second half th visitors were awarded a penalty which Smith successe fully negotiated, and this proved to be the winning point, Birkenhead thus gaining the victory by three goals to one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Hudson's v Newton-le-Willows.</head>
        <p>Hudson's v Newton-le-Willows.</p>
        <p>Hudson's won the toss, and kicking with the wind forced a corner. Marr &amp;poilt tneir chances by shooting wide. Play remained in the visitors' half, Brook andA Morgan trying their luck without success. Not to bit denied, the hotnesters pressed aud Akin netted the ba after twenty minutes play. Howard soon afte re 4istered a second. Midfield play was indulged in for a spell. Half-time--Eludson's 2, Newton 0. Final : Hudson's 3, Newton, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AND DISTRICT LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Results up to November 24th 1900. Goals.</p>
        <p>lsr DIVISION.</p>
        <p>Club.</p>
        <p>P. W. L. li. F.. A. P. Trinity A.thletic 6 . 3 0 . 2 ... 7 ... 4 ... 8 St. tleiens L a N.W. Recs. 7 ... 3 ... 2 ... 2 ... 8 .... 5 ... 8 Orford Barracks St. Mary's 4 ... 1 ... 1 ... 2 ... 4 ... 4 ... 4 Peuketh Newton Res. floats.</p>
        <p>Club.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <p>I_lOW TO GBT A BILLIARD TABLB FOR 18s 6d GEO. G. BUSSEY &amp; CO., of 36 and 38 Queen Victoria-street, and 213 Regent-street, London, make an ingenious set of arrangements whereby a good game of Billiards can be played on an ordinary dining table. Ths fittings are easily fixed, and do no damage to the table. The price includes everything required to play the game. Ask the lo2al Sports Dealer for descriptive pamphlet and Indoor Games List. Advt (3).</p>
        <p>ar.* Secretaries of Footb%ll Clubs are requested to note the reports of matches played on Saturday must be sent to the Head Office, Bewsey-street, Warrington, flat late than 10 o'clock on Thursday morning or they cannot be Inserted</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION-</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION-</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SENIOR COMPETITION.</p>
        <p>Warrington Win at St. Helens.</p>
        <p>The return match between Warrington and S. Helens was played at St. Helens on Saturday, in very cold, foggy weattET. Only about 1,500 spectators braved the blast and climbed to the top of Boundary-road, and even they were not repaid by a very brilliant game. St. Helens could only place a crippleu team in the field, Traynor, Barnes, and Liversage all being absentees through injuries. They had the services at centre of a player named Pearson, a member of the teaching pro- t,ssion, and an ea Yorks County Rugby Union player. Warrington had their best team in the field, though the injury to Morrison early on nullified that advantage. Mr. Priestley, of Broughton, was referee,and the players lined out in the following order _</p>
        <p>Warrington : Hallam, full back; Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson, and Hockenhall, three-quarters ; Burgess and Duckworth, ha:f backs ; Taylor, Morrison, Board- man, Mereweather, Scholtze, Eden, Swift, and Andrews, forwards- _ _ _</p>
        <p>St. Helens : Foulkes, full back: Doherty, Unsworth, Pearson, and Peacock, three-quarter 4 ; Pennington and Boyle, half backs; Dale, Briers, Thompson, Mooney, Melvin, Caveney, Prescott, and Green, forwards. St. Helens won the toss and took advantage of the wind in be first half. Taylor set the ball in motion,the Saints immediately returning and setting up a hot attack. Warrington defended resolutely, Hallam and Fish kicking effectually and clearing the line. St. Helens came back again with some daeb, Dickenson checking their progress with a cool mark off Pennington. Warrington held tneir own well in the pack and smarc combination between Burgess, Dickenson and Hocken- hall carried play into the home half. The ball was quickly sent across to the other wing, Fish having no chance however, and getting knocked, in touch. A fine follow up by the Warrington front rank resulted in Peacock being upset and Morrison hurt in a collision. The Warrington forward's collar- bone was seriously hurt and he took no further part in the game. Very pluckily the remaining sevenstuck to their guns aud St Helens obtained little advantage from Morrison's absence. Isherwood and Dickenson got away nicely, but the latter neglected Hockenhall. Foutkes re- lieved the pressure by touching down, not before Fish had a fine opening veiled be a loose .pass from Dicken- son. The Saints, headed by Briers made a rush which took them to the Warrington line, where scrimmage after scrimmage was fought out. The tackling was of the dealliest character, Boardman doing excellent ser- vice in this respect. Pearson had an abertive shot at goal and Pennington made several strong dashes for the line. Doherty attempted once to pass Fish, but only got a severe shake-up for his pains. Briers made a fine dribble through the Warrington backs but Hallam picked up beautifully and punted clear. Just before the interval Boyle threw to Unsworth near the line, and the centre had the easiest of chances when he chose to pass to Peacock, who knocked on badly. Warrington cleated the line, and at half-time there was no seo.e. Warrington soon made their intentions manifest in the second half and St Helens were forced back. Burgess and Duck worth wcrked hard but their tactics were not such as lead to scoring. It was left to the three-quarters themselves to make the openings. Isherwood dashed in when St Helens were trying to pass, and snapping the ball between Pearsoe and Peacock was off Ii e a shot. Dickenson was in attendanco and Foulkes had no chance whatever with the two centres. Isherwood passed at the right moment and his partner romped in ' behind the posts. Fish easily added a goal. St Helens made no headway at all, and a penalty being given against them Fish kicked another goal. Briers was prominent with good defensive play, but Warrington were easily the stronger team with the wind behind them, Hallam repelling every attempt to change the scene of operations. Fish picked ua smartly am' dodged Doherty, but when appearing almost over a for- ward got bold of him and brought him down. Just before the close he had another shot at goal and a nicely judged kick failed throueh the ball striking the upright. Time was called with Warrington winning by 2 goals 1 try (7 points), te St Helens nil.</p>
        <p>Salford v. Runcorn. Tight Game. rhe first meeting this season of these clubs tonk place at New Barns. So far as the S:inior Competition has advanced there has been a marked contrast between the teams in their endeavours to scramble up the League ladder. The Chzshire men, who are making a bold bid for supremacy, have only been beaten twice, by Swinton and somewhat unexpectedly by Widnes, and have defeated, amongst others, Broughton Rangers and Old- ham, while the Salford contingent has not only succumbed to the two last-named but to four others. Of course, curiosities of form will happen, and amongst tho victims of the "Reds" is Widnes, the others being Barrow and Millom. The Lancashire club had every- thing to gain in the encounter, and naturally whipped up their strongest side, the tall score of the visitors against Wigan being a broad hint that not only the attack but the defence would requ;re atteation. During the week Salford hal secured the transfer from Aspatria of John Varty, the crack Cumberland county centre, who figured in the team, and a further change had been made in the half backs, while the visitors' team also underwent alteration in the three-quarters line, the Anal selection beine as follows : Salford : Smith, back ; Bone, Tom Williams, John Varty, and Hadwen, three-quarter backs ; Grey and Griffiths, halt backs ; J Williams, Rhapps, Tunny, Buckley, Shaw, Heath, Worthington. and Fisher. for- wards.</p>
        <p>Runcorn : Houghton, back ; Jones. Richardson, Wilkinson. and C Butterworth, throe-quarter backs ; Jolley and Faulkner, halt backs; Walker, Farmer, Light. foot, Reece, Darlington, Tomlinson, Messenger. and Dix Jo, forwards, Referee : Mr. M'Culcheon, Oldham.</p>
        <p>Before a rather poor gate Mr. J G Groves. M.P. for Sltrth Salford. started the game frt Salford, with Mr.</p>
        <p>Lees-Knowles, M.P., at his right hand. The game was promptly carried to the Salford end, but relief was effected, and a mark by Jones removed play to the centre, where Griffiths came away to Houghton, who, failing to gather, let the home team in. The same player retrieved the loss of ground till by good passing Bone got off only to fall a victim to Butterworth. Salford CD1.1.. tinued to press, and Varty was nearly captured by Farmer, but a free-kick to Farmer and a mark by Faulk- ner carried hostilities to the visitors end. Tunny got possession, and made a fine run, supplemented be a dribble to Houghton, who, failicg to return, saw a fine attack by the home backs, Varty being prominent. Houghton defended with a mark, but the advantage I lay with Salford, till Jolley from touch r !Hayed to the home half. Again Salford came away, and Houghton was in difficulties, the outcome being a scrummage, and passing by Williams' Varty and Had wen ending in a try by the last-named, Griffiths failing at goal. Following the re-start. Run- corn passed, and Jones was very prominent, the home quarter being reached, where a stoppage en- sued,through temporary injury to Faulkner. Then Had- wen and Griffiths punted to the half-flag, where Faulk- ner from the pack again gave his men an opening, Richardson improving the position by a long kick. Kick- ing exchanges followed,and the home forwards dribbled to the visitors' line, where gloughton put in a good de- fensive kick, and although he momentarily cleared, the home forwards dribbled down, Jolley defending well. The Salford men could not, however, be ousted from their position, and a series of scrummages followed in the visitors' quarter. Securing from one of these, Had- wen and Grey attempted to gain ground, but the Run- corn defence was dodged, and Farmer dribbled to the half way, where the ball went into touch. Again going Williams made a fine effort, but unfortunately slipped after a brilliant run and when on the point of feeding Bone. The visitors gained a little after this, but Griffiths, Grey and Had- wen forced Houghton to make a mark. Tunny got the ball, and winding through, passed to Williams, and he to Bone, the latter eluding Butterworth and Houghton, and scoring at the corner. The difficult shot was not improved by Griffiths. From the drop out the visitors went off with a rush, but the passing of the home backs again came to the rescue, and after a fine bout Bone bad the ill-luck to lose the ball on the line, Houghton being hurt in the tackle and having to leave the ground Jolley going to full back, and Richardson half. Matters remained pretty even till half-time, Houghton returning as the whistle blew. Bone made one more effort from a pass by Williams, but to no avail. Half-time :—Sal- ford, two tries (6 points) ; Runcorn 0. Fermer put the ball in motion on restarting, Smith effecting a splendid return, and further kicks by Runcorn being charged down. Salford found themselves under the Runcorn goal where Hadwen secured and passed to Varty, who was brought down on the line. From the following pack the ball travelled to Williams, who dashed over on the other side, grounding under the posts, and Hadwen landed a fine goal. Caming down to the Salford end, Jolley was entrusted with a mark and landed a goal. Resuming, Walker and Lightfoot dribbled to the line, where Varty made a daring save after Smith had failed. Shortly after Richardson received a, pass and scored in a good position, but Jolley failed a: goal. The centre now became the scene of operations, with a slight advantage to the home team, but Grey was too well looked after, and Runcorn crossed half-way,where Jones made an effort but failed. A mark by Jolley left things as they were for a time, till Faulkner got an opening, but kicked too hard the ball going dead. Run- corn were at this stage going strongly. Jones. Richard- son, and Faulkner pressing, and Vasty and Griffiths defending with marks, the last-named having eventually to kick dead. A mark by Richard- son again gave Runcorn a footing in the home half, and a further mark by Jolley nearly lowered the home goal Salford roused themselves after this spell of defence, but Walker got nicely going, but was collared in time. Jolley, however, gathered from the scrummage and scored, Dan Smith being hurt in tackline. Tne Runcorn man added a goal. Three-quarter time : Salford, 11 points ; Runcorn 10 points. Things were now in a state of great excitement as the last phase of the metch was entered upon, and the players were re- peatedly encouraged by their supporters. A long spell of kicking was against Salford, Tunny trying to break away, but the ball rolled in touch, and Salford were forced hick. A rush of their forwards, however, cleared the danger, and heavy work followed at centre, Sadwen dribbling to the Runcorn line. The long exchanges of the players were a treat to witness, and lasted several minutes. Tunay eventually inking the ball dead Result :—Salford, one goal, three tries, eleven points ;. Runcorn, two goals, two trite., ten points.</p>
      </div>
