        <head>N.-W. Recs.</head>
        <p>N.-W. Recs.</p>
        <p>At H3cvlay, on Saturday. The Saints opened the attack,- Hastrey, Flerniug, and Meachin having hard luck. The Rocs broke away, but Fleming and Robinson got play back in the S. Helens quarter. At the interval there was no score. On the rt-start the visitors pressed, but Hill and Hannigan cleared. Meachin and Robinson made some fine runs, but could not score. Final : St. Mary's, nil; S. Helens, nil.</p>
        <p>Sudden Death of a Colliery Proprietor :near Warrington. The County Coroner ;Mr. Brighouse) has been notified of the death of Mr. William Turner, of Daisy Bank, Occupation-road, Irlam (a colliery proprietor, and owner of the Wigan Junction Col- lieries), which took place in a first-class carriage on the C.L.C. Railway between Manchester and Glaze- brook on Saturday night. It appears deceased had suffered from an affection of the heart and had been attended by Or. Jays, of Cadishead for this com- plaint, up to about two years ago, when he was advised that he must oo no account nurry himself. Deceased attended to his business on Saturday at Wigan and returned home, where he had tea with his wife and family, and was then in his usual health. He left home to go by the 720 p.m. train from Irlam to Manchester. I.le was seen on the platform at the Central Station, Manchester at 9.25 and was hurrying Co catch the 9.30 p.m. This train turns back at Glazebrook, and the guard went to the carriage door to inform the deceased of this, and was horrified to find that he had expired. The body was removed into a first-class waiting-room. The Coroner has informed the police that he will not hold an inquest.</p>
