      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <p>8</p>
        <p>c,</p>
        <p>The SUBSCRIPTION LIST will OPEN OR MONDAY NEXT, December 3rd, and will CLOSE on. WEDNESDAY, December sth.</p>
        <p>h •</p>
        <p>TH7, SOUTH LANCASHIRE ELECTRIC TRACTION AND POWER COMPANY, LIMITED.</p>
        <p>INCORPOR IT-ED UN)), R THE COMPANIES' ACTS. SHARE CAPITAL DIVIDED INTn</p>
        <p>600,000 Six Per Cent. Cumulative:. Preference Shares of £1 each £600.010 250.000 Ordinary Shares of £1 each £250.000 In addition to the ahoy. Share Capital the Company is inning £750,000 Four and a Half Per Cent. First Mortgage Debenture Stock,</p>
        <p>The following Stock and Shares a-e now offered for Subscript Ton at par :- 2750.000 4 Per Cent First Mortgaze Debenture Stock. £400,000 6 Per Cent. Cumulative Preference Shares. PAYABLE AS FOLLOWS Debenture Stock. Shares. On Application On Ailotment lO per cent 2s 0.1 And the balance in Calls of not more than 10 per cent. each, and at intervals of not less than two months. Redemption Policies have been effected with the Norwich Union Life Insurance Society. the Sun Life 0 ffloe and the Alliance Fire and Life Insurance Company, whereby the return of the whole of the £750.000 First Mortioge Debenture Stock, on the date hereafter named, is provided for. Under these Policies only a small Premium will be payable for the first four years. The Debenture Stock, and the interest thereon, will be secured by a First Mortgage to the Trustees for the Debenture Stockholclers of the whole of the Company's Assets. The Stock will be allotted in sums of not less than £5O. and in multiples of £lO above that amount, and will be transfer- able in any some not involving a fraction of a pound. It will he redeemable at par on the 31st December. 1950, or at the option of the Company at any time after the Ist January, 1920. at the price of £lO5 per £lOO Stock on three calendar months' notice, and in the event of a winding up it is also redeemable at a premium of £5 per cent.</p>
        <p>Interest on the stock will be paid half -yearly. on the let January and the lit July, the first payment, calculated from the dates flied fer payment of the several instalments, being made on the let July, 1901.</p>
        <p>The Preference Shares rank, both a 3 to Dividend and Capital, in priority to the Orainary Shares. but will not share In surplus assets in the event of a winding up. The Dividend thereon will be payable on the Ist April and the let October in each year.</p>
        <p>Hold t Preference Shares will have as full power of voting at General Meetings as holders of Ordinary Shares. The balance of the Preference Shares will only be issued to meet capital outlay on any further extensions, for which powers may be hereafter secured. Allotments of Stock and Shares may be paid up in full on allotment under discount at the rate of three per cent. per annum.</p>
        <p>TRUSTEES FOR DEBENTURE STOCK HOLDERS. Sir William B. Forwood, Bromborough-hall, Cheshire. Joseph Beecham, LP., St. Helens, Lancashire.</p>
        <p>DIRECTORS.</p>
        <p>Sir John A. Willox, M,P., Huyton (Chairman of the new St. Helens and District Tramways Co.. Ltd.) HOl2. Arthur Stanley. M.P., Knowsley, Prescot. Lanc‘shire. B. K. Muspratt, JP.. Seaforth-hall. near Liverpool. James B. Atherton, Manhattan, Gatacre, Liverpool (Director and General Manager of the British Insulated Wire Com- pany, Limited) Jacob Atherton, Hurst-par k, Prescct. LE nealhire (Chairman of the E.outh-Eastern Metropolitan Tramways Company).</p>
        <p>BAIIKBRS. Parr's Bank, Limited. Bartholomew-lane, London, EX.; Liverpool ; and other Branch .Is.</p>
        <p>SOLICITORS. F. L Leslie and Co., 15, Union-court, Liverpool, and 15, George-street, Mansion-house, London, H.C.</p>
        <p>AUDITORS. Cisainiers, Wade and Co., 5, Fenwiek-street, Liverpool, and 10, Coleman-street, London, WC</p>
        <p>BROKERS. London—Fielding, Son and Macleod, 2, Copthall-buildings, B 0. Liverpool—Farr and Rae, Queen Insurance-buildings.</p>
        <p>CONSULTING ENGINEERS. Kincaid, Waller end Manville, 29, Great George-street, Westminster, S.W.</p>
        <p>ENGINEBE-IN-CHIBF. Alfred H. Gibbing'', M. Inst. E.E.</p>
        <p>SECRETARY AND OFFICES (PRO TEM). Alfred Tracey, 41, Castle-street, Liverpool.</p>
        <p>kROSPECTUS.</p>
        <p>This Company is formed to acquire the beneficial interest In. and to develop as parts of a single system, the concessions; and rights now owned or to be obtained by the South Lancashire Tramways Company (Incorporated by Act of Parlia- ment) and the Lancashire Light Railways Company, Limited. The undertakings of these Companies are most intimately related with each other, and for the common advantage should be controlled and worked together. Owing, however, to the statutory and other obligations, the most feasible way to effect this union of interests is by bringing together the whole Into the one common ownership of this Company, which has accordingly been formed for that purpose.</p>
        <p>This Company therefore accinires, and will bold, by itself or its nominees, all the issued Shares in these Companies. whilh will for the future be controlled exclusively by this Company. By this anangement this Company will, for all practical purposes, stand in the same position as if the various statutory and other powers had been originally granted to it. No additional Shares in any of the Companies will be issued except to this Ccmpany or its nominees.</p>
        <p>rinterest will be paid by the Company on the Debenture Stock now issued during constructian of the lines'. Thus sub- scribers for Stock will receive a regular income from their investment.</p>
        <p>SOITTFI LANCASHIRE TRAMWAYS.</p>
        <p>The South Lawashirs Tramways Company was formed by ths South Lancashire Tramways Act. 1900. which authorise the construction of Viectrie Tramways, between St. Helens and the Manchester District, by two main trunk Lines, on from Haydock, passing through Ashton-in-Makerfleld, Abram, Hindley, Westhoughton. Little Hutton, and Waikden. to Swinton, where it meets the Tramways leased to the Salford Corroration ; and the other from St. Helens through Hades- town. Newton in-Makerfield, Lowton, Leigh, Atherton, Tyldesley, Bolthstown, and Worsley. to the boundary of the Borough of Eccles, where it also meets the tramways leased to the Corporation of Salford. Branch Lines also connect Lowton with Golborne and Abram, Hlndley with Atherton and lyldesley. Worsley with Swinton, and the Borough of Bolton with both Westhoughtou and Atherton.</p>
        <p>The authorised Lines are indicated in red in the Map accompanying the Prospectus. and include 84i miles of single track, a longer distance than ever before authorised by a Tramway Act. They will traverse or connect some of the most Important centres in the busiest commercial manufacturing districts of Lancashire, with a population of several millions.</p>
        <p>Along molt of the authorised routa there is an almost continuous line of houses, with many large factories, and other industrial works, and at present there are hardly any tacilitiea for intercommunication. From some ,if the towns the nearest railway station is nearly a mile away, and for boil traffic there are very few trains in the day. As stated by the Mayor of Leigh in his evidence in support of the Company's Bill, the question of intereommuni-ation between these districts has been a pressing one for years, and this Company's proposals are welcomed as cffering a practical solution of a great public want.</p>
        <p>The At was passed with the approval of the Lancashire County Council and the nineteen Local Authoriti.s whose Dig Wets are affected, and with the active support of nearly all of them. The powers it confers are perpetual, subject to rights of purchase by the Lrcal Authorities. These rights are exceptionally favourable to the Company. being exercisable only after 35 years, or, in some cases, by agreement with certain Local Authorities. in 30 years. and leaving the Company with concurrent running powers for 21 years longer, thus giving them the right of using the lines for a minimum period of 51 years. _</p>
        <p>The laying and equipment of the Lines will be carried out on competitive tenders. invited from the best firms, end wit be commenced at once. mad the intention is that a considerable part of them may be ready for working by the end of 1931 Powers will be sought next Session for the extension shown on the Map, which will, if obtained, be valuable adjuncts to the system.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>LIVERPOOL AND PRESCOT LIGHT RAILWAY.</head>
        <p>THE LIVERPOOL AND PRESCOT LIGHT RAILWAY.</p>
        <p>This Light Railway Order was granted In 1898 to the Lancashire Light Railway Company. Limited, till whole of whose Shares will be held by this Company. The Line, nearly 31 miles in length, will commence at the end of the St. Helens Tramway system at Prescot, and terminate at the City boundary of Liverpool at Knotty Ash, to which point it is expected that the Liverpool Corporation's Blectric Iramways will shortly be completed. The application had the support of all the Local Authorities of the Districts through which it passes. but Its special importance lies in the fact that it must form the connecting link between the two Tramway systems of Liverpool and St. Helens, and through the latter with the South Lancashire systems to Manchester and district. Its route is indicatel in green on the Map. Although granted under the Light Railway Act, it Is for all practical pur- poses a Tr amway. and its construction will be the same as that of the South Lancashire Tramways.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>ST. HELENS TRAMWAYS.</head>
        <p>THE ST. HELENS TRAMWAYS.</p>
        <p>Thee e Tramways. coloured yellow on the Map. which are in lease to the new St. Helens and District Tramways Com- pany. L imited, lie between the Liverpool and Prescot Light Railway and the South Lancashire Tramways. and connect the two. A continuous ionte will thus be available from the Tramway System of Liverpool to that of Manchester, and to the whole of the places served by and in connection with the Fouth Lancashire Tramway Company's system. The nt. Helens Company has undertaken to grant facilities for an interchange of traffic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>GOODS TRAFFIC.</head>
        <p>GOODS TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>Byer, class of goods can be carried on the Tramways and Light Railway. and it is intended to establisb this business on comprehensive Beale. A. glance at the Map will show the immense possibilities of a thorough system of gools carriao and distribution by these lines</p>
        <p>ELECTRIC LIGHT AND POWER SUPPLY,</p>
        <p>The South Laneashire Tramways Company Is empowered by its act to supply electric energy to local authorities for all purposes, and arrangements will be made accordingly. Such a supply cm be given economically in conjunction with the Tramway Plant, thus securing a regular load on the stations for traction, light, and power, which will enable them to be worked on the most economical basis.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>COMBINED WORKING OF THE UNDERTAKINGS AND ESTIMATE OF PROFITS.</head>
        <p>COMBINED WORKING OF THE UNDERTAKINGS AND ESTIMATE OF PROFITS.</p>
        <p>By this combination of interests a complete system of E'ectrie Traction In South Lancashire will be rendered possible. with cars running at frequent intervals between Liverpool and Manchester, through Present, St. Helens, Newton, Hades- town, Leigh, and Eccles, and with branch connections from the Man Line to Wigan. Bolton, and other important centres. Messrs. Kincaid, Waller and Manville, the well known Tramwey Engineers and Experts, have given tbe following esti- mate of profits :</p>
        <p>29, Great George-street. Westminster. 29th November, 1900.</p>
        <p>To the Directors of the South Lancashire Electric Traction and Power Company. Limited. Gentlemen.—We have personally surveyed the whole of the 'Tramway Routes authorised by the South Lan- cashire Tramways Act, and the Liverpool and Preecot Ligir, Railway Orden From our intimate knowledee of the working of the other Electric Tramways, we estimate that some 5,000.000 car miles wilt be run annually by your tramcars, and that your gross receipts should amount to 9d per car mile, or a total of £187,5C0. The total outgoings. making full allowance for maintenance of lines, plant. ac,. on a liberal basis, and all expenses of management, we estimate at 55 per cent. of the gross receipts, thus leaving a net profit of £4,375 per annum.— Tours faithfully,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>ELECTRIC TRAMWAYS IN SOUTH LANCASHIRE.</head>
        <p>ELECTRIC TRAMWAYS IN SOUTH LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>aggregate length of these lines is altogether 107 miles, and the importance of the system will be realised when it is stated that it estatiishes unbroken tramway com- municatiban between the cities of Liverpool and Man- chester.</p>
        <p>All the principal towns of South Lancashire are touched en route. By one line the towns of Frrscot, St. Helens, garlt&lt;stown, Leigh, Tyldesley, and Eccles are connected; while by another circuit Ashton-in-Maker- field, Ince, Abram, Hindley, West boughton, and Bolton are joined together. From the latter centre, Bolton, there is a further project to be submitted' to Parliament in the ensuing session for extending the system north- wards to Darwen and thence to Blackburn ; while on the southern side of the county there is a contemplatel extension to Warrington by means of a line branching off from Earlestown. If these several extensions are sanctioned by the Legislature, they will add another 24 miles to the system already autherised,,and establish a continuuus network of electric tramways to a total length of over 133 miles. The population of</p>
        <p>the district numbers several millions, so that there is an assurance of an immense passenger traffic between the several districts. As everyone knows, South Lancashire also covered with great numbers of mills, factories, and collieries, and as the Act of Parliament authories the carrying of goods traffic and the supply of electrical power, these twu departments open up opportunities of great public usefulness. The rails will be constructed of the i3ost substantial materials suitable for light and heavy traffic, and the central generating station at Wel will be favourably situated for getting cheap fuel at the coalpit month. The resident engineer will be Mr. Alfred H. Gibbings, who designed and laid down the splendid electrical tramways of the Corporation of Brad- ford.</p>
        <p>This important scheme for developing the trade of South Lancashire is now about to assume tangible shape, an I arranl4ernents ara being made to commence immedi- ately the construction of the works. A company has been formed to take over the privileges conferred by the several acts for tramways and light rail ways, and to</p>
        <p>combine the whole into one undertaking, with concen- trate(' management and unified administration. It will be known as the South Lancashire Electric Trac- tion and Power Cmpany, Limited. The capital will be over a million and a half ster:ing, namely £850,000 in shares, and £750,000 in mortgage debenture stock. The promoters are sanguine that the project will prove a lucrative and successful investment, but apart from this prospect the public will no doubt regard the scheme as likcly to be of 'great convenience in affording cheap and rapid locomotion for passengers, and in facilitating goods traffic for the industrial interests. Electric tram- ways have a marvellous effect in developing local travel- ling, as illustrated by the experience of Liverpool, Blackpool, St Helens, and other places where the system has been introduced. There is every probability that similar results will follow in the teeming population of South Lancashire, and that, whatever the results to the investors, the advantage to the public will be great.</p>
