      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>BEER POISONING EPIDEMIC.</head>
        <p>THE BEER POISONING EPIDEMIC.</p>
        <p>Cases at Burtonwoo&amp;</p>
        <p>Numerous rumours have been circulated in War- rington during the past week that a serious enidemic of disease due to beer drinking existed at Burtonwood. On Thursday, a representative of the " Examiner " visited the village and found there was what may be called a mild scare amongst the villagers. From inquiries made, however, the epidemic was not of such a serious nature as the rumours had led one to expect. Undoubtedly there are a number of cases of sickness amongst adults and the nature of EOCII3 of the cases corresponds with those of peripheral neuritis which have been so numerous in Manchester and Salford and which have been attributed to arsenical beer poisoning. One patient has been removed to the Warrington Infirmary for treatment. A number of the employos at the Collins Green Colliery have been affected as have also a number of farm labourers in the neighbourhood. The effect upon the drinking habits of the people has been instauto.- neous. The demand for beer has decreased con- siderably in the neighbourhood and beer-seller.: are suffering seriously in their business in conse- quence.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>VENO'S GUARANTEE.</head>
        <p>VENO'S GUARANTEE.</p>
        <p>A CURE OR NO PAY. VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC IS THE ONES MEDICINE ON EARN' BACKED wird BANKABLE PAPER, BECAUSE IT IS THE WORLD'S OREA.TET REMEDY FOR STOMACH, LIVER, KIDNEY AND BLOOD, DISEASES. AND NEVER DIS- APPOINTS.</p>
        <p>AN ABSOLUTE GUARANCEE TO THOSE WHO PURCHASE IT.</p>
        <p>If you are suffering from any ailment arising from a diseased condition ditties stomach, liver, kidneys and blood, \ratio's Seaweed Tonic is guaranteed to cure it. With every 2s 9d bottle will be found a bank cheque f.-)t 2s 61 payable through the London Trading Bank, Ltd., which will be promptly paid if V,no's Seaweel Tonic fails to cure. It is the only remedy backed with bank- able paper, because it never fails. Its effect is marvel- lous. The cures surprise everybody. If you tuy a2s 91 bottle, and it fails to cure, you cannot lose your money. The checoe is as good as gold. You will be paid with- out any fuss or bother. The remedy is as good as man can mike ic. The proprietors take this method of guaranteeine it. Try it for indigestion, wind on the stomach, constipation, biliousness, headache, weak and painful back, arrenia, general weakness, palpitation, impure blood, and sleeplessness. - VENCVS LIGHTNING COUGH CURE</p>
        <p>stops an ordinary cough in one night, and cures chronic coughs, bronchitis, asthma, and influenza. A splendid rernorly for children's coughs You will find this a scientifioremedy, far superior to ordinary cough mixtures or any of the emulsions. It contains a pre- paration discovered by Dr. Gibbons, in California, and which was fr.und to be an absolutA cure for bronchitis, asthma, and infiu.nza. You will never be satisfied until you have tried 'his r,ltn^dy. AN IMPORTANT LETTER FROM MESSR9. WALLER AND RILEY, BGIANUFA.O rutuNG cumusrs, BOLTON.</p>
        <p>BOLTON, NOVEMBER 19TH 1900. The Deno Drug Go., Dear Sirs,—The sale of your remedies in all our shnpq, has, we believe, seldom been equalled by any pro prietory article, and certainly never been exceeded even by the extensively advertised patents which are owned and advertised by the large syndicates. They must have in.rinsie merit, as they are highly spoken of by purchasers who regularly repeat their orders for them. We think your poster very striking, and difficult to pass without arrrsting attention. Your faithfully. WALLER AND RILEY.</p>
        <p>c&amp;urioN:—When you ash for either of Veno's remedies, Bee you get it. Avoid the mm n who tries to palm off another medicine. DENO'S SEAWEED TONIC &amp; VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE are sold at is. lid. and 23. 91. per bottle. Sold bv BOoTS, LIMITED, Warrinc:ton ; PEABE, town, and N-wton ; WESTON AND CO., RtVICOM ; WALL - BRIDGE, St. He!ens, and all Chemists.</p>
        <p>A Curious Wager-7,000 'Miles with a Perambulator.</p>
        <p>A young Austrian, named Anton Hanslian, who in order to win a wager of 2,000 dollars from the proprietors of a New York newspaper, has arranged to push a perambulator containing his wife and child a distance of 11,000 kilometres—which is roughly, 7,000 miles—in 250 days, was in Warring- ton, or. Tuesday evening. He put up at the Queen's. Hotel, Sankev-3treet, and left early on Wednesday morning for Liverpool, whence be will sail for New York, and from there walk to an Francisco, his destination. by way of Chicago. Hanslian started on his curious journey from Vienna on September 12th, and during the tour has already worn out two pairs of strong boots, two suits of clothes, and several hats Hi 9 arrival was witnessed by a large number of people.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>A CORRECTION. In our issue of last week, in connection with a case of the sudden death of an infant named Green, it was stated that Dr. Watkins was called to the child, but before he arrivea it was dead. This was an error, the ;medical man called to the child was Dr. Latham.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>P.S.A.</head>
        <p>P.S.A.</p>
        <p>The usual weekly meeting of the Eulastown and Dirrict P.S.A. Society,was held in the Co-operative Hall, on Sunday afternoon. There was a good attendance presided over by Mr, A Morris. The principal speaker was Mr S Harvey,and vocal items were given by Miss L Jones,whilst Mr J Astall con- tributed recitations. Miss Harvey presided at the piano. PoucH CASE. _ _</p>
        <p>At the Esrlestown Police station on Monday morning, before Mr J W Ninmett. Thomas Sergeant and William Liptrott both of Earlestown were charged with being drunk and disorderly in Earles- town on Saturday and were each fined 28 6d and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>ALLEGED THEFT AT BURTONIVOOD,</head>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT AT BURTONIVOOD,</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional uourt on Monday, before Mr James Tinsley, Mark Abrahams was chlrged with stealing £lO 10s and a cash bag, the property of Harvey Burnham, at Burtonwood on the 25th inst.—Only suflhient evidence to justify a remand was offered and the case was adjourned un- til nest Wednesday, when the prisoner will be brought before the magistrates at the Warrington County Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>k•- T. JOHN'S CHURCH SUNDAY SCHOOL. A social gathering ill connection with the senior class of the above Sunday school was held in the ission:Hall,Earlesto wu on Saturday ,3vening. About 130 persons were present,and dancing was indulged in until late in the evening. The music was sup- plied by the Earlestown Orchestral Society, and Mr Roth well officiated as M.C. The arrangements were in the hands of the Class Committee and the proceeds are to be devoted to the proposed sale of work.</p>
        <p>THE PROPOSED ALTERATIONS AT EARLESTOWN STATION.</p>
        <p>A reply has now been received from the London and Niirth Western Railway Company to the peti- tion which was sent up by the tradespeople of Queen-itreet, stating that the petitioners may rest assured that the approaches to the station will not be far removed from the present position. On Tues day morning a number of officials of the Compa - visited the station, and its precincts in connecti on with the proposed alterations and improvemen ts which it is said will shortly be proceeded with, BARLESTOWN WESLEYAN BAND OF HOPE.</p>
        <p>The annual testival in connection with the above was held in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, on Saturday evt ninz. The pl'oceedings were initiated by a tea, at which about 50 persons were present, the Com- mittee of the Band of Hope attending to those at tea. The subsequent proceedings were presided over by the Rev C E Dove, supported by the Rev J A Lee, and Mr 0 Webster, secretary.—The report sub- mitted by the Secretary regretted that the Com- mittee had not seen greater fruits of their labours. They were sorry that the juvenile choir had had to be discontinued because of the lack of a conductor. The work of the Band of Hope was dependent upon a few stalwarts in the cause of temperance, and the Committee were disappointed in not seeing the young men taking more interest in temperanle work, and thus help to stem the tide of intemperance in their midst, but they hoped they would rally round them and help them in their great work in training the young in the paths of sobriety. The meetings of the society were held fortnightly and he took the opportunity of thank- ing Miss Blair and her committee for the excellent programmes submittal from time to time. They were sorry to lose Mr J Marsh from their ranks, but they wished him God-speed in his new sphere of labour. Their membership was about the same as last year, 120. The receipts were £3 193 64, including a balance from the previous year of .El, and the expenditure was a Os 61,1eaving a balance of £1 103. Addresses were afterwards given by the chairman and the Rev J A Lee of Liverpool. The following programme of songs &amp;c., was efficiently submitted. Quartette "Sweet and low" Misses 0 Clare, and F Holliday, Messrs Twemow and Webster; song "Love the pedlar," Miss 0 Clan; song, "The old brigade," Mr W I Laycock ; recital. "The women of Mumble's Head," Mrs Francis; duet "The two sailors," Messrs J Gee and W I Laycock ; song " The river of years," Miss F Holliday ; song "I seek for thee in every flower " Mr Gee: recital, Mr G Warburton ; song, "Do ole umbrella," Miss 0 Clare ; duet," Excelsior." Messrs J Gee and W I Layeock ; quartette, ",Pro Phundo Basso," Misses 0 Clare and F Holliday, Messrs Twemlow and Webster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>EXTRAORDINARY INCIDENT AT A HAYDOCX COLLIERY.</p>
        <p>Twenty-four firemen and haulage bands employed at the Prince's Pit, Haydock, St. Helens, bad an alarming experience on Friday night. It appears that about six o'clock the men assembled at the bottom of the shaft, which is about 300 yards deep, and got on to tho three- decked cage dreparatory to being borne up to the top. The signal was given to the engine-winder, who started hie engine, first lifting the cage which was at the top of the shaft up a few inches, so that the catches might be withdrawn. By some means or other, the browman or the parson in charge failed to get the catches clear, and the cage rested upon them once more. The engine started off at full speed, with the result that the twenty four men at the bottom of the shaft were lifted about 200 yards into the middle of the pit, and the heavy wire rope at the top of the shaft went flying all over the place causing consternation among the browhands. The men in the cage were kept in this position for about three hours, until the winding could be-put right. A message was sent to them telling them not to stir out of the caga The ventilator made the satt terribly cold, and when the men were ultimately got out they were suffering from the effects of their long wait, cooped up in the cramped positions they had to assume when travelling up and down the abaft.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>ALLEGED BIGAMY BY A WIDNES</head>
        <p>ALLEGED BIGAMY BY A WIDNES</p>
        <p>MAN.</p>
        <p>Prisoner Committed for Trial.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Police Court, on Friday, John Walton, of 8. Mill Field-street, Widnes, was charged with marry- ing Elizabeth Caldwell, well knowing that Margaret Ellen Walton, his first wife, was still alive.—James Walton, brother of the prisoner, said he was present at Farnworth Church, on the 28th January, 1883, when prisoner was married to Margaret Ellen Whitfield. The latter was present in court that morning.—Elizabeth Caldwell stated that she was married to the prisoner on the 12th February 1898. She married him under the belief that his first wife was dead. Prisoner repre sented himself as a widower. She had been living with him until recently. when she found out that his former wife was alive. They went to Earlestown to be married because witness was a Roman Catholic and prisoner was a Protestant. During the last few months prisoner had been cruel to her. Witness had never seen his first wife until that morning—Mary Thelwell, wife of Richard Thelwell, Ditton-road said she was present at the marriage of the last witness and prisoner in 1898.—P.C. Farish said he apprehended the prisoner by warrant on the previous day. Whin the document was read over to him, he replied, " All right; go with you" When charged at the police station, prisoner replied: " I thought she was dead. I was told so."—Prisoner was committed for trial at the Liverpool Assizes.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT. ---- The New Judge.</p>
        <p>His Honour Judge Bowen Rowlands, took big seat in his judicial capacity for the first time, at the Runcorn C3unty Court. on Thursday morning. Be succeeds Judge Gwynne James, to the No 7 Circuit. lomprising Birkenhead, Warrington, Runcorn. etc , whose trans fer to the Bath Circuit came as a considerable surprise, for it is hut a shlrt time ago since Judge James succeeded Judge Ffoulkes. When His Honour entered the court Mr. W D Jolliffe, the registrar, tendered to him a hearty congratulation and welcome.</p>
        <p>Mr. Joseph Burton, solicitor, Runcorn, on behalf of the solicitors practising in that court did likewise on behalf of his colleagues. His Honour in suitable terms thanked all very much for their kind congratulations.</p>
        <p>Walton Lea Gardens Open to the Public. . .</p>
        <p>Owing to the generosity of Councillor John Cros- field, the public were afforded an opportunity of viewing the fine display of chrysanthemums at Walton Lea Gardens on Saturday and Sunday after- noons. Mach day from two to five o'clock there was la continual arrival of wisit Ire, and it is estimated that on Saturday 800 persons were present, and on Sunday several thousands. Altogether in the con- servatory, greenhouses, and fruithonses, over 200 different varieties, new and old, were shown, and it is doubtful whether Mr. Kipps, the head gardener, ever had the pleasure of exhibiting finer specimens. The finest show of flowers was in the conservatory. The incurved specimens were shown on the sides and the Japanese in the centre, with the pompcns (smaller varieties) at the foot, while the anemones were seen to advantage in the corridor. In the greenhouses other flowers, such as begonia and azaleas were inspected and admired. Mr. John Crosileid's generosity in again throwing open his gar- dens to the public is much appreciated, and the only regret is that the condition of his health did not allow him to be present on Saturday and Sun- day.</p>
        <p>inWhen asking for Cocoa, Insist on having CADBURY'S sold only In Packets and Tins, as other Cocoas areoften substi- tuted for the sake of extra profit—Advt. (1)</p>
      </div>
