      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>Impure Beer.</head>
        <p>Impure Beer.</p>
        <p>Sir,—As the public mind is just now considerably disturbed, and very naturally so, by what is known as " The Beer Scare," we think it may allay anxiety to some extent if we state that the beers brewed in our breweries (Wilderspool and St. Helens) are and always have been absolutely pure and wholesome, and entirely free from arsenic and other deleterious matter.—Yours truly, FOR GREENALL, WHITLEY, &amp; Co., LTD. THOMAS J. DOWN, Director and Snretary. November 29, 19)0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>Canon Wood and " Ignorant Runcorn."</head>
        <p>Canon Wood and " Ignorant Runcorn."</p>
        <p>Dear Sir.—The following are the extracts from the report of Canon Wood's speech given In the newspaper, and to which I objected viz : • They had an intellig- ence of A kind "and "The intelligence was represented by the school teachers." If Canon WO3d did not use the words will Mr Stables please explain how they got into the paper. Can any interpretation other than the one I used be given of the words? If so, perhaps Mr Stables will favour us with it. There was nothing in the report of the speech to show that it was a joke or that Canon Wood was referring to his" personal friends" and I very much doubt if these "personal friends "will thank Mr Stables for his direct insult.</p>
        <p>Mr Sttbles• defence of Canon Wood reminds one of the usual defence in libel actions viz : I did not say it but if I did, I did not mean it.</p>
        <p>So long as the words I quote above remain on record as being used by Canon Wood ought be not to apologise to the people of Runcorn for having used them ? Yours truly, "ONE OF THE IGNORANT."</p>
        <p>The Rev. W H Stables of Weston, has interfered in a correspondence that did not concern himself in the least, and finding himself worsted in the discussion he, last Friday, attempted to close it in the high-handed, arbitrary way usual with his priestly class. Why did the reverend gentleman intervene? We, who have the extreme felicity of living in Runcorn and district, knew with what an iron hand the vicar rules not only his curates but his flook. His will is the law against which there is no appeal. I argue from this that Mr Stables has been allowed, perhaps commissioned, to make smooth (if he could) the places made rough by the vicar's indiscreet utterances. The charge was that the vicar had described the people of Runcorn as poor and ignorant. Poor it was allowed we were, and there was a tentative denial (by Mr. Stables) that the vicar had said we were ignorant. Now after a far night's further consideration Mr. Stables brings himself up to the sticking point and denies that Canon Wood ever said " any such thing," and mark this, that as the speech was a humorous one, it was ridiculous to write as "one of the ignorant" did in regard to it. The Vicar humorous! This is enough to take one's breath away. The Vicar may be described by half a dozen other terms of approbation but he has yet to display himself to the general populace as a humotht and if this were his first attempt in the footsteps of Hood, Math Twain, Gilbert Sec.„ then he had better take a few more lessons in the art before he again exhibits himself in public. It has never yet bean stated that C mod Wood used the word "ignorant "in his speech, but he implied that the thrify and striving people of Runc3rn were ignorant in language plain and unmistakable. It is not necessary to call a man a liar to make him know that you believe he is not telling the truth. In fact it sometimes only needs a shrug of the shoulders (English not French fashion) to tell a man you don't believe him and Vicar Wood is an adept at shrugging his shoulders in the pulpit and out. And there are also more ws es than one of describing a man or a people as ignoreec, and the Vicar adopted one mode. Canon Wood's speech has been nrinted,will he place in parallel columns his re- ported speech and his explanation of what he meant when be used the words the reporters have credited him with? Then we may he nearer arriving at the truth than we shall be reading the irresponsible epistles of Mr Stables. Perhaps, as Mr Stables desires, we may after- wards draw our own conclusions as to the Vicar's speech but we may require more enlightenment from Mr Stables before we understand "making assertions in public and withdrawing them in private letters." r You a truly. s' No ! No!"</p>
        <p>SPECIAL VISIT TO PRESTON —Mr. Thomas Ison, Ocu- list and Auri.t (of Don's Eye and Ear Dispensary, Leeds), establiAed 1871, will visit the Castle Hotel, Preston, on ThurFday December 6th, hours 11 to 5.30, and may be consulted on all cases of failing sight and hearing. New treatment without operation. Sufferers should avail themselves of the present opportunity of consultation. Spectacles adapted for weak or drfective sight. Those unable to avail themselves of a personal comultation, should write for printed form of questiona forwarded free. Lltters to be addres.stsd to 71, GREAT GEORGE-STREET, Leeds.—Advt.</p>
