      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Warrington Ambulance Men's Experiences.</p>
        <p>Severe Fighting. The following letter has been received by Captsin Waring from Private W. E. Longmore, a Warrington S!' John Ambulance man attached to the 24th Field HeePi,: tat of the First Division in South Africa r—" Botha:1 Farm. near Zeerust, October, 1900. "Dear Captain Waring,— Just a line or twpoto let you knew our where' abouts• We left Lichtenbteref on August 16:h after be; ing in the Boers' hands ten days. fhirty or forty,^' them escorted us over the border, making us march 20e miles, with less than a half-hour for dinner. Anyhosel we were glad to get out of their sight. We got int° Ma.feking next day. On August 29th we had a terrible time of it,as a tornado broke over the town. This ieg: sand, rain, hail, and wind storm. There were about "1 large marquee tents and 30 bell tents,in which were over 100 patients. Among them was one of our men vrbe. 1 expect, will be invalided home before this reaches The patients were all got safely into different place; I: the town, and were sent down to Kimberley during tb' next two days. Then on September sth we had ordercei to join Lord Methuen's First Division. He harl orders to clear all the western side. Since joining 131,:,; we have had nothing but fighting and trekking. %Id have been travelling between Zeerust and Vryburg. 5131, whilst on our way up again, at Klip Drift, we had 4,4 baptism Af fire. We had just started to move toweluo Rustenburg when the enemy started to shell us. CI shell went right under one of our ambulance wagget3i; in which was Lord Loch. (He had been shot throng. the foot on the previous day.) Luckily it did not base" The Boers paid the penalty for it, as our artillery Pd it them hot, and afterwards our mounted men saw p",i, dead Boers in one heap. We passed through OliPbseeto Nek and into Rustenburg. This is a very dangeruB part, as mountains are on both sides. This is where ve Wet slipped through Methuen's hands. On went again towards Zeerust, and when about seven miles from it we had anot shelling far worse than the first. We had 3tl9d got the hospital up and the patients comfortably P 13,5 d: „e,, when they let us have some of their 15. pounders. first one went over our heads and dropped a fey Vir away among the North Lencashiree. (ego poor folio i who had just lay down was killed. The same eb.e,il shattered another's arm and leg, and he afterwards die' Another shell served one of the artillery just the savael the first. Such a sight I never want to see again. shells kept whistling over our heads until our VI Battery gave them a few, which soon silenneui them. N-xt day we mar'e the Boers " scoot" out Zeerust, which we occupied. Fellovving them up day we got them close to Botha's Fern', where 1: artillery sent 400 ',hells of lyddite, shrapnel. 30.1 pounders into them. They must have suffered heacllC. as nothing could live under auch a bombardment. A. I write this our men are letting them have it again. expect you know that I, Buckley, Dimelow, Maddens: and Johnson have signed on again for six months long°. and by the time yon receive this we shall have eIOS,I itehorkeeinmanfnerthwsarodf tthoecommeictelandh mweentwhes. re Wpeleasaerdestio hear of Newns aed Holloway arriving home saieiti Give our best wishes to the brigade member, SP". receive the same yourself.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1900-11-30_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>HARRIERS.</head>
        <p>HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>Runcorn Harriers. The Runcorn Harriers entertaine I Suttrot2 (the Liverpool and district champions) at Rnncorn 0- ' Saturday. The visitors were fully represented, whilo rhie, only absentee on the Runcorn team was W Ray,;%";„htes% up the duty of trail layer. Seeing that Runcorn ,:ien second to Sutton in the district championship, greri:i;nl terest was taken in the run. Trail was laid over miles of good country, including some good juniPa both hedge and ditch, in the direction of Rocksavr:c and Sutton, returning by Hailav,o3 home again. A P was made from the BullN Head about four o'clock, tlte 1. pace being taken up by Webber, Hopkins, and 00°d I,,se After an absence of 50 minutes the first man wag signb and proved to be Webber,. closely followed by 90d• • 0 war* and Goodall. Runcorn were most unfortunate 0 not breaking the first three men, and although theY rare eight men home in the first 12, when the numbers Ive counted it was found that Rtncorn were defeated by tbo6. narrow margin of four points, being 44 against 40. 119 g tato corn were defeated but they were not disgraced, % into consideration that Sutton have not yet tasted defet7e this season. After the race a tea was provided at tf'e Bull's Head by Mr T Richardson, when 58 partook ° og hearty meal. After the tea a pleasant evening vise sPeth songs being given by Messrs J Goodall, Webber, SO' wark, White and other members of the clubs. Farnwortit v. Widnes. In connection with the above club a ten-mile nori.l6 handicap took place on Saturday, the head quarters y 4 ing the Commercial Hotel. There were 20 entries and starters. The winner was John Brown, five seta. start, his time being 60 minutes 31 seconds. Be :o closely followed by A Dreedson, ten seconds start, %I"- n I Pime was 60. minutes 32 seconds. The third 910 T alien. The contest proved a most exciting one.</p>
