        <p>ANOTHER. UNSOLICITED TESTIMONIAL. PITTS' S, LONI)ON-ROAD, MANCHESTER (Just past Station, through Railway Arch). ALSO AT PITTS'S PANTHEON, GAYTHORN (Just past Aanchester Central Station). BUSINESS HOURS, 8 to 7. SATUIZDAYS. 8 to 9. THE OLDEST FIRM IN THE TRADE. fa 11-I, WAD SOME POWER THE GIFTIE GIB I US, TO SEE OURSEL'S AS ITHELIS SEE US," sang Bobbie Burns, but had he been the proprietor of a pros- perous Furnishing Business, satisfactorily fulfilling the requirements of the public, he would have had no need to so express himself, as innumerable opportunities would have been afforded him of seeing himself as' others saw him. Almost every other post brings us such, of which the tollow- ing is a fair sample : Blackburn, 12j9/10. Mr. 8. Pitts. Furnisher, London-road. Manchester, Dear Sir. • I am enclosing the balance of my account with you, which amount I retained until the furniture should prove satis- factory. lam very pleased indeed with it so far, and must say that the goods are exceptionally reasonable. I may tell you also that before deciding tol place my order with you I compared the prices and goods of so no other firms, both in Manchester and here, and found them to come nowhere near you for the price. You are at liberty to make whatever use ,you like of this testimonial, providing you do not give publicity to my name or initials —Wishing your business every success, I am, yours faithfully, j 'The original of this letter`san be seen in Pitta's show win- dows, with the signature obliterated of course. PITTS'S COULD EASILY FILL THIS NEWS- PAPER with expressions of approval similar to the above, but as one straw shows which way the wind blows, probably one testimonial will suffice to direct wide-awake people to the right emporium where 25 to 50 per cent can be saved. PITTS'S RENOWNED HOUSES OF FURNITURE at 16§, 22§. 23§, 38§, 50, 75, 100, 200, up to 1,000 guineas, excite wonder and admiration in all who see them. THEY STAND UNRIVALLED. They are a sour