        <p>HOSPITAL SUNDAY. Meeting of the Committee. The RECTOR (Rev F W Willis) presided at a meeting of the Hospital Sunday Committee held at this Infirmary, on Friday afternoon. There were also present, Revs H V Pigot, E C E Carleton, W Pollock Hill, L W Thomas. C H Hockm, J L Milne, Councillor John Roberts, Messrs T Birchall, H Thornton and A J Jodey (bon sec). Let ters of apology for non-attendance were received from the Rev F E Powell, Alderman Monks and Mr John Fairclough. SECRETARY'S REPORT. Mr. A J JOLLEY reported chat seven places of worship which contributed to the Flespital Sunday Fund in 1899 failed to do so in 1900. Three recommenced contribut- ing. On balance there was a net loss of £4 is 2d under these heads. The total receipt, had been £264 16a 101, an increase as compared with last year of £l7 5s 2d The expenses had been £l4 13s as compared with £l3 19s in 1899. Comparing th 3 returns with 1899 Mr Jolley pointed out that the amount collected within Ore Borough in 1900 was £176 13s lid as compared with £l6O 16, id in 1899,whilet the collections in the churches outside the Borough in 1900 were £BB 2s 11d as compared with 15s 7d in 1899, Councillor JOHN ROBERTS stated that the collections were very satisfactory considering the numerous calls which had been made upon the public. Mr Bram/Lt.', observed that he was glad there was a slight increase on the previous year. The SECRETARY: It is the highest amount collected in the history of the fund. Mr HENRY THORNTOIsI 111017 ed.• and the Rev C H HOCKEN seconded the adoption of the report which was carried unanimously. ELECTION OF HON SECRETARY. The RECTOR said he was sure they all felt that Mr JoPey had served them admirably and they were greatly indebted to him for his work. (Hear, hear.) Councillor JOHN ROBERTS moved the re-election of:Mr Jolley as hon. secretary and said he did not think any man could possibly have done the work better than:he had done it. Mr T BIRCHALL seconded and said Mr Jolley always did his work well and he was a man in whom they had every confidence. He (Mr Birchall) always looked upon Mr Jolley as one of their model public men, always ready to do some good work. The RECTOR said they wonld all like to swell the chorus of praise. The resolution was unanimously carried. Mr. JOLLEy acknowledged the complimentary remarks and said the highest satisfaction any man could bate was to feel that he had been of some service to his fellow citizens.and that they were able to recognise the motives Prompting him to be of the highest order. DATE OF HOSPITAL SUNDAY. It was deciied, on the proposition of the Rev E C E CARLETON, seconded by the Rev H V PIGOT, to fix Sunday, March 17, as Hospital Sunday. The meeting concluded with an acknowledgment of the Chairman's services.</p>
